      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>SELECTED READING&amp;</head>
        <p>SELECTED READING&amp;</p>
        <p>TRUE GENTLEMEN. Truce courtesy •is . "the - beauty of the heart." How well is it that bo one class has a monopoly in this kind of beauty; that while favourable circumstances undoubtedly do sender good manners more common among persons 'moving in higher than lower spheres, there should zertertheless he no positive hindrance to the poorest classes having good manners. Here is an illustra- tion of true politeness exhibited by both classes of society. Csee day, in hastily turning the corner of a ,crooked street ill the city of London, a young lady ran with great force against a regged little boy, and nearly knocked him down. Stopping as soon as she could she turned round and said very kindly to the boy, "I beg your pardon, my little fellow; I am very sorry that I ran against you." Tae poor boy was astonished. He looked at her for a moment in surprise. and then taking Off about threequerters of a cap, he made a low bow and said, while a broad pleasant smile spread imelf over all his face, You can hey my panting, miss, and welcome; and the next time you rue agin me, you may knock me clean down and I won't say- a word.' After the lady had paesed on, he turned to his companion and said, 'I say, Jim, it's the first time I ever had anybody age my parding, and it's kind o' took me off my feet." One very cold day, the American preacher, Henry Ward Beecher: bought a paper from a very ragged little boy. "Poor little fellow!" said he, "ain't you very eold "I was, sir, before you pessea," replied the boy, with natural good manners. Politeuess has been defined as benevolence in small things. This is well illustrated by the followine incident, which is related in the "Life of General Sir William Napier, K.C.8." Taking a country walk one day, he met a little girl, about five years old, sobbing over a broken bowl, which she had drcpped in bringing it back from the field to which she hen taken he father's dinner. She said she would be beaten on her return home for having broken it. With a sudden gleam of hope she innocently looiced up into his face, and said, "Bat za can mend it—can't eel" He explained that he could not mend the 'oowl, but he would give her a s:xpence to buy another. However, on open- ing his purse it was destitute of silver, and he had to make amends by promising to meet his little friend in the same spot at the same hour next day, and to bring the sixpence with him ; bidding her ' tell her mother she had seen a gentleman who would bring her the money for the howl the next day. Tae child, entirely treating him, went on her way comforted. On hi a return home he found an invitation asking him to dine in Bath the following evenieg, to meet someone whom he specially wished to see. He hesitated for some little time, trying to calculate the possibility of going to meet his little friend of the broken bowl, and of still being in time for the dinner-party; but finding this could not be, he wrote to decline accepting the invitation, on the plea of a pre-engagement, saying, "I cannot disappoint her. She trueted me so im- plicitly." This anecdote of Christian chivalry on the part of a really "gallant "genuine gentleman is all the more impres.ive from the fact that the action was not only in itself a little one, but was done for the sake of those "little ones" who are made the test of purity of principle.—Qmiver. HOME LIFE.—In every home we find new varie- ties of inmates; also a different degree of happiness, and surroundings differing from those in every other home. How comprehensive and interesting, then, is the subject of home life. In some homes we greet light-hearted parents and children happy in their love and wise traiuing ; the clouds seem to pass by these households, leaving them the warmth and brilliancy of unbroken sunshine. In other homes we find discord, unrest, and consequent unhappiness; no member of the household is satisfied; all are restless, some are ill-tempered. The sun seems never to shine into these darkened places. Then there are the homes where life is marked by fluctu- ations, differing as elonds, • shower and sunshine alternate on days in April. Some of the inmates are merry for 4 time, while others, morbid or de- spondent., clash and alternate in their moods until the chaotic conditions produced are filleting to realise. The masses of humanity drift, and are buffeted by the waves of chance, finding their home wherever a combination of circumstances lands them, irrespective of their choice in the matter. No thought or attention is given to the important points which make or mar health and happiness. Instead of grappling with circumstances and making them subservient, these illifting people permit cir- cumstances to control them and their fortunes. In their home life they sometimes wear shackles which often cut to the bone, and sooner or lets • wear oat their victims. Loose morals otten resa. -rem the recklessness which this state engenders; chil- dren are born into the worst conditions for their happiness and healthy growth. In fact, a so, vey of the interior of too many homes rev,,le conditions which should incite to active meaeures for the enlightenment and retorrn of their inins.:,es. Those who would be useful in such beneficent worm saould begin in their own homes, and strike deep at the roots of all discordant elements and baneful influences. Then they will be armed with wisdom to direcs and with strength to aid those who are groping blindly lathe obscuring confu don of chaotic home life, dreading Its shadows, listless, apathetic, drifting into vice, and exposed to its fr:ghtful penalties. It would be an inestimable boon to those unfortnnete people df they could be awakened to a re dising sense of the importance of thought and action toward securing Intelligent happiness in home life.—Mrs. H. IL Ite:hbun.</p>
        <p>SMALL CALES AND VEXATIONS.—SmaII cares ard vesAt:ons tad to the Ir of all. It is not the petty-minded who can bear them with fortitude and calmness, but who con rise above them into a region of puror llonOlt, where they can obtain a wider view. It is Lucca less effective to attack them one by one, with perhaps a vastly exaggerated idea of their importance, than to occupy the mind with more worthy matters and the heart with aoblor purposed. Then they cf ten sink into smaller and truer prcportions, and it is easier to bear them with courage .onci sttrenitv. Alms OF EDUCATION.—E,Theation, in its true ' bens,. rut have tau thatiuct ahaa—to inform and devemp the mind and to inspire and influence the heart. The success of each of these depends largely upon the success of the other; and upon their united and harmonious action will depend the strength and.excelleuce et the character and the purity and value of the We. All knowledge is like good seed—it cannot. be _too highly priz..e.d or too widely disseminated ; hut to realise its possibilities, It must be placed in fruitful soil prepared to receive and to vitaliAe it.</p>
        <p>KNOWLEDGE, like the blood, is healthy rnly with .6 brisk circulation. Its work is to supply the veins and arierks of our mental hie, thus continu- ally being, transformed into new thought and fresh activity. I, should feed our whole lives, making .them r. cher, ti,ippicr, more pawerfu), more valu- able. The knowiedge that cues this has attaiiced Its object, wbettic-r it be the h.:gtiest culture of the sehni)!K or the practical business of the adze or the factory... \ AN IiNTEUTHFUL BOAST.—We sometimes hear a man boa-t of inter having known fear; but it is an idle vaunt. It c•innot be true; but, if it, were, it would bespeak him less, not more manly. One of the bravest soldiers cutifesred to having telt tortures of fear in hi :4 first battle; yet he swerved not an inch, and his comrades thought him utterly fearless. His caerai:e kept him atm in the path of duty, despite his fears.</p>
        <p>To cultivate the liumane and benevo'ent feelings is not a more pleas Jut privi:bge to avail ourselves of at pleasure • it is an obligation rebting upon us ail, withont the fulfilment of which any claim to justice, even in outward conduct, fails to the ground. No man can be happy without a friend, nor be sure of his friend till he Is unfortunate.</p>
        <p>• MR. EDWARD BUTLER, in his new volume. "A Consideration ot Gentle Wayu," tells the following anecdote, which should dispel the idea of want of gratitude in a cat,: A lady had a favourite cat to wilich she had given many a tit-bit. One day the grateful animal leaped up on her lap at dinner, and deposited a fine mouse in her plate. Jr• your friend is in want, don't carry him to the tavern, where you treat yourself as well as him, and entail a, thirst and heisdache upon him next; morning. To treat a poor wretch with a bottle of Burgundy sod fill his snuff-box, is like giving a pair a laced ruffles to a man that has never a shirt on his back.</p>
        <p>PA !s4 MILLER CARTE i'S LITRE Get abottleto•day or LIEF/ pm&amp; PE7NY DAVIS'S small ~. „to, IN:AUT I'. 7-- 11 PAIN KILLY...P., Pia AcUi direetl7 on the Sinall use of that dui CAW sett!. of ?tn. Cares Dose . ~,, .IT n tv ,00.1, ,yn, v 5, aro la . Oalds.C;atzhe. awl- ...„ PraeTb.ut. &amp;MI" Cramp Pal, la lhe 9,ornect, tilree Co, D 1 ii:4h. to a-: Forty in a.. SOZODONT elio'T;'• 4-T• • Sugar Coated whirii wAI spf, eely :r.:12.1-t - v eal Chennots ~ , ~ , tabie CA 3. ii all ta it Avoid 1 ‘,...0-re•Z V epe ~„,.,,, b.. le., t ~ ~,• , „,. al lint!..V.;:ne.B tincirrr Cure 5 orpld Lwer ;---,-,- ' . ' --1 n... um .0-t. a cet ~.f .a: fill'llia,a.-ag .-..rae3. Without Farl . fragrance to t'le bre,: h PM ill XiLL ER Chemuts: 15. lia. Au uleugsts seu it. 24</p>
