      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>' ''Y' . i .. r .1.- ''' ' 11 I I Ilk ' . . A: i 1r '[HE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 13, 1893.</p>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT. ai</p>
        <p>The death of Mariii-XichioneSs-ntAilesbury-re- moves onetof the bi;st known women in the fashion- able world. There has been no _more easily distin- guished figure at aiistocratic receptions or gather- ings of any sort during the last thirty years than this stately dame, who was born in the same year as Mr. Gladstone, and has been a widow since 1856. " Lady A," as she was familiarly addressed in Society, was almost invariably one of those invited to meet royal guests at a country house. As a con- versationalist she had few equals, and up to the last she carried her tall figure with an ease and grace which many women half her age might envy. Her qiecial feature was a quantity of fair brown hair dressed at the sides of her head in a manner pecu- liarly her own. 6' Lady A." dressed very effectu- ally, and was noted for her emeralds.</p>
        <p>In politics the late Dowager Marchioness was a strong Liberal. Unlike most ot those with whom she lived, she never professed any admiration for Lord Beaconsfield. Mr. Gladstone was her ideal kf a statesman. She had not forgotten, although tbe present horsey Mniquess has, that until the last twelve years, the Ailesbury family were the back- bone of the Liberal strength in .:siorth Wilts. The Ailesbury estates are now relieved of all their join- tures to dowager*. Two years ago there were three to pay. Lord Roberts is being followed home by more than one distinguished soldier who has served abroad for the best part of his term of active ser7 vice. His military secretary, Colonel Nicholson, of the Engineers, is due this week from Bombay. He has a distinguished record on his own account,and has been instrumental in carrying out many of the ex-Commander-in-Chiers administrative reforms. Major-General Herbert, the Commandant of the Canadian Militia, is also here by coincidence at the same time. His particular mission is to confer with the War Office on the defences of Elquimault.</p>
        <p>Mr. Herbert Gardner is more than a mere Minis- ter of A.grictilture. lie is an essayist, poet, amateur actor and dramatic author. In compliment to his versatility as many us possible of his colleagues on the Treasury Bench intend to be present at the first night of his sketch "Time Will Tell," at the Trafal- gar Square Theatre.</p>
        <p>Many congratulations have poured into Mr. Isaac Holden, the veteran member for the Keighley division of Yorkshire, who attained his eighty- ‹ixth year yesterday. He has sat for ,lieighley since 1885. but before that had represented Knares- borough and the North-West Riding Division. Mr. Holden carries his weight of years with a vigor that :eems to throw I;ff the infirmities of age. He is the head of a large manufacturing firm in Yorkshire.</p>
        <p>Dr. Churehill Julius, Bishop of Christchurch, New Zealaut, who preached for the Bishop of London's Fund at St. Martin's-in-the-Fields on Sunday morn- ing, is the very ideal of a colonial bishop, lithe, wiry, young, and toll of energy—just the very man to go up the steeple and bless the big cross at the top,which is just what Bishop Julius did awhile ago at Christchurch after his spire had been toppled over by (12.1 earthquake. He has a stentorian voice, and is no book parson, but talks straight and does not feat the growing democracy. One could have pictured him joining the throngs on the adjacent embankment, marching with trumpets and drums to Hyde-park, and then preaching a sermon from the tallest treetop. We could do with a few more such prelates as Bishop Julius. Lie made short work of the London Fund.</p>
        <p>Lord Petre, who died on Monday, in London, had OHO unique distinction, lie was the only Roman Catholic priek4 since the Reformation who had held a sent in the House of Lords. He was also, pro- bably, the richest cleric in England, for not only ,lid he inherit the greater portion of _his father's large fortune, but he had an income of considerably over £20,000 a year from his Essex estates, without Touch outlay for the maintenance of. the family place, Thorndon, which was burned down fit teen years ago. Lord Petro held the post of domestic prelate at the Court of the Vatican. His brother Bernard, who succeeds him, has had a military training, and, like all the Petre's is a staunch Catholic.</p>
        <p>Father Bernard Vaughan, the new rector of the Church of the Holy Name in Manchester, and as ~uch the superior of the group of Jesu:. -houses in the district, is a brother of the Cardinal-Archbishep of Weatminister. He was intended by his father Lieut-Colonel Vaughan, of Courtfield, Herefordshire for the Army, but of his own choice adopted a religious life, and entered the Order of Jesus. He was educated at the famous Stoneyhurst College, :lard by Preston, becoming prefect of tile students in Philosophy, and afterwards migrated to Beau- mont College. For four subs( quent years be 4tudied theology at St. Bennos, in North Wales, and was finally ordained in 1880, and stationed at Farm street, in the metropolis. There he soon acquired a reputation as a preacher, and was specially set aside for pulpit work, his services being requisi- tioned all over the country. In 1886 he was made -,uperior of the church of which he is now rector, and so successful has been his rule that, contrary to use and wont. be has remained in the same place for orer a decade. What his congregation think of !dm was shown a while ago by the presentation of cheque for a thousand guineas.</p>
        <p>Particulars of the "Abington" Baird will, which have now been made public, disclose the fact that no portion of what remains of Mr. Baird's coloisal fortune is willed away from the family. The per- sonalty amounts to £831,719, SO that Mr. Baird does not seem to have tirawsn so much on capital as those who professed to know, declared after his death. This sum, of course, does not include the real estate, which would probably amount to about as much again, alt hough there is no knowing the extent to which they are mortgaged. On the whole, there se:ms to have been method in Mr. Baird's madness. His disreputable frionds could not wheedle him into spending more than his in- come, and he showed his real estimate of them by leaving everything to his mother for her life.</p>
        <p>The Congregational Union has done honor to itself in electing the Rev. G. S. Barrett, 8.A., chairman for the ensuing year. Mr. Barrett, who is the' son of a Jamaica missionary, became pastor of Prince's- at. congregational Church, Norwich, on leaving college, some twent3-five years agl, and, in spite of countless •• calls" elsewhere, has remained faith- ful to his first love. The maxim "when in doubt play trumps," has, has its prototype in the Congre- gational world, " when in doubt try Barrett," the ! result being teat nearly every lending church in the denomination which has happened during the last 20 year. to be without a pastor has endeavoured to allure Mr. Barrett. A man must be made of true metal who can build up And keep together a large congregation for a quarter of a-century. This Mr. Barrett has done by his genuine sincerity,his power of putting r old truths in new lights, his generous sympathy 'with modes of thought different from his own (one of the rarest of gifts), but mainly, perhaps, because he always gives of his best and that best is very good. Mr. Barrett's position as a leader among the younger Congregational ministers, therefore, makes this bestowal of the blue ribbon of the denomina- tion peculiarly fitting. He is a living exponent of muscular Christianity, being a keen sportsman. Ills friends declare that, had he not been a minister ht would have been a doctor, for second only to his belief in the inspiration of the Gospels comes his' belief in hydropathy as a panacea for physical evils.</p>
        <p>Mr. Barrett was the editor of the "Congrega- tional Hymnal," and he has recently brought out an admirable book entitled "Religion in Daily Life." He is not, ho.wever, one of those ministers, who no sooner have they preached a good sermon, than they exclaim, "Go to, let 118 publish it"; his admirers complain rather that he does not publish enough. Ur. Barrett is intending to issue before long a reprint oi a course of lectures given by him in Norwich on the light thrown hyreceut scientific discoveries an the Old Testament.</p>
        <p>Mr. Michael Devitt, in his letter to the Irish Australian Press from the House of Commons, de- clares that Australia is rapidly building up a party in the Imperial Parliament. After alluding to the election of Messrs. Curran, of Sydney, Mr. Davit,t remarks:— "Now we have Mr.,,J. F. Hogan, the Irish Australian,' as he is called here in London, returned without a contest for Mid-Tipperary, and he is sure to be welcome addition to our. ranks. Australian members ,are evidently at a premium in your party,' observed a Tory member to me to- day when the news of Mr. Hogan's unopposed-re- , tutn I.,ached the House, and this is true. The United States has one representative, Canada one, and Australia three in the Irish party. You can- not therefore, acetise us of any want of apprecia- tion in this respect at least."</p>
        <p>DO not overload the plate of a guest, or press upon anyone that which they pave once declined. The paper and pictures on the walls, the carpets and curtains may affect the mood of a sensitive person. °kroneloth can be —utilized aa kettle rests. They saye the ebony Impression Qf blaCk 'het 'upon,' wood- work. Never say or do, or countenance in others, the saying !or doing of anything rude or impolite at the table.</p>
        <p>I The ;ide of the -Spocm is to -be placed to the mouth, ,except in the case of a man Wearing a moustache, when !the point of the spoon leads the way.</p>
        <p>I Infants' Food. I One pound of flour, quarter of a pound of finest oat- :meal, thoroughly mixed, and baked in a slow oven. When given first, mix with hot water, filled up with milk, and then boiled to the sconsistency ot thin cream.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>11111 - DEAREST NELLTI,</p>
        <p>Thanks t• he ihe unusually long spell of dry weather -most placer; are looking so bright and gay whit their fresh foliage end brilliant flower-beds, that toilettes need to he in harmony with such sur- ronnaings.. Dice the weather, too, the fashionable tints are muob brigliterAhan they have been for many ir long day, and should consequently be mixed in thoroughly good taste. The two moat popule.r wool shades of the season—green and violet—are produoed in innumerable gradations, yet many people choose such crude hues as to'pain- fully, Offend an artistic eyo. On tii4 other hand, nobody oan go far wrong with the. lovely shot materials which blend hit° such 4arming cos- tume% (,!</p>
        <p>. , No collection of millinery ip fomplete without a sample the ISO st) le, earl I hive found a capitalone in green chip. with a ratfierligh crown trimmed W:t.,11 white lace and groups of black ostrich le4thers nodding over the tbplClovely little bonnet has a crown of light chestnut brown coarse straw, with a flat open worked brim of white guipara ; this is trimmed with bunches of gardenias and tiny little green wings and sprays of forget-me•nois. A black and white bonnet is effectively decked with bunches, of cherries and a bow of white lace ; a lovely hat has a broad brim cut up in the front and turning hack on either side, scroll fashion, with a huge bow of shaded ribbon set in the aperture, and at the back of Ulu{ is a bunch of most convincing-looking mos.</p>
        <p>Amonfint the many bonnets to be sren at present ; a very stylish article is made in gold or coloured embroidery, with quite a new crown formed by two wings of black velvet. Two small clusters of roses trim the front. This bonnet can be had in any colour at a very moderate price. Blouses are no longer simply made, and just with a little fulness back and front, but are more like bodices of the short-banded type, and with elaborate frills, revers or epaulettes, rut all tending to give the now fashionable width to the shoulders, aud the short, tightly belted waist. As I have already pointed out very few blouses are now made without a lining, but where there is a short Figaro-top of some substance, or the blouse is to be worn under an open jacket, the lining is some- times dispensed with, and mlkes very little differ once to the style, although the unlined blouse does not give such a neat, trim figure as where him kept firm aud smooth by a shaped lining.</p>
        <p>A most delightful travelling or walking gown is made in gendarme blue Moth of a filiu texture, and braided a la militaire with a broad, coarse black silk braid ; this, agsin, can be outlined by a tiny black silk cord, flecked with gold tinsel. The gown is a tailor-made desk" and is suitable for copying in fancy tweed, serge, fa.Sed-oloth, or in any of the pretty diagonals now so popular. The skirt, which should be gored to the present fashion, and per- fectly plain,, with the exception of the braiding should be cut in one with the ;icing, both of whioh should be made up together, forming the packet hole in the centre of the back, while the lower edge is merely finished with an invisible hem. The bodice is plain and tight-fitting, hooked down the ccnfre to a little below the waist and is prettily braided. The sleeve lining is also tight- fitting, with the material fulled over it, and wathered in pleats on to the shoulder. The quantity of forty-two-inch cloth requited would be kiiX yards, awl fifteen yards of the black braid. Novel eves for the spring are out just long ehough to meet the waist. Some have a round cut yoke to which a full- flouir:e is gathered'; others are composed of three graduated flounces, set full one over the other. The capes for winter wear were mostly made of coloured cloth; b'ack is being chiefly used now, either in corduroy or diagonal cloth or velvet. Pipings of tartan velvet at the edges of short capes are much used. Such mantles as these are only suitaole for young ladies and matrons. Older women should adopt in preference three-quarter rapes, which can be composed of handsome maferiAls lined with silk and finished with jet and other trimmings.</p>
        <p>A very pretty French demi toilette gown is of white ailk crepon ornamented with lace insertions. Flounce at the edge of the skirt. Straight collar and folded sash in white !milli ribbon.</p>
        <p>I scarcely think you would be pleased with the effect of the fancy flannelette mixed with the serge, un!ess it is introduced merely as a vest. I should advise you to' have a short opeu-froate(l. coat matching skirt, and to make two or three Blouses to wear with it. The coat should only reach to the hips, and should have a tight back, and lotie fronts turned back with wide rovers on the chest, the barque being rounded off below the waist. Then you might have a close-fitting waistcoat of flannelette, and a couple of pretty cotton blouses —say one pink and one with pale blue and white stripes, to wear in change with it. These might have folded waistbands of cambric, or the skirt could be sewn to a wide baud of navy blue " Petersham" belting, which could be worn over any blouse. If you intend to wear the coat always, you need of course only have blouse fronts instead of entire bodice.</p>
        <p>Yours sincerely, JEN:az</p>
        <p>Domestic Hints.</p>
        <p>An outside door, or even the glass in it, may tell secrets. •</p>
        <p>Distribute good cheer, sweet thoughts, tender remem- brances,</p>
        <p>A' glean bright light candums tp social, friendly chat at tea-time.</p>
        <p>Eat slowly, and do not fill the mouth with large quantities. No argumentative or in any was' unpleasant topic ihould be broached at the table.</p>
