      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>How to clean White straw bonnets and hats.</head>
        <p>How to clean White straw bonnets and hats.</p>
        <p>; Scrub the articles in soap and water with a lump of soda in it. When glean rince in water in which oxalic acid has been dissolved—a tablespoonful of acid to a quart of boiling water. Brush them in this ; then lay them in clean cold water for about half an hour, and afterwards stiffen with stiffening made from parchment shavings.</p>
        <p>• Shall we go gloveless ? There is a rumour moving mysteriously; about that gloves may shortly go, to some extent, out of fashion. One or two well-known dames, have practically ceased to wear them. One of these is Lady Tichborne, who May often be seen out of doors with hands bare to wind and weather. Another is Lady Grey Egerton.who always goes to the play without them. The beautiful Lady de Grey, too, generally takes her gicives off whenever she has a chance. But as yet few ladies have the courage to be seen about the streets altogether gloveless. Should the fashion come in, says " The Cticket,” in the Spring numblir of" Hearth and Home" I for one shall hardly welcome it. In the first place, gloves give a finisli to a toilet ; in the second, it is hardly pcssible to keep one's hands perfectly clean for any length of time without them. This last fact will surely keep the fashion out. Then hands get brown in the summer sun, and though brown hands may be all very well in the country, they hardly do for evening wear in town. A white erne with a brown hand at the end of it is scarcely prettier than a pals face decorated with a red nose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>SPEAKING OUT.</head>
        <p>SPEAKING OUT.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Spicer, MP., presiding at the meeting on Tuesday cd the Congregational Union of England and Wales, urged that it was the duty of the churches to de all that was possible for the improvement of the material as well as the spiritual condition of their fellow-men. " Are we," he asked, " to declare that we are sincerely desirous of saving our fellow-men from their sins, and then go direct from our Services to homes we are susl. taming on ir scale higher than we can afford except by use of the extrethe benefit of a low market price of labour? Or PO6sibly, to give notice 'to some oid em- ploy 6 who has served us faithfully for many years, because owing to the present state of the labour market we can fill his place more .ecoi.omically ?" Mr. Spicer went on to express his belief that the true remedy for existing la'aour difficulties was to increase the oppor- tunities for labcur, and how, he asked, could that be done until at any rate the land of the country waS open to labour. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>TEETH.</head>
        <p>THE TEETH.</p>
        <p>The teeth are constituted with fixed relations to</p>
        <p>the nstine of the •substances designed for human food, and if their laws of constitution and relation are obeyed, the teeth will never become diseased. The teeth are among the last organs which mani- fest the deterioration or melioration of the constitution ; hence some may violate the laws of nature in a manner calculated to destroy the teeth, and yet die with sound teeth; but, if the same habits be continued, the third or fourth generation will be afflicted with miserable teeth. On the other hand, people with teeth predisposed to decay may observe the constitutional laws without being able to Preserve their teeth from disease; yet they will preserve them longer than they would otherwise last, and _auger . . .</p>
        <p>_ . ...,. little ,pain ; and, if iliese habits are persevered in, tlfec third or fourth generation will have excellent teeth. A sea captain, who observed the teeth of different nations, found that where the people use much hot drink and hot . . . .</p>
        <p>food, and smoke tobac. co-or other narcotic substances, their teeth are black and much decayed ; but in the islands of the Pacific, where the people seldom take any- thing hot into their mouths, and are simple. and natural in their diet, their teeth were regular and free from decay. Culinary preparations lead us to masticate too little ;.o swallow too but, and cat too much. By eating our food in a natural state, or with that preparation which still requires full mastication, we would avoid all these evils, and preserve the teeth. Among causes act- flog on the teeth, heat is the most powertul. It is com- mon to hear people speak of substances as injurious ; but if these were never swallowed, the teeth would suffer little from them. If the food is soft and hot, con- centrated and highly seasoned, or otherwise vicious,and masticationis neglected, tartar will gather round the teeth, irritate the gums, separate them from the enamel, and irritate the membrane which surrounds the roots. Decay will' follow, and the teeth must be lost, unless the progress of the disease is arrested by correct habits. Almost all artificial preparations are injurious to the tee' h. Fiverything unfriendly to the constitution and health of the teeth is far more pernicious to them in childhood than in later periods of life. During the pro- gress of second dentition, every disturbing cause in the organic domain strikes at the very constitution of the teeth, preparing them for early decay.' Calomel (a pre- paration of mercury or quicksilver), and all medicine which has a general effect on the system, is often destructive to the permanent teeth, before these are formed. Everything in the dietic habits also which is stimulating, producing feverishness, and calculated to hasten second dentition, has an unhealthy effect on the teeth. Hence it is of importance that the diet of children should be simple, and every measure taken to preserve the general health. In his work on "Dental Degeneracy," Dr. 'Edwin Cox, L D.S., R.C.S. says Scotch, Irish; and the agricultural labourers of the northern counties in England, who , have been brought up from childhood on porridge and milk,brown or wheat- meal bread and an almost eiclusive vegetable diet deve- lops sound, strong teeth,ard generally retain them until late in life, while those reared in towns and manufactur- ing districts who are brought up on white bread, hot slaps, in the form of tea and tvatery coffee, leave fragile brittle teeth, which decay early. The Scotch have, as a rule, a largely-moulded and often splendid physique. For well-arched palates and capacious mouths, for healthy, bone-coloured, square, often fine filbert-shaped teeth, sometimes of gigantic size and provokingly steadfast, Commend me to these true Northerners. Yet even their superb dentures not infrequently prove susceptible to those decay-producing influences which lurk behind the gentler breezes and commercial prosperity ,f rs more temperate latitude. The "poor Irish' are Will r:cher In vigorous and all-enduring vitality; but a few years' residence in this country, involving more or l s change of diet and modes of life,proves perceptibly deteriorating to their remarkably strong and durable teeth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>" LOYAL " LONDON ALDERMEN.</head>
        <p>" LOYAL " LONDON ALDERMEN.</p>
        <p>The Court of Aldermen *of the City of London dis- cussed on Tuesday the Lord Mayor's action in proposing at a recent banquet the toast of The Holy Father and the Qaeen." A resolution having been proposed and seconded expressing regret at 'this departure from con- stitutional precedent, Alderman %Mil said he had no in- terr ion of placing any person above the civil and tem- poral position of the Queen. lie regretted, however, that any words of his should have given pain to any of his fellow-citizens. The resolution was agreed to 1111. j au imously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>SIISrENSIO.N OF ANOTHEIt</head>
        <p>SIISrENSIO.N OF ANOTHEIt</p>
        <p>AUSTRALIAN BANK.</p>
        <p>I TUESDAY—The MELBOURNE, LUESDAY.—The Bank Of Victoria has suspended payment to-day. The subscribed capital of the bank is £1,200,000, in shares of £lO, in which £5 per share has been paid up, but the liability extends to twice the amount of the share. The shares were origin- ally of £5O each, £25 paid, but on registration each share was divided into five, the proportionate liability being the same The liability to depositors now amounts to £6,250,000, of which £1,250,000 is owing to British investors, chiefly in B,mtlancl. At the date of the last balance-sheet,deposits and other moneys payable on demand amounted to £7,057,000. An Rlinburgh telegram says the • suspension of the Bank of Victoria was totally unexpected in that city, where £lO,OOO deposits have been made, but the Scot- tish deposits are chiefly through Glasgow. The banks which have suspended "pending flacon- struction in Australia were liable in the aggregate for deposits amounting to 41 millions, of which 11 millions had been receiyed from Great Britain.</p>
