      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>DARKWOOD.</head>
        <p>DARKWOOD.</p>
        <p>A TALE OF LONEIIOOE.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. HARRIETT LEWIS, Author of " The Sundered Ilearte," "The Bailin Scheme," "Time Double Life," &amp;a</p>
        <p>CHAPTER. XXX. BAULKED.</p>
        <p>ILord Darkwood an-u-o-tin-cedto his household that he was summoned to London to confer with Mr. Sutton on business. Contrary to his usuage, he declined to take his valet with him. Pietro acqui- esced in this decision without a suspicion of tilt truth underlying it, and really thought his master's absence a very fortunate thing. So the marquis ,journeyed up to Loudon, and arrived in time to secure a compartment in an express train going north, and when night came on was speeding on his way towards Yorkshire. Soon after daybreak he alighted at the Penistone and Thurlstone Junction, and found his way to the • inn—the New Rose and Crown—the same hotel which Pietro had patronised. He ordered a private room, made his toilet anew, refreshing himself with a partial bath, and then descended to the coffee room. After breakfast he ordered a horse and spring cart, and while it was being prepared he strolled in the direction of Thurlstone, and finally to an old (March, into whose grave-strewn yard he entered. Remembering what Pietro had told hini of the visit of the former to the place many years before, he sought out the lone grave in the far corner marked by the broken slab upon which was chiselled simply that name—" Magdalen." • He stood long beside that dishononred grave, staring at that name and at the weeds that grew above the mound. He was a bad man, capable of the greatest wickedness under provocation, bold and unscrupulous to the extreme. He could have seen his own child die, and smiled at her pangs, if he had deemed her in his way; but bad as he was, there was one soft spot in his heart, one cherished memory, which death only could obliterate. He had loved Clara,Markham with all his evil soul. He could never think of her without pain, and yet it was lie who had destroyed her. "And she lies here," he muttered. "She seemed made for love—a bright, capricious, loving creature, wilorn I fairly worshipped. And yet I killed her! She was proud as well as loving. She could not survive that last interview of ours, and fled, crazed and bewildered, back to the home that had sheltered her youth, to perish there in shame and agony. Clare, Clare, if the dead can hear, you know now that I, whose idol was ever self, loved you still better than myself." 1 His face wore an expression of intense bitterness. What regrets came to him in that hour heaven I alone knew. What bloodguiltiness lay upon his soul heaven also knew.</p>
        <p>"Seventeen years !" he muttered. "What lies under these weeds? Only a few bones, a tress of hair a fragment of garment. Clare is—where' Shall I meet her some (lay, and will she then accuse me of her awful death? Bah ! lam an idiot. She's dead, and there's an end. All the regrets one tould feel could not undo the past, and if it could bE undone I would not undo it. It is better as it is."</p>
        <p>He walked away, returning to the inn. His horse and cart were ready. He mounted in the vehicle, received insttuctions from his landlord, and set, out for Lonemoor. He had scarcely left behind him the small market town when the snowflakes began falling softly in a fleecy white veil. The moor wai one vast, (hoar, and desolate expanse. No person was to be seen upon it, far and near. "An awful place for a weak girl, crazed, ill, alone. in a snowstorm at night!" thought Lord Darkwood. " And it was upon this moor she perished !" . . . .</p>
        <p>Ms horse jo,ved on in the snow, picking his way bqween the borders of furze and heading for Lone. nmor, wh:eli in due time appeared in view. The marquis found the gates open, and drov( directly into the stable yard, as Pietro had done. A stable boy, openmouthed and wondering, cam( forward and held his horse.</p>
        <p>"See lier 2, my lad," said Lord Darkwood, tossing him a shilling, is Squire Markham at home ?" The boy grinned. The question seemed to him SU perlatively ridiculous. "No, sir, he bean't" he responded. "He bees genie to furrin parts these seven teen year, and isn't like to be home ag'in for seventeen more." "There's a housekeeper, I suppose ?" said the marquis glancing towards the open house door. " Yes, sir, Mrs. (linnet, she's the housekeeper." "I will see her then," said LOH. Darkwood. "Here's another shilling for you, my lad. And ah, by the way, was there a foreign man here lately to make inquiries ?" "Yes, sir, a thin, black-muzzled chap, with long thin, black legs. He was here—a Eyetalian, I think," said the lad. "He came to inquire a'ter Miss Winter." Lord Darkwood seas 'satisfied. Pietro had been at Lonemeor. Pietro was playing a double game. He set his lips firmly together in a very unpleasant expression, and strode towards the house. A maid met him at the door, and at his request conducted him to the housekeeper's room. Mrs. Quillet rose up at the entrance of the visitor, and her face was severe and forbidding as she coldly greeted the marquis. , "Have I the pleasure of seeing Mrs. Quillet, the housekeeper of Squire Markham asked Lord Darkwood.</p>
        <p>"You have, sir," said the housekeeper stiffly, disliking him at sight. "And. who are you, may I ask, sir.' Now Lord Darkwood did not wish to proclaim his name and rank. He answered, therefore— "l am Captain Tollish, madam, of Her Majesty's army. Lam come here to see you upon business of the utmost importance to myself and another. You have had for many years residing with you a young person known as Gwendoline Winter. Is it not so?" Mrs. Quillet looked her surprise, but bowed assent.</p>
        <p>"She quitted Lonemoor recently for London," continued the marquis. "I am come here to obtain her address."</p>
        <p>"Mrs. Quillet contemplated the fat, sinister face of her visitor for a moment, her dislike of him grow ing. She was keen enough to mark the craftiness in his eyes, the cruelty about the mouth, the hard- ness o his expression and she exclaimed— ' " Well, no one could help your coming, Captain Tollidge, but one thing's sure - you won't get Miss Winter's address ! There was a man here for it the other day—a foreign chap—but he went away no wiser than he came. I can't tell you where she is ?"</p>
        <p>"You mean you won't !" "Well, sir, I put it more polite," said the woman, grimly. "But it conic* to the same thing I suppose." Lord Darkwood flushed angrily, but managed to say blandly— "lt will he to your interest, madam, to give me Miss Winter's address. lam her friend, and if you'll tell me where she is, I'll give you fifty pounds." It was now 'Mrs. Qiiillet's turn to flush angrily, and her eyes snapped, as; she cried out— ' " I wouldn't tell you now, sir, net to save your life. Fifty, pounds to me! A bribe to me ! You don't know who you're talking to, Air. We come of decent families, John and me, and this is the first time we wero ever offered bribes." .</p>
        <p>"I beg your -pardon, madam;" &amp;pologised the marquis. "But you see it is very important to Meto discos-or Miss Winter-",</p>
        <p>"If it is so important, its singular you did'nt come years ago," sneered the housekeeper. "I could not do so. /ti'ow I am prepared to educate Miss Winter—to make e lady of tier—and I must find her. I knew her mother, Mrs. Quillet, and what, I desire to do for the child is only a mat- ter of justice !</p>
        <p>,lle at down, and removing his damp gloves, stretched out his hands to the b:azo.</p>
        <p>" You knew Miss Winter's Inothr ?" said the housf!keeper' slowly. ''You know I,ier mother r . • " Yes, I knew her,".a.aAa-ered Lord' Darkwood, wondering- a little at the wornan's strange toe. " And you knew her, also, although you've kept the se,:ret well. M 153. Winter's mother WEIS your foriner;miatreas, Mins— •</p>
        <p>t*.fitish !" cried the hre,r,(4:r.eper, sternly. ." Do. not speak that name liere. The wtlis have ears ! You riy that you kne7,..-• iss 'Winter's mother. When did you k'novii her, !</p>
        <p>"Both before and after the ann6ttheement of her death !"_</p>
        <p>Qmillet's eye!: note/carefully the fineness of. her 'visitor's dre,s, thc iings" upon his lingers.' Lie was evidently rich, and As evidently he belonged to the:upper class. nad he ever Lee n one to w,in, the love of a romantic girt ?. Seventeen years _ago, this man might have been a girl's ben). _</p>
        <p>"You knew her both before and after that announcement," said Mrs. QuiHet. •" Did you love her?"</p>
        <p>man's face changed. "Love here" he repeated. "I worshipped her! Ah, if I had loved her she rAght.havo been at•this moment alive and happy'!" He uttered the WOrtli half unconsciously. "It was you, then, who tilled her I " whispered the woman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>t;</p>
        <p>lie „iowed his head. • Mrs. Quillet's face was ghastly pale. She re- treated a few steps in horror of hun. "You killed her—body and soul !" she said, "You destroyed the sweetest, fairest creature that ever lived on this earth. May your riches slip from your grasp, and the curse of her wandering father and of her old nurse be upon you, living and dying ?" Lord Darkwood shivered, then laughed uneasily. "Yu ought to go on the stage, Dame Quillet, YOU do the tragic admirably," he exclaimed. "I have done no murder. Why accuse and abuse me so insanely? I knew her, and I loved her. I will provide for her child—" • - • You will not! What the child is she owes to heaven and me She shall not take one penny of your money, Captain Tollish. I'd rather see her dead than under the protection of. the man who destroyed her mother—were that man ten times her father !" .. • ,</p>
        <p>"Then you Utterly refuse to tell me where she is?" • "I do refuse !"</p>
        <p>." You refuse her the advantages I could give her? You refuse for her a luxurious home, refined companionship, the training of a'litcly ?" "I do refuse. I'd, rather she'd die in a gutter than be raised to a throne by the hand tbat killed her mother Go !"</p>
        <p>The marquis saw that he might as well appeal to stone as, to this woman, and walked slowly to, the door.</p>
        <p>' "Let me tell you, woman," he blustered, halting at the 'threshold; "that I will find her wherever she is hidden ! And when I find her she will belong to me !"</p>
        <p>With a satonic smile, he crossed the threshold and returned to the stable yard. . . . .</p>
        <p>"And that is Miss Gwen' father !" ejaculated the housekeeper. ." That is the man who killed my darling! May retribution come to him in the hour when, he thinks his greatest triumph won!,,</p>
        <p>Did that wild prayer ascend to the throne of Him who avenges the wronged? And would that prayer be answered?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CHANTER XXXL A GIiOST !</head>
        <p>CHANTER XXXL A GIiOST !</p>
        <p>Lord Darkwood lingered in the stable yard to interrogate the ostlers concerning Miss Winter, dis- daining no source whence might come information upon the question he so much desired to solve. The stableman who had declared to Pietro that Miss Winter could not compare in beauty to coarse, red-cheeked Meg, made the same statement to the marquis; and, oddly enough, the latter received it as truthful and just, forgetting that a rude ostler's ideas of beauty may ditler widely from those of the cultivated, refined, and intelligent- . .</p>
        <p>"Not pretty, eh '!" thought Cord Darkwaxl. "It would be very singular if she were. Ignorant of course, unrefined, and not as pretty as that brawny bouncing milkmaid ! Snell is Clare Niarkliam's ehid while Clare Alarcluim herself was lovely as a poet's dream."</p>
        <p>_ He was about to resume his questionings, when a waggonette, drawn by a stout cob, drove' into the yard, and Mr. Orkndy, the land bailiff, alighted. " Who is he ?" asked the Marquis. "That's the land bailiff," replied the ostler. "He knows all about Miss Winter. Better ESPCiIi: to him, sir."</p>
        <p>Lord. Darkwood hastened to act upon this advice. He approached the bailiff, who paused, looking at him superciliously.' . • "I beg pardon, sir," said the marquis airily. "I am Captain 'Mash; of the army. Have I the honour of addressing Mr.7-1111—Mr. —Orkney, the land bailiff of Squire Markham l" . . .</p>
        <p>"You hit;e, sir," said the bailiff, somewhat stiffly. "1 have called here to obtain the address of Miss Winter,". said Lord Dark wood. "The housekeeper ref uses to give it to me. Can you oblige me with it 2" .</p>
        <p>"1 cannot," said Mr. Orkney, who from dint ot often hearing Gwen's name at home from his Wife and daughters, and not in approving terms, had. grown to hate it. "The young woman is gone to London. She should have been put in a foundling hospital in her infancy, or brought up as a servant. But she has high ideas of herself, has Miss Whiter. Wherever she is, she'll soon find her level l" "Has she friends in London ?"</p>
        <p>"1 believe not. Such as she do not have friencls,"' Bald Mr. Orkney. " She left no friends here either. Even the Quillets, who brought her up, are tired of her, and glad to he relieved of her support." " London is scarcely a safe refuge for a friendless girl," observed Lord Darkwood, thoughtfully. Have you any idea what she may be doing in town? Is she likely to have become a servant ?" "Ndlie. She has probably gone to the bad." The Marquis started. - "You think so? She is not a favourite of yours, Mr. Orkney 7" "No, sir. She is a Delilah—a Circe—a siren," cried r. Orkney energetically. "She is lower than the lowest milkmaid at Lonemoor in social position and birth, but she contrives to bewitch men. I have a son now in London whose one thought is to find ker. The Quilleti very properly refused him her address, but he is searching everywhere for her, being bent upon marrying her. She had another suitor, a sportsman, who spent a month or two at the shooting lodge with a party of friends., He was a regular nob. He meant. her no good, my wife told me, and the Quillets sent him away." ' "The girl must be attractive." "That's a matter .of opinion," said Mr. Orkney. shruggint,e his shoulders. ." I don't like the girl, and I don't like her looks. Have you seen her; sir How comes it that you are inquiring after her ?" "I have never seen her," replied his lordship. "I am employed by a friend to search for a younk woman who ran away from her home' some smien • teen years ago. Hearing that a young woman hail appeared in this vicinity at about that tune, I mill*: here to make enquiries. I have seen the grave (it Miss Winter's mother, but Ibat tells me nothing. •If I could see Miss Winter, I might trace' in he features, some resemblancb that might put an end to my doubts and inquiries." "Miss Winter undoubtedly resembles her mothei in disposition, if not in features," declared tie: &amp;Miff, grimly. "I regret I can render. you ne assistance whatever. If the Quillets will not tell you where the girl is, no one else can." Ile bowed and moved on. Lord DairkWoOil climbed into his spring cart vai.; drove out of the yard. His investigations had resulted in the formatimi of certain fixed opinions, the chief of which wa. that Gwen was awkward and ill-favion red, igoorani and into and unprincipled, yet somehow posses:day: a certain sort of attractiveness which had won hei the attentions ef two. Men. "Even the homeliest Gill has her Jack," he mused. "Meg yonder has her admirer in that stable load • So the girl has admirers. But it's clear that she not a proper young person. She will drift in:: evil ways and die young if left to herself. I wo:11.1 leave her to herself but that I fear Pietro will fin.: her. No', I must discover her Iltst, and get he: beyond his reauli. Being the girl she is, she'd- marry, Pietro at a jump. I have not waked to the danger a moment too soon." lie returned through the falling snow over tlis bare bleak moor to Peniatone. Threading thn crowded street' ho returned to the New' Rose Crown, and, to a private parlour therein. Here he was served with dinner. The day wore away slowly enough, and he was promptly at the railway station as the time drew near for the night express to depart. He had seen no one at Penistont who knew him, tied as he pacet the platform he congratulated himself Upon the whole ort the manner in which lie 01 conducted his investigations. . "Pietro learned no more than I did," hesolilo; quised. "I'll go back to Shropshire and watch fni his letters from his beother. I like the idea ol making Pietro the eatspw.v to snatch my chestnut,: from the fire.. If he should ever come .here again, -Nnd hear thatlCaptain Tollish had been here, he'd b.igin to think that I was cleverer than he ha II ven me Credit for."</p>
        <p>, He laughed softly in anticipation of Pier cliscomfitUre. The steamed into the station. He secured a finit-chos cc.sinpartment, which, by dint of bribes, be ret tined to his own sole use throughout his journey to London,</p>
        <p>He airivcl in town next morning, proceeded to an hotel. subsequently called Upon Mr. Sutton, made me purchases, received a few calla, and so spen'r., the day to account for his journey to town, be thenglit, with antisfaet ion, deeming it .probable that Pietro might entertain some curiosity as to his mover:len:a, ancl seek to investigate them. The 11','Xt. Lty he ro tuned to 'Shropshire. De had t ch-rt-va::d his butler to send the carriac.,e to mt et him, bud the carriage whit attendants w,ls at the Pi;;:t.i,rn when hoorayed. His stout thorough- breds or Mtn speedily to Dunholm Cattle, and he hastened up to his rootna to dress for dinner.. Pietr&amp; a-as in attendance,, as his lordship was pleased to tee Ho had balf-feared,. half-expected OA Pietro had been summoned to town. Thc valet had laid out a dress suit for his lordship, and was busy in the drawing-room when the intte-; eatcret.l.</p>
        <p>A single glance at the face of the Illnltese con- vinced his master that he had received no reply as yet to either .of his advertisomAnts, and also that he had conceived no suspicions of tho real. cause of imt,tcr's absence. •"111, Pietro," Said his lordship pleasantly. "here I am again: I was detained a day 1-ong•er • than I. en!)ccted, tart have made some valma,lc Ito roli:ises--deao, bargains, too. Lord Dark wood can IMy clinaoer than, Captain Tollish could, I find. koy news,r. • , None; • signet", ,'• " be new governess getting on well 7" inquired -1/m1 I) familiarly. familiarly. • "Or has the Lady • Georgina turned her away, or wearied her, out 7" ' "Neithcr, my: lord," said Pietro. "The new governm.s and the Lady Georgina are already the dtvtrest or friends." "'Glad te hear it. Dress me, Pietro, and be quick</p>
        <p>about it." . , The valet obeyed. • Lind Dark woqd, as soon as dressed, descended to the drawipin--rooin. He did not send for his daughter. _ . ..... _.</p>
        <p>- - , nor. did she come to welcome his return. Teen: mis /late 16'4 between the-two. -He thought more about the beanfiful young governess.during these moments of Waiting than he did of his daughter, Gwen tniving made a striking impression upon him. He dined alone in state, and after dinner returned</p>
        <p>to the drAwing-room. It was full of art treasures, of which he had not examined the half, and he spent bouts in examining them, and exulting in hi sense Of ownership. There .were rare pictures on the .wails, beautiful. statuary, portfolios of engravings and etchings, Indian cabinets filled with coins, with shells, with specimens illustrative of natural histoic. Ocistly'artidltS of wrtu, and a thousand interesting articles from every corner of the earth.</p>
        <p>Lord Darkwooii amused himself in looking thes,4 over, reading their labels, and speculating as t 4 their money value, whilst his thoughts were luit7 with Clare Markham's child and Pietro's designs.</p>
        <p>He grow restless and ill at ease as the hours wore on. A singular gloom fell upon his spirits. lie paced the floor, he sought in vain to interest him- self, but he could not shake off the oppression tha had seized upon him. At eleven o'clock he. retired to the library. Tll€ butler locked up the castle, extinguished the drtivi-- ing•room lights, and was dismissed to bed. The household was wrapped in silence. .4"fhey are all gone to bed," said Lord Darkwood to himself. "Pietro waits for -me, but the rest steep. I feel somehow as if I should never skop again. How stiffing the room is l" 'Ho i-ent to one of the French windows and</p>
        <p>opened it. He stood in the aperture some mint-ties, breathing in eagerly the fresh night air, in which Was the chill of earthly winter, The night wgs dark and starless. The cas'le rains lay as steeped in blackness. No lights gleamed from the . now, inhabited divelling,'-except that which poured in a yellow ilrearn from. the oven French window of the library. Lord Dal kwood. • turned away, walking back an forth with long and hasty strides. • , 4This oppressiort grows upon me," he muttered. "What is it ails We ? I feel so strange I What.s that?" The little.fievres clock on the mantel chimed the .hour of twelve. It was midnight. "Even the silver bells of the clock sound strangelY to-night: I'll shut the windows and get to bof.L • Even the chatter of that rascal Pietro is better than thin solitude, so full of creeping terror " Ho turned to retrace his steps- to the windowhalted—a low,- agonised cry bursting from his lip • There, in the open. French .window, stood ttlg strange and ghastly figure of a man regarding hitt; with weird intentness—a figure-locking spectral aj,( unreal in the yellow glare of,the.dight against blank background of. the night.:l:• • "A ghost ! " said the mareitt,. in a hollow whlsl?,:4 "A ghost I Heaven have mercy on me 1" (To be continued).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>APID REA D Eitk3.</head>
        <p>APID REA D Eitk3.</p>
        <p>• , Macaulay possessed the faeulti of rapid reading in high degree. He would take up a volume for an over;i lag's intellectual enjoyment,. and before he retired the contents fully impressed upon hs marvellous Min' Dickens was another of those reraarkahly rapid reader.;' George Elliott's "'Adam Bede "came to him one ti",(i Before his ordinary be,dtime he had read it, and 1389 prononneed this remarkable dictum . That book %Ore written by a woman." Cthers required days of leisno to read it. and thetquestion "of autlior,hip waS t r riddle of the time in literai, circles. Charles Butvneit." was another man who possessed this happy faculty. • book whether it was a volume or law or ht _ _ . - his</p>
        <p>pr, correspondence or ft work of fiction. passed under ,9 eyes as if by a quick succession oi glances. It was tua !same with Daniel Webster, who himself stated t°, „- friend tat when in college he read Don Quixote : a single tight. In the cash of hut h tles distinguisn men, what they read in this way reappeared in a n dress in their speeches and in their writines• to. e course a retentive memdry was neeeesary to render results of this rapid reading. ilVaikalle, but it is PI4I Asa thht in their capacity to read rapidly they possesenormous advantage over their fellow men.</p>
        <p>1 POLITICAL . PARTY NAMES. ' The origin of Liberal rind Radical, ,it is ! impossible to trace; but there can be no doubt 01./11 he Word Conservatiyee. .ws firs, used in an firtiel°- , the "Quarterly Review " by Croker in the year and that it was readily aSsurned by the Tr-ry , who upon the passing of the Reform Act were a f Icredited party,and were notuuwilliug to get rid° 0, flame which carried with it associations of °we popular kind. "We despise,"said Crrker,"and abouhls the details of partisan warfare, but iae now are, a° oo alyeays have been decidedly' and tionseientioUsls. 'tacked to what is called the Tory party, and Which o more propriety might he, callechthe Conservative par 1.70%. By that message Croker has actieyod an immortal reno tation. The asset:uplink of the name was resentet;.k, the Whigs, and Macauley- called it " a new cant, vi',',lol ,Even in 1837 the name was comparatively stranger "If se that year Lord Joon Russell thus declared Stroud : " If that is the name that pleimes then), if,ra say that the obi distinction 'of Whig and Tory show 40 longer be kept up, lam ready, in opposition to the to of Conservative, to take the name of Relorrnerr 3nririer standly that opposition.!' But the sord Rei°' do- seeme never to hay, become popular, and Liberal o:pee ally took the place of Whig. The gradual disaprearf toe rof the names Whig and Tory is a good instance i Ivey ° • lc go - riche° n which party names, given originally la t and contempt, make way for natreit of a better td kiocio with a fuller meaning, and More ciiiirouant critli_nott Igreater polish of a later age. The oid names fall the ear like the echo cf a bygone time. The Word woo is still used, no doubt, but chiPtt?tithe Libera?',. it io employ it expressly to 'dificredin their opponent St lo' OP not a name in which the modern Conservative seito feels any inclination to ot Ty, though iu suCha p!'rio re' , Tory Democracy an attempt is sometimes thaue d vive it. The name Whig is still