      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT TRADES</p>
        <p>COUNCIL,</p>
        <p>INTERESIING OEM OYSTRATION.</p>
        <p>A very successful., elemenstration was held at Widnes on Sattiirlaee tinder the anepiees of Widnes Trades Council. Although this Council has been in existence for two or, elieee years sad has done gni service in a qu:et WaY; it had et: ver eiteyieusly vette turd upon a demonstration, and Saturday's pro- ceedings , evoked gieat intereet, thrieighout the ! town. The proCessiote formed on the Me'rsey inenade, Mr. J. Byrnes' being; the 'Chief letir;hall, rind the trades were represented in the folk; vitig order :—Bakers, tailors, 'aseurance agente, joi item Coopers, bricklayere, plumber., painters, end deco- rators, There would be 'about 750 skilled werkinen in , the procession, and as banners were flying and the em- blems of the various ejatteesvete eerried aloft it was full of interest. Gostiages, Sebscription Jubilee, and none corn Town brass tends, led the bakers, coepers, brick- layers and plumbers. respeetively, playing inspiriting airs. After traversing the Own to the bottom of Peel- house-lane in Albert-road, the prier scion returned to the west side of St. Paul's Church -a' plate-mete lied been eiected and a meeting was held. 'Above 2,0001 Or- sone were present. Mr.'. Wade Deacon preeided: The Chairman congratulated the Members upon the magnificent demonstration which they„. had un&amp;le that day, and said no one who saw the procession IaHSS along the street could help being impressed by throorderlinesse , steadiness and earnestness et the men who compoaed.it,' 1 (Applause.) The object of their meeting was to express ; their. sympathy with the growth of trades unionism.• Thoughmuch might bts dene by leeeslation, he was con- vinced that the great thing for the labouring classee was to rely upon their own exert eerie. It depended very much upon the men themselves whet her t boy got, elierter hours, and improved Conditions of life, and it was there- fore with great pleasure that he had • an; oppnetunity of showing what sympathy he could with the efforte et the men to combine together , for their own Mutual safe- guard and improvement. (Applause.) He looked upon combination as the best if not the only .way iii whiOh men could gain their rights. (Hear, hear.)., Some people said mistakes were made; and that combinations went wrong. Of course they did. As long as men were human beings se long would they make mistakes and so from time to time combinations would make mistakes, but that did not say that there nnis anything better to put in their place. (Hear, hear!) The- speaker men- tioned that it was comparatively easy to organise skilled labour but difficult to organise unskilled labour, and said his hearers would epee With him in deprecating tile exercise of violence; it must not be exhibited if trade, uniontsm.WAS to prosper. It was an unfortunates thing that at a strike, there were always men hanging (demi who would not. work, and who were always .ready to kick up a row, and who brought discredit upon the strike at large_ (Applause.) etrikes were always air evil and his reason for supportitut trades unionism was that he was satisfied that by thorough union they would have fewer strikes; leseeined severity, and better feeling all round, and that they whuld see a great deal. accum- plished by arbitration inetead of vine, to war and strik- ing. (Applause.) There was a great future before trades unioniern. • (Appleeve.).• Mr. T. B. Threlfal, if Southport, reeved " That this meeting expresses its continued pdherence ti eraele Union principles, and records its heartiest satisfaction at the extent of their innueric.i. it re,peettully im- presses on working met, oho are T:4lt yet seine:ere tee absolute necessity f joining ttaiir ri spective donna: and thus strengthen them in,this ate ula hr the rights 4I the toiling masses, it also regards with s mptithy the question of laflieur representation, and trusts that it will .in the near future, • secure more thorough 'and united support." Mr. 'flirelfalt pointed out that there were several important industries that dil not belong 0 tile Trades Council. If it was desirable fer the eceemon good that men should cembine in trade organiz it ione. It was equally necessary and important anti for the common good that these trades.shonld ratite in forming a Trades Council. He therefore hoped .that the printers, the ironworkers and engineers and chemical werkers and other industries in Widnes would at the first favourable opportunity thrown in their lot with the Trades Celina and assist in doing something in elevating the people : and educating public opinion and bring in outsiders to find salvation in the ranks ()eta lemr. Labour stood in its present noble position in the van of Progress by its ' unaided exertions! What would' they de in the next men or twenty years, when' all that was wise noble and progressive in the country was behind rilein and helping forward the chrine of leuotir. (Applause). The speaker described how, during the last, quarter of a century, improvements had been effected in the wegesehours at labour, and conditions of working in numerous trades in this country by means of Trades Ussione. time Abject. of Trades Unions Was not to leaky the rich poorer, but to make the poor richer -by giving them a fair day's wage for a fair day's work, and tie urged the importance of getting labour members in the House of Commenter he better to accomplish this. They bad got a Town Council that employed labour of various kinds, and his ad ice was to strengthen their Trades Council, arid when next November came,send to that Council a number ot labour members, and thus he able to stop a goad many things that hold againet labour. (Applause.) Mr. J. Jenkins, general secretary of Bakers, London, seconded the motion. He said if workingmen wished to improve their position, there was only mitre way of doing it ;' they must combine together to help one another in doing that which they could not do singly. As to politics he bad been a Radical for twenty years, and tie had come to the conclusion that there was very little difference between the Tory landlord and the Liberal , capitalist, and his advice to Trades Unionists was to use 1 their votes for their own benefit and not for a leery or: Liberal. He was strongly in favour of a statutory' enactment of an eight hours day, same as the landlords I obtained when they got hold of the land. The landlords ; of this country received 60 millions a year in rent and they paid less than a million a year in wages for culti- vating the land. That was a nice state of affairs. Work was noble, but in order to make it noble, men must do as much work in a given number of hours as ; they could without hurting their bodies. Woikingmen had come to the belief that they did not get their fair share of pleasure, and they intended to have it. They wanted more practical Christianity and not so much Churchianity in this country. (Applause.) They did not want Christianity on the basis of an Archbishop at £15,000 a year, while thous inds of people starved. (Applause.) In Widnes there were some good shops and some bad shops in ilk tradeesed they were going to try to ' make the sweater in Widnes into an honest man. They wanted the people of Widnes to help them. They not want any money but simply that they would not ' buy bread from sweaters. (Applause.) The resolution was carried unaitimously. Mr. W. Davies, ex-chairmau of the Amalgamated ; Society of Carpenters and Joiners, moved That this ; meeting approves of the Employers Liability Bill, intro- educed by the Home Secretary, and trusts that this long delayed measure may speedily becotne the law of the land." The present bill was a greet step in advent, of the old act. fie ;mentioned the. great advantages of trades unions being organised into a trades council, and he expressed the hope that the trade he represented would join the Widnes Council at an early date. Councillor Shaft° J.P., of Bradford, seconded the motion, and observed that trades unions had been the elevating force ot the workingocracy of this country. He contended that strong unions would protect the , rights of property a great deal better than the military. The motion was carried. Mr. Gardiner, chairman of the Whine.; Trades Council, ; moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman and speakers. The Council were proud to have been so well supported at their first demonstration. • Mr. A. G. Lowry (secretary) seconded the motion,and remarked that although only a small council and recently formed, they had succeeded in getting both the Corporation and the School Board to pass the Sweating Clause, that was that every tradesman employed by these bodies would have to pay the trade union rate of wages. In securing this the Council had donesoinething to earn the confidence of the trade Unionists of Whines. The resolution was carried with enthusiasm and Mr. Deacon responded, and alluding to some of the speeches said he believed an eight' hours day throughout the kingdom an absolute impossibility. Afterwards the members of the Council and the visi- tors partook of dinner at the Central Hotel. Mr. Foster, the proprietor, catering in a satisfactory manner. Mr. A. G. Lowry presided and he was supported by Mr. H. Wade Deacon, Mr. T. R. Threlfall, Mr. W. Davies, Mr. J. Jenkins, Mr. Councillor Shaft°, and Messrs. J. Gar- diner (chairman of Council), J. Clarke (treasurer), R. Carlisle (vice-chairman), T. E. Dykes, E. Guest, J. Lewis, T. Hampson, W. Parr, S. Tideswell, J. Byrne, A. Sammons, E. Hardman, J. McLeod, Tatton, ft. Jones, J. McCabe, Caldery, Nerman, Mm acockeand J. Pearson. ; Mr. Clarke gave the toast of "Trades Unionism " to which he said he had served an apprenticeship for the past 21 years, and he believed that combination was tire, worker's only hope. Mr. Threlfall responded, he was proud and happy that the trades Unionists of that district had formed a Trades Ceuncil. It was 0. very healthy sign indeed. If the members of the Council did their duty it would become a powerful influence for good in the district, and would really lead all the great social and industrial movements on behalf of the working classes. It was a grand and inspiring thought that as labour had emancipated itself it had not become a revolutionary agency. As greater freedom had come upon lebour, so had it become more peaceable, and so much stronger had the constitution of the country become. (Hear, hear.) He hoped that the Trades Council and its Unions would thoroughly carry pred ictpedastshaat out these splendid and noble traditions of labour. He generaltt'e g Househ futurehours day. would in the Mr. Gardiner gave "The Visitors" and -</p>
        <p>Mr. Deacon, in responding, remarked that Mr. Threl- fall thought the hours of labour could be limited to eight per day with satisfactory results. What was the experi- ence of other countries. In Australia the workmen had for a long time the eißht hours thy in operation, and in that country in 1890 there was a universal strike</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>amongst the shearers. That strike utterly failed because there was a large number of unemployed men in Austra- lia who took the place of the men on strike. That was a new cauntry where everything was favourable.for thy workmen. Si' that it should he bOrneiin mind when .was seriously proposed that an eight -hours day would cure the unemployed question. In his epiuion it was not a cure, but it would to some extent for a time limit the number of unemployed. The question of the unem- ployed Was the question of the future, and it was time for Government to recognise the (act that something must be done to solve the question. It would not be easy, some great change would be necessary before its accomplishment, but there were palliatives which could be applied in the meantime. There could be organised e labour bureau to collect reliable statistics of the unem- ployed, and the workers in the various trades ; local authorities could execute works of public usefulness in times Of depression. A reform of village life, so that the men should be &amp;Wet° live there and maintain them- selves and families with respect, and be able to lay by something for the future. In this way help ought to be given to the unemployed. (Applause.) Mr. Jenkins proposed " Stigmas to Widnes Trades Council." He hored the Council would succeed in gett- ing, all trades affiltited, and then try to Put their COM- bination to practical usefulness. There was a vast a mount of work to be done in cleaning up the thorough- fares and backshops ; they were a disgrace to any town in the world. The Council could put pressure upon the Corporation to have this state of things remedied. If he thought he had to stay the remainder of his days in Widnes be wriuld go down to the river and coma* suicide. (Laiieliter,) The smiling valleys had been turned into cinder heaps to make a few men rich at the expense of the general community —that was what had practically taken place in Widnes. The lives of the workers, all decenoy, or of anything in the shape of I comfort, had been sacrificed to the god Mammon in that town mon than in any place he had visited in his life,' He knew Eneland from one end to the other' and he had never seen any town half so miserableas Widnes. They had got eemething to do, and he hoped they would de it. Dealing with the eight hours question, Mr. Jen- kins said he would not argue that it was a panacea for all the ills that flesh was heir to hut it would have a more , immediate effect upon the labour market surplus than anything likely to take place during the next 20 years. It was dot possible to obtain their salvation by any one method, and something might also be done in the direc- tion indicated ny. Mr. Deacon. The land laws would have to be altered. But supposine the eight hours day eranted, there would be instantly 50,000 men wanted on the railway Biwa. In Lxidon alone they would want 10,000 oi 1.1,000 mire bakers. What had been a success in the textile industries would he a success all round. The Chairman responded and said he had not noticed that Widnes was such a God foreetton place as Mr. Jenkins wished them to believe. When they got used to it; it was not so very bad after all. AA the instigator of the Trades Council it was a pleasure to see the success achieved so far, but he hoped next year that they would be in a stronger positian.</p>
