aced lathe iron mine, or else in tlw coal pit. Etch country is striving to supply its own wantsetni naturally so, and the country that is content to allow a few men to lead paupers lives by drawing, aegb slime as royalties, orbo keep land lying idle as it is doing in England at the present time must go down. But thiS cannot he; a great change is coming—has come. Here, in the States, strikes were never BO numerous, shorter hours, cod more pay, is the popular demand. Belgium and Germany seem to be almost in a state of civil war. In England from Hull to Liverpool disaffec- tion has culminated in riot In Lancashire, labour and (Vital have shaken hands,and agreed to try to be wiser. Federation? Yes, but it will be the federation of work- men. Who will bring it about ? Not the workman. He doesn't seem to have the brains. Possibly, there may be some more feathers about who will improve on the preeent one, and not ask that ten bouts work shall be crowded into eight hours, because competition de- mands it.</p>
        <p>GAS AND WATER.</p>
        <p>The Committee rep vied that samples of water from Stock's Well and Netherley had been submitted to Dr. Campbell Brown for analysis, who reports both samples to be clear and colourless and of excellent quality. It is important and interesting to note from the foregoing analysis the decrease in the hardness, and more particu- Ley so in regard t• the Netherley sample. This was taken from No. 2 borehole, which is 575 feet deep, and up through %Melt comes the hulk of the itxreased yield of water obtained from these borings,thus proving that the quality in the lower strata of the rock to be so satisfactory. Restilved—That the charges for ws ter suphlied for building purposes he as follows, viz : Within the borough-sa. per £lOO of building contract price. Outside the borough-10s. per £lOO of building contract price, or, at the Option of Lila person requiring Ithe aupply,by meter at the rate of charges then in force, ' provided that this resolation shall be subject to the .fodowing regulations, -viz :—" Every person taking a supply o water otherwise than by tncter, shall provide for the prevention of waste or misuse of water, a strong box, fitted with a lock and key, for covering the standpipe and tap, the same to be approved ot by the Corporation." Supplies for small jobbing and building repairs—by arrangement. Alderman Tiinmis moved the adoption of the minutes and said they had hOW got over their difficnlties at Netherley. The extra expenses would have to be borne by the contractor.—Councillor Davies seconded— carried.—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL CLUB. -- OFFICIAL STATEMENT OF THE YEAR'S WORK.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL CLUB. -- OFFICIAL STATEMENT OF THE YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>A SUCCESSFUL SEASON.</p>
        <p>In the annual report which BUJ. E. Warren, sec of the Warrington Football Club, will submit tO. the annual meeting next Wet k,he gives an analysis of the year's scoring which we published last week and al.q) makes the following statement : SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Netwithstanding the many drawbacks which the Club has had to contend with during the past season I me p!eased to be in a position to give a very satisfac- tory report of the condition of the club's affsirs. Tue inauguration of a League competition by the Courey Committee, or perliape I should say the adoption of a League Scheme, which had already been put into partial , working order by the secretaries of most. of the clubs at present forming the first division gave an impetus to the Rugby game at the commencement of the season, which in some parts of the county was much needed. ! The scheme was adopted so near the commencemeet of the season, that it was found to be impossible to put all the rules into force, and in consequence has only been a partial succe,s. Our team has come out of the ordeal very creditably, securing fourth elace in the list. ' Next season the rule debarring clubs from playing any hut duly qualified players will be strictly enforced ; in consequence, I have little doubt that our team will do still better. A local Junior Club League Competition , worked,on practically the same lines as the Lancashire ! League, has been br might to a successful conclusion, the Parish Ceurch winning. The club is indebted to our President, Mr. Percy Davies, for the cup and medals given, as well as to Asssits. Rowe, Gilbert, Bowers. and Hewitt, who composed the sub-committee to work the competition. Some uneasiness was felt at the corn- mencement of the season that we might lose our field at an early date, in consequence of the passing of the Derbyshire and East Coast Railway Bill. but as yet we have had no notice to quit, and the probability seems to be that it will be some time, before the field will be wanted ; still if the railway ever does reach Warrington, , one field will be wanted, and it will be well to keep this in mind and provide as far HA possible against such an eventuality. Our Annual Sports were a complete suc- cess, several noted athletes competing. Our thanks are due to the Mayoress (Mrs. Greenall), who took a very kindly interest in the meeting and presented the prizes at the conclusion of the sports. The smallpox epidemic which was raging in the town in November and Decem- ber was answerable for a number of our fixtures being either cancelled or postooned, the abandonment of the annual tour which is always a source of enjoyment being especially unfortunate for the fist team. Two of our players, James Jolley and Joseph Boscow, have attained " County Honours " this season, Boscow assist- ing Lancashire as full-back against Cumberland, York- shire, Durham, and Glatnorganshire, in addition to these Jolley played" forward " aeainst Cambridge University arid Davon: A very regrettable incident occurred on the occasion of the Salford match at Warrington on April 17th., when a section of the spectators who disagreed with the decisions of the referee " mobbed " that official on his departure from the field, in consequence of a hich our ground was suspended. I can only imprego on our can to discoun- tmenemaibLrssteficehnee.preuestedy attainted by it, and apart from the less of gate caused ionfgds.oinLallptoisiesible good can be y by a suspension, is not readily effaced. Iu last seat's report I mentioned a stigma is placed upon the club which ' with some degree of pride that the feud which existed between our club and Runcorn had been settled and matches arranged. I regret to find, as circumstances have proved, that the old bitterness still exists on the Runcorn side. We fulfilled our fixture at Runcorn and ! were then informed by the Runcorn Secretary that owing to the disapproval of our style of play, the heme fixture as well as next year's fixtures must be cancelled. TA proceed to the doings of our teams I find the first ae 3nt c 4 h beatenr ne sa,t2c 1h matches, since 1; 1 88w8l won 18t, 6; 77 the or drawn 6. ;tree' ti srn,alwv) have virtateigi pewl al e' ya se dp nl a3oyt6 e dbme drawn. A fact worth noting is that out of the 8 matches lost, 5 have been lost by a bare try to nil, and one by a dropped g0a1...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>'IiOW THE" A "TEAM WORKED.</head>
        <p>'IiOW THE" A "TEAM WORKED.</p>
        <p>The "A "ream under the captaincy of Mr. T. Davies has bad another Successful season, and so well did the team perform that it was not until March the llth, its victorious career was checked. Mlstortunes then ensued and four additional matches were lost, but the team lied the satisfaction of knowing that no defeat wag inflicted on their own. ground. 27 matches have been played of which 20 were won. 5 lost, and 2 drawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SECOND TEAM.</head>
        <p>THE SECOND TEAM.</p>
        <p>The season is remarkable for the large number of players whb have taken part in the matches, no fewer than 70 players having played in the various matches. This seems to show that we are not likely to lack reserves for our other teams as out of such a number of pliyers we cannot tail eventually to develop talent in time. There were 20 matches played 8 won, 8 drawn, 4 lost. These were the scorers :—Gibson, 7 tries ; Rowe, 6 tries; Joseph Lawless, 4 tries ; Daniels, Smith, and Thornton, 3 tries each; Threadgold, J. E. Speakman, Russell, For- ster, and Potts, 2 eanh ; C. Daniels, Houghton, Woods, Hinchcliffe, F. flampson, Eden, Barrett, Dakin, Garrety Slater, and Hughes, 1 try each; dropped goal Etamblett, 1 ; penalty goal, Phillip; r ; placed goals, Beldsworth, 5 Speakman, Frank Turner, and Harnblett, 2 each ;J. Warburton, J. 1.4 WIPES, 1 each. ATTENDANCE OF COMMITTEE. The folio wing for forty-two meetings is the attendance of members :—T. Bowers, 42; W. Hewitt, 42 ;J. E. Warren, 40 ; T. Pemberton, 39; E. Gilbert, 38; J. Jolley 37; W. Speakman, 37 ;J. Boyle, 34 :T. Barnes, 33; W. Povey, 33 • G. Smith, 33 ; H. Ashton, 32; J. Evans, 32 ; T. Davies, 31 ; Percy Davies, 30 ; W. Antrobus, 23 ; H. Rowe, 22 ; J. T. Bate, 21. TREASURER'S REPORT. Mr. T. Pemberton, the hon. treasurer. In his annual I report, makes the following encouraging statement o— " After contending with the epidemic, floods, and ' failure of clubs to keep their engagements, and the ground being suspended, I am glad to ;port that the club is still in a flourishing condition. The receipts show a falling off in subscriptions and gate money. The sports were a great success financially and other- wise. In the items of expenditure I be to call your attention to the Insurance Fund. The premiums charged by the Insurance Societies are so large, and the scale of payments for injuries so small, that the committee, after careful consideration have unanimously agreed to adopt a scheme of losorance for next season as follows : 'To take 10 per cent, of the gross gate to meet all I claims for injuries sustained by the playing members.' I Last season our balances in the Lancashire and York- shire Bank, and the Savings Bank, amounted to £lOO 9s. 3d., less £1 14s 40. due to myself, and as late as February last, through the before mentioned adverse circumstances, the Committee were compelled to draw upon the reserve fund deposited at the Savings B t ilk, but I am pleased to inform you that we have been she to re-deposit the amount, and finish the season with a balance in hand of £55 18s. Bp., less £ll ls. 80., due to the Junior Rugby League Competition."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CA !ITER HANGS HIMSELF AT WARRINGTON. AN UNPUNCTUAL JURYMAN.</head>
        <p>A CA !ITER HANGS HIMSELF AT WARRINGTON. AN UNPUNCTUAL JURYMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at the Warrington Infirmary on Tuesday, on the body of Wm. Norman aged 52 years, who corn initt.d suicide by hanging himself on Monday at 17. Dixon-street. The inquest was fixed for 12 o'cinek, but Mr. Albinson, a juryman did not arrive until 1220, and the inquest WAR delayed in consequence. When Mr. Allinson ar- rived, the coroner asked him how it was that he was so late to which he replied that he had been very busy and could not get away —Mr. Brighouse " Here are eleven of the jury and myself who have been waiting twenty minutes. I find that the total amount of time lost by us all is 240 minutes or four hours. I never like to be unfair, but I do think that punctuality w.uld be ad- visable."</p>
        <p>The inquest was then begun.—Mrs. Marshall said that deceased was her brother, and lodged with her at DIXOTI- at. He was a earth'. She last saw h'm alive at about eleven o'clock on Sunday night when he went to bed. She was informed of the suicide of her brother by her daughter next morning. The police were informed. Deceased had been drinking very heavily for some time, hut she never thought he would take away his life. He was unmarried. On Sunday he could not hold his hands still. He had beers discharged from work on Friday night, hut he was taken on again on Sunday.—Sarah Alice Buckley, a young girl, said that deceased was her uncle. She last saw him alive on Sunday night. On Monday morning she got up at seven o'clock, and found deceased's dinner in the kitchen. She called for him, but got no answer. She then saw the closet door closed and went to open it, when she saw a man's hand. She then went for the police, who cut deceased down. ()wen Sherida, Said that he lodged with Mrs. Marshall. On Monday morning he got up at 5.20, and deceased was also downstairs. Witness said to him "It's a nice morning "to which deceased replied in a very low tone " It's very fns." Deceased then went out at the back, and witness went out at the front door, anti went to Lymm. Deceased did not appear to be any worse than usual. He had taken more drink than usual the last fortnight. He had never expressed an intentim to take away his life, and witness never expected him to do such Ething.—P.C. Jones said that the witneis Sarah Alice Buckley cane to him about 7.40 a.m. on Monday and told him there was a man in the closet. Witness went there and found eceased hanging by a rope from a beam in the ceiling. His feet touched the flonr, lie was quite dead. Witness cut him down.—The jury returned a verdict, to the effect that deceased had committed suicide whilst in an unsound state of mind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>FINANCH : A DSCREASED RATE.</head>
        <p>FINANCH : A DSCREASED RATE.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee reported that the estimates of expenditure for the current year had been submitted and considered and it was resolved that a general dis- trict rate of Is. 10,1. in the £ tot levied for the year 1893- 1894 payable by two instalments, the first on demand and the second on the 30th September next. Councillor Owen in moving the minutes, said it must be gratifying to the Council to know that notwithstanding the addi- tional expense incurred by the incorporation of the town that the rate was a penny less than under the Local hoard. The borough rate which was included in the poor rate, was a small fraction over 7d. in the £ and this added to the district rate, gave a total rate from the Town Hall of 2s. 51. ; last year the rate was 2s. ett. The estimates ot the various Committees amounted to something over Is. 10d. in the £, and he urged the Committees to take C4ro they were within their estim- ates.—Councillor Elodgkinson seconded the minutes Councillor Shores drew atten inn to the fact that the full estimates had not been submitted to the members of the Committee and Council and it was decided that in the future this should be done.—The minutes passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>TECHNICAL SCHOOL.-INCREASEDBSTIHATE.</head>
        <p>THE TECHNICAL SCHOOL.-INCREASEDBSTIHATE.</p>
        <p>'1 he icinutes of the technical instruction Committee, showed that the total estimated expenditure in the erec- tion of the Technical School ' and Free Library was £10,785 made up as follows :—Builder's tender £8,200, terracotta work £1,300, street making £BOO, taking out quantities £l9O, architect's commission £475. The architects, 14essrs. Woodhouse and Willoughlev, of Manchester, had submitted a BA of deductions from the scheme, which would reduce the cost by £2,676 18s. 6d., but the sub-Committee which had the subject in hand ,sa id they could not be :adopted without entirely destroying the utility of the building as a technical school and the; therefore recommended the Council to carry out the scheme in its entiretv.—Conncillor F. Neil moved the adoption of the minutes, and said the original estimate was £B3OOO but Mr. tlartley whq was called in to advise the Board in the selection of the plans, informed them that the accommodation they required and asked for could not be obtained for the sum of £13,000 and his words had proved to he cptite true. The lowest builder's i tender was over £B,OOO. The Committee had to provide for the future as well as tho present and no less scheme would suffice. The work could he accomplished out of the penny rate, The technical school and library were</p>
        <p>combined and the income from the rd., wits £l,lOB, arid the graht from the County Council £439, total £1,533: —Tim estimated annual expenditure on library rate 3 n.l repayment of principal and interest on loans was £974 leaving a balance of £Bl4 to carry on the school.—Mr. Owen seconded the minutes. After a brief discussion in which no opposition was raised; the minutes passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>PROPOSED C.EMETERY.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED C.EMETERY.</p>
        <p>Cduneglor Hodglcinson moved that the returns as to the number of interments during the year 1890, 1891,and 1892, in the several Burial Grodnds within the Borough of Widnes, and the present available space for interments in such burial ground respectively, laid upon the table at the last meeting of the Council, be referred to a special committee to consist of nine members, who shall inquire and report to the Council as the Local Sanitary Author- ity as to the necessity or otherwise for providing a Public Cemetery for the Borough. Councillor Hodgkinson declared that he was personally in favour of cremation, but public opinion was not ripe for the adoption of such a method of disposing of the dead, but as the returns presented at the last meeting allowed that the present burying space would not last , many years, he thought it was time they set about pro- curing a cemetery. It was the largest town without a cemetery in England, and be pointed out that as time went on land in the district would have an advanced value and they had better move at once.—Alderman Tlmmis said he objected to grave yards in the midst of people on the score of health. Personally he preferred cremation, he was a member of a cremational society, but as a public man he had to recognise the prejudices of the people and he would second the motion so that an Inquiry could be made.—Mr. Shrres opposed the motion on the ground that it was premature. He pointed out that 7 or 8 acres eould be added to St. Bedes ground.— The motion was carried by a large majority and the following committee was eleeted to deal with it A : Merman Tinsmis, Sadler, Williamson, and Councillors Owen, F. Neil, Shores, Quinn and Elodgkinson.</p>
        <p>_ CCUNC/LLOR DULLOUGIt AND TUE COUNCIL. The. Chairman (Alderman Sadler) gave notice that a• the next meeting ha intended to draw attention to a ret markable speech which had been delivered by Councillor Bullough at a recent social gathering and to move the following resolution : " That this Council regards the statements made by Councillor Bullough a breach of the privileges of the Council, and condemns the unwarrant- able assertions cast upon the majority of its members and hereby renews its confidence in the Committees as constituted." This conclue,ed the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>"THAT PUBLIC HALL AGEN ! "</head>
        <p>"THAT PUBLIC HALL AGEN ! "</p>
        <p>Three hundred Peaund fer a Clock ! I Merciful Providence howd me I Never bed aw sitch a shock I 0, that they never hed towd me ! Glorious Commissioners these ! Not that aw mean to revile 'urn ; But, are )0 sure its no risk Hevin. um eaut o'th' Asylum ? Three Hundred Peaund fer a Clock 1! Doesn't it seauud like extortion? Yah, wen its gooin' t' be spent On a greyt Public Abortion 1 Ovver my Ratepayin' chums, Me an' my muse sadly lingers; Coxe aw con see they'll wake up. Only wen brunnin' ther fingers! Three undred Peawnd fer a clock! I Stoat in a top-heyvy turret.; Wile there's smallpox let a shock, An' thay'n no Hospital fer it ! IVeel may a doctor exclaim— Startlet at these mad expenses— Lord ! when Commis,ioners rave, Bring urn soon remind to ther senses!" Three Hundred Peaund fer a Clock ! ! Whisper it low, keep it Gullet; Lest yo be thrill'd wi' a shock, Throo Common Sense runnin' riot. Sewage may stagnate an' stink, Payne may badly need doin'; .But they rnun wait, till tboose chimes Play up "The High Road to Ruin ! " Three Hundred Peaund fer a Clock Wile Trade's but goo&amp; hauf timin'; Why, its gen me sich a shock, That aVV'M compAl'd te be rhymin'. An' aw this brass 'ull be spent— tee the Owl Cheermon's tipeechea— Puttin' a big clock an' chimes Into th' Sucveyor's new breeches Three Hundred Peaund fer a Clock I ! Oh. by the meek holy Moses ! Ratepayers will get a shock, Payin' fer Time trim ther noses I Well lit they'll stond this wild fad, Owd,Nyowton's folk, aw mean, mainly; It serves um jolly wee! reel, An' that's informin' um plainly. Three Hundred Peaund fer a Clock! ! Its made me welly nigh chockin' ; Per it is beaun't be a shock, Beaut the wild skamers are jokin'. Bariesteawn to get a grand clock I Nyowton to pay most o' th' money! Gosh ; i'th' wole cour.,e o' my Aw ne'er know'd owt kauf tz funny! TUM 0' PEGS.</p>
        <p>Rose Bonk, 12, 5, 93.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>REMA.RE 'kBLE CHARGE AGAINST MINERS. "SCUTILING" AT 'rHE BOrrOXI. OF A MINE.</head>
        <p>REMA.RE 'kBLE CHARGE AGAINST MINERS. "SCUTILING" AT 'rHE BOrrOXI. OF A MINE.</p>
        <p>Eleven youths, whose ages varied from thirteen to nineteen, where charged at Salford on Thursday with a contravention of the Cal &amp;times Revd:idea Act in the Clifton and fiersley Colliery. 0-fend:ants, in order to engage in a" pcuttling " affray 500 yards under ground, left the part of the colliery in which" they were engaged, and pulling down fences and other obstacles forced their way to another part of the mine. By interfering with the ventilation they endangered 700 lives. The case was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>THREATENING MR. GLA.DSTONE. PRISONER CONIMICI7ED FOR TRIAL.</head>
        <p>THREATENING MR. GLA.DSTONE. PRISONER CONIMICI7ED FOR TRIAL.</p>
        <p>On Thursday at Row-street Police Court, before John Bridge, William Henry Townsend, t;unmaker's assistant, Sheffield, was charged with firing a revolver in Down- ing-street to the common danger, and also with sending a threatening letter to Mr. Gladstone. In this extraor- dinary communication the prisoner urged the Premier to •• drop that cursed bill "—the Home Rule Bill, and th re itened that if he did not abandon it he would mur- der him. Extracts from the prisoners memorandum book were read. The entries were chiefly to the effect that he had followed Mr. Gladstone to Brighton', and also kept watch near his town house, in the hope of shooting him. Evidence as to the revolver Bring baying been given, the prisoner, who reserved his defence, was com- mitted for trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ALL ABOUT A HALF-PENNY.</head>
        <p>ALL ABOUT A HALF-PENNY.</p>
        <p>THE POWERS OF RAILWAY COMPANIES.</p>
        <p>An interesting case arising out of the revision of rail- way rates was heard at Macchtsfield Police Court, en Thursday. Joseph Hudson, fruiterer, of the town, sued the London and Narth-Western Railway Company for the value of a flat of watercress. On March 17 the watercress was sent to plaintiff from Manchester, but the officials at Miccleslield station would not permit him to take the flat unless he paid 10i. carriage. The charge was 91.; 4j3 tit the Company said they did not take halfpence, and the plaintiff refused to pay 10d. The watercress was taken into the market place and sold to another person. The Company contended that they were entitled, under the revised rates, to charge one penny for the fraction of a penny. His Honour Judge Hughes concurred, and said, with regret, that the plaintiff could not recover. He declines', however, to give the Company costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>_ " SILEN CE IS GOLDEN."</head>
        <p>_ " SILEN CE IS GOLDEN."</p>
        <p>-- There is little doubt that one thing which has tended greatly to disconcert the Opposition's, says the London correspondent of the " Manchester Guardian "the rer- sistent silence of the Ministeridlists. Thelrish Unionists especially have counted on the speeches of the National- -Ist members to provide them with matter for the continuance of the debate' and it was on the assumption that Mr. Sexton, Mr. flealy, and Mr. Dillon, would enter into competition in heated declamation with their oppo- nents that the Unionist predictions as to startling " scenes" in Committee was based. As it happened, the Irish members have resolutely refused to be drawn, in spite of continued taunts from the Unionist benches, and there is no question that their reticence has saved hours of Parliamentary time. It is not so long ago that Colonel Saunderson candidly admitted that lie "could not speak without interruption," and it was only by the heti) of the occ ision ti interruptions from the Irish henehes that he managed to give any life to his speech on Ttiesdar. There is, in fact, no more dreary proceed- ing for an Opposition than a Parliamentary debate in which nine-tenths of the speaking is on their own side, and it is this which is telling upon the Unionists.</p>
