        <p>HaYDOCK "MAY QUEEN- FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>Unlike last year the Committee of the Ilaydock May Queen 1 estival were ou Saturday afternoon favoured with warm genial weather. A stiff breeze Was blowing, which, hut for the forethought of the Uayilock Local Board, would have raised Clouds of dust along the route the procession followed, and would thus have militated against the comfort of those takiug part, and also the large number of aeectators. shortly before the time annowiced for the departure of the procession the water cart was sent out, and the dust was effectually laid along the whole bee of route. Profiiiine also by last year's experirlice the Commiqee hi.,cl ude- great improvements in their arrangements, and consequently they were enabled on Saturday to turn out a show of which they might be preul. The flaydock foetbali field was again pieced at the disposal of the Committee, and here the procession was mardrillel. A start shnuld have been made at a quarter peat two, but some little delay took place, and it was .quite half-an-hour after that time before-a start was made. When all was in order, the procession left the field headed by Mr. Pimblett on hatelsaA, as Marshall, the Haydeck brass band following. Then came the May-pole dancers, a bevy of young lases attired in white, with blue and pink sashes, and caps to match ; coming next was a giant, attired in ancient fighting costume, with brass shield, helmet, breast plates and sword; a lurry followed containing a boat, is which was a young girl represent- ing" Grace Darling"; then came sailors, flower girls, "Robin Ilood,", in the historical green costume, with bow and arrows, Buffalo Bill on horsed) ick ; the seasons, representing Spring, Summer, Autumn and Winter, day and night, bakers,, with white caps, aprons and tins, butchers with steels, the ancient Darby and Juan ; Red Riding Rood, and a number of girls with red cloak and hood, wartherwomen on lurry, with tubes, wringing machine, and clothes pegged on lines,in process of drying. Then came Ally Sloper,with t he bottle of unsweetened peeping from out the folds of his lappet, following him were professional boxers ready for the fray, and to watch that they were not too badly mauled was a company of miniature soldiers, and these pre- ceded the carnage containing last year's Queen, Miss May Greenall, and the first year's Queen, Miss E. Can- liffe. Dressmakers followed, and teachers preceded the Earlestown Brass Band. To teud the players who might perchance receive some injury came a batch of hospital nurses, and behind these it motley crew of ,colliers. On a hurry was Britannia, Miss Taylor, while at each corner of the lurry were gins representing Ireland, Scotland, Wales, and America, 'filen came servants, newsboys, soldiers, akipping rope girls, d Aver girls in conveyance, milkmaids ou fumy, tollowinz the occupation of the dairy, gipsie., sceptre bearer, Mr. P. Haselden ; crown bearer. Mr. Taylor ; and behind these came the Queen, Miss Ada Carter, seated in a carriage drawn hy a pair of horses, and attended by her train-bearers Miss Heaton and Miss Pimblett, and escorted by four members of the Lancashire Inman mounted. is. fitting termination to the procession was a lurry containing a troop of minstrels, wha inade!thiugs lively by their antics, and representing the tail end was a clown seated upon a donkey with his face to its tail. The dresses of the principal characters werrevery pretty and appropriate, and had been hired from a costumier. The pi OCPBBiOII, which numbered about 820 individuals, wended its way to the Owl's Nes,. by way of the toll bar, and back through the village to the Ram's Flrai, returning to the field near the Wagon and Horses Hotel, for the corona- tion ceremony. Suitableprovision had been made this , year to prevent any breaking in by the crowd, and as ! a natural consequence everything passed off plea- santly and successfully. After the queen was ; seated upon her throne, - which was a neatly ; arranged structure, the Maypole dancers opened the ! festivities by plaiting Cl,,' rim:inns. Tile girls who had been trained by Mr. Ralpae, performed their work I remarkably well, not a single hitch eccurring either in the plaiting of the rib:ions, or in the restoration of them to the position they occupied at the commeneement.• Their movements Were g.rareful, and they deserve praise for the excellence of their work. The usual cermony of presenting the crown and sceptre to the queen was then gone through, and the cortniationowt3als,iz the waedotuiontbayl Miss Orinak6 Dreytoti, the band Anthem. The queen who was a! tired In a heliotrope stuff dress. with sel,et train of the same colour, with wreath and fall appeared very nervials under the weight of honours, but on the whole the little maiden beim her- , eels well. she was attended by her maids of honour, Misses Dearden, Davies, //Antler and Atkinson, and two boys attired as hussar officas, as a body guard. After the prese n tat ai of fler Map-sty's.loyal SubjeCt, an en- tertainment the Uphill Jack minstrels and the ion with the festivities were rpirnemeecendti n was givene onnhpcy t brought to h close Previous to the priceelion being formed, an inspection of the turn-mita was made, Mr. J. Middlehurst officiating RS an d. e, and he awarded the positions As follows :—Mr. J. Siintu (Messrs. R. Evans and Co.) first prize 355., Mr. Joe. Carter (Messrs. R. Evans and Co.) second 254., Mr. Jne. Highem, grocer. third 155., Jno. Cawley. farmer, fourth 12s. .6,1 , W. Ralharry, farmer. fifth 7s. 6 1 , Mr. FL Griffin, sixth,. 7s 61 The whole preeeeeiegs were carried out with great success, by a large committee, of which Mr. H. Griffin was chairman and treasurer, and Mr. U. Were- ley secretary. after.the queen had vacated the throne, the spectators were allowed within the enclosure, and dancing was indulged in until dusk.</p>
