      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>-EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE -EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATMADAY, MAY 13, 1893.</p>
        <p>THE progress of the Home Rule tliPeositinn 'Tatum ' Bill has been accompanied by some remarkable changes of front on the :aotv, 0,,f the Opposition forces. We all remember ' usfore the meeting of Parliament, the Gov- ti-"lent were continually being invited—some- the lan&lt;mage of passionate denunciation at other times in that of almost pathetic "Ift,restY—to reveal all the details of the Bill. was "en Parliament met, and when Mr. GLADSTONE scb teady and eager to divulge the whole of the :kle) a strange reluctance to enter upon .tbe tt seemed to seize upon the valiant politicians day side of the House, and the event- tedi ‘J was postponed as long as possible by "3os and obstructive talk. The Bill was at vaari'rrith laid before Parliament and the country, in nts fulness, and then wd were told that it was so 7ti/oIY had that, after its full meaning had been allAsined, it could not survive the condemnation 4ad ridicule of the country, and that it would Proceed to a second reading. Yet, the c°lod reading has come and gone and the Gov- majority shows not the slightest sign of we:ln:less or diminution. What signs of weak- for ate to be seen are all on the other side, sur Government has, as a rule, been by more than its normal majority. *an that the is in Committee we have the _ ,08", Peetacle of politicians who declare it to not "oPelessly futile and irrational that it can - 7ven be mended, proposing "amendments "in "4"*.rs sufficient to keep up the discussion till ,u,Ufristinas if vigorous measures be not taken to ()ree the pace And all this, too, in face of thell°3itive assurances from the same quarters that u °llBB of Lords will throw the Bill out, and that the Unionist party will triumphantly ride in- • Nwer when an appeal is made to the cceintry however the decision of the Peers. There are, two reasons which quite adequately ex- ', , Plain the self-contradiction of the Opposition, and ;111:1 glaring contrasts between their professions and 'the actions. In the first place, notwithstanding le Vary confident predictions in which they are wont to indulge, they do not at all feel certain that " aPpeal to the country will result favourably to themselves. They have already seen so many of their prophecies with regard to the Home Rule . Rill falsified, that they are afraid a dissolution n2lght result in the return to power of the Liberal Government with a mandato from the e°Ple which the hereditary House will not dare to question. And, then, in the second place, the G°vornrnent's programme includes many measures of socizl and political reform,besides that of Horne 11111 e. To several of these the Tories are as reso- lutely, and perhaps even more resolutely, opposed than they are to any proposal for the better government of Ireland, though 'they may think ie , priideut to dissemble their hostility. They know that in obstructing the Home Rule Bill they are</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>also delaying the passage of these other measures, and this alone in their eyes will be a sufficient reason for the unscrupulous tactics they are pur-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>suing.</head>
        <p>suing.</p>
        <p>THERE is .at the present time an The Afro-American. interesting visitor to this country in the person of Miss IDA BELL WELLS, an American negro lady. Miss WELns is described as a quiet, soft-voiced, pleasant young lady, and the object of her visit to England is to explain the operations of " Lynch law" in the Southern States and to plead the cause of Afro- Americans generally. The United States are often spoken of as the land of freedom and equality, but in some of the States the negro,and those who have negro blood in their veins, enjoy very little of either. The lynching of " black men "is becoming a painfully popular pastime, so popular indeed that unless the doing to death of the unfortunate victim is accompanied by circum- stances of hideous ferocity very little publicity is given to it. Miss WELL'S own experiences speak eloquently of the persecution to which her race is subjected. As an editor she has seen her news- paper summarily suppressed, and the contents of her office destroyed, because she has ventured to protest against the too frequent lynchings in the South. Fortunately she was herself at the time in New York, or she might have fallen another victim to race prejudice, but as it is a prominent citizen of Memphis has registered the amiable vow that he will shoot her " on sight " should she appear again in Memphis within the next 20 years. And all this because she had exercised in a perfectly legitimate way that right of free speech which is so often, among the white people them- selves, allowed to run to license in America. Al- though a lady of education and refinement, she has been dragged from a railway car and left be- hind at a station because she refused to go into what is contemptuously termed the "Jim Crow " car, in which coloured people are expected to herd together. For this gross breach of contract on the part of the railway officials she was awarded five hundred dollars by a State Circuit Court, Uut the verdict was set aside by the Supreme Court of Tennessee on one of those quib- bles which expert lawyers are never at a loss to find. The negroes are not without sympathisers in the Northern States, but the sympathy is not of an critspoken or effective kind. Only one of the religious denominations—that of the Metho- dist Episcepal Church—has even ventured se far as to condemn lynching. Under these circum- stances the hopes of the nogroes have been turned towards England. and it is satisfactory to note that-Miss WELLS is pleased with the reception she has met with in the largo English towns she has visited. Toe politicians of the United states are never slow to protest against what may seem wroag in their eyes in the "effete despotisms" of Europe ; it is surely, therefore, not out of place for Euro- peans, on their sil;e, to condemn the legal and illegal infamies under which the Degrees of the Southern States stiffer. •</p>
        <p>THE weather is, to say the least, The Weather. getting interesting. A continua- . . .</p>
        <p>tion of the drought threatens to bring about some rather serious results. At the meeting of the Manchester City Council on Wed- nesday,Sut JOHN HARWOOD gave a timely warning of the falling oil of the water supply of that city,and a caution to be careful in the use of the fluid which might soon become precious. In many country districts which are dependent for domestic sup- plies upon wells and pumps the lack of water is already beginning to be inconveniently felt. Most of us are consoling ourselves with the assurance that the drought cannot lest much longer, but that is a presumption which is not supported by the interesting weather notes contributed to our columns this week by MT. ROBERT DAVIES. On the other hand, it will be seen that Mr. DAVIES'S notes, which relate to the dry year of 1868, do not countenance the presumption that because we have a droughty spring and summer there will be a wet harvest and autumn. Mr. DAVIES'S notes cover the period from February till November, and they show that in 1868 there was an exceptionally fine warm spring,and that there was little rain until well into August. On the 29th of February the goose- berries and white rose trees were bursting their buds; on the 31st May haycutting commenced; and on the 4th July there were" prayers every- where for rain," and the farmers were cutting their beans and oats green for their cattle Even a slight thundershower on St. Swithin's Day, the 15th July, did not break the spell, for on the 2nd of August it is recorded that the weather was " better than ever." A repetition of the experi- ences of 1868 is certainly not to be desired, but yet at the same time it would in many respects be preferable to the deluges of rain with which some prOphetg threaten us.</p>
        <p>IN charging the Grand Jury at the Child Insurance. Liverpool Assizes this week, Mr. Jvs- TICS Wm's spoke out strongly against the evils of infant insurance. That insurance of the life cot infants is, in some cases, really an insnr- ance of death is a conclusion to which he is more and more forced the longer he sits on the bench. He admits that by far the greater number of poor people who insure the lives of their children have no sinister designs in view, but that there is an appreciable number of cases in which insurance has a bad effect, is, he asserts, not doubted by any judge who has had a long experience in the crith- inal. court. This is certainly a weighty and out- spoken indictment of the practice of infant insurance, and it is an indictment which will, we believe, be supported by many who have a special knowledge of the origin and rhotives of crimes against children. Whilst, however, fully admitting that insurance money which depends upon the frail life of a child is, in the nature of things, a grave temptation to the brutal and vicious and even to the severely proverty-stricken, it should not be forgotton that there is much to be said on the other side. Of all people the poor are, of course, the least able to meet out of their ordinary resources the ex- penses which the death and burial of even a child entails. That is a view of the question which will, and which ought to be, duly enforced if over the. subject is brought forward for legislative treat- ment. In the meantime the judges themselves may do something towards eradicating the evil they complain of by visiting with condign punish- ment all such offences as those referred to against children.</p>
        <p>DIE -opening of the Imperial Insti- Tbo imperial Institute. tute, which took place on Wednes- day,, is, by common consent, des- cribed as an "imposing spectacle." It was fa- voured by two cinumstances highly cOnducive to spectacular display, namely, the presence of the QUEBN, and the pretralence of " Queen's weather." Then there were present even more than the</p>
      </div>
