      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 13 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 13 1893.</p>
        <p>usual elements of a State pageant. There were dusky and turbaned warriors from India, black sailors from the West Indies,uniforrned volunteers from Australia and Canada, besides the numerous contingents of horse and foot guards and the naval detachments. The Imperial Institute has been founded to commemorate the QUEEN'S Jubilee. Such a method of commemorating the Jubilee was suggested to the PRINCE of WALES by the success of the Colonial and Indian Exhibi- tion of 1880, and he wrote to the then Loan MAYOR of LONDON suggesting as a suitable memo- rial, " An Institute which should represent the arts, manufactures, and commerce of the Empire." He considered that the Institute would be a means of "drawing closer the bonds which unite the Empire," and he defined it as at once a museum, an exhibiticn, and the proper locality for the discussion af Colonial and Indian subjects. Now that it is completed, it is said to be all that and more. It is a club, a library, a school of modern languages, a technical education centre, and a commercial intelligence department.</p>
        <p>•SPECIAL OFFER. 12,000 A. LUSK AND CO.'S is. TINS AT 70. PElt TIN. EXTRA CALITY APRICOTS, 14,500 SPECIAL QUALITY 9,1. TINS STEAK SALMON AT 7d. PER TIN. HENRY MILLING &amp;.,.CO, LTD.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>lALLICORD SAVAGE HOW= BY A MOTHER AND SON AT WIDNES. At Widnes Police Court on Monday. before Mr. George Pilkington, Catherine Burns, 34, Major Cross-st., and her son Patrick Burns, were brought up charged together with unlawfully wounding a labourer named James Burke, residing at 49, Alforde-street, on Saturday night. The evidence given was to the effect that at about a quarter-past eleven Burke met one of the female prisoner's eons, who was drunk. Burke took him home, and Mrs. Burke came to the doer. When she , saw her eon's condition, she accused Burke of making him drunk, at the same time striking him in the face' with her clenched fist. Mee. Burns then went into the I house, and returned with a rollingloin, with which she struck him on the head,inflicting a serious wound. The male prisoner followed his mother out, took off the belt he was wearing, and beat the prosecutor unmercifully on the head with it.—Dr. O'Keeffe said he did not con- sider the man out of danger yet.—The magistrates re- manded the prisoners for seven days. WESLEYAN DAY SCHOOL, Wr.srox. A very successful entertainment in aid of the funds of the above school was given by the children to a large and appreciative audience. The chair was occupied by Mr. W. R. Harp, C.C., under whose direction along and varied programme was gone through. The items con- sisted of recitations, dialogues, musical drill, May-pole dance, and waxworks, all of which were well rendered by the children. Two pianoforte solos were also ad- mirably performed by Miss Elibbert of Runcorn. Mr. Earp showed his sympathy with the object in view, and materially increased the receipts by giving a donation ot LS. A vote of thanks was very heartily accorded to him, proposed by the Rev. Wm. Arrowsmith, seconded by Mr. B. Crosby, and supported by Mr. Epps, the headmaster, who also returned thanks to other donors, amongst whom was Mr. T. T. Still, the popular manager of the Weston Chemical Works. JOSEPH PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller. 51, High- it., Runcorn.—Advt.</p>
        <p>_ PRESENTATION TO THE Ray. W. STANTON JONES, WIDNES. On Thursday evening, the quarterly social meeting in connection with St. Mary's Young Men's Bible Class was held in Waterloo-road Schools. The proceedings were commenced with an excellent " spread," to which about 120 sat down. After the tables were cleared, an adjournment was made to the boy's school, where a capital entertainment was given by Miss Lamb, Messrs. F. Parr, A. Kenyon, T. Davies, J. Paton, R. Goddart, Fox, T. Risley, J. Nixon. Mr. A. Bootwick presided. Daring an interval in the programme, the Chairman called upon Mr. A. G. Lowry to present a beautiful travelling companion and a purse of gold to the Rev. W. Stanton Jones, the beloved leader of the class, as a token of their regard for him' and deep regret at his removal from the parish. Mr. Lowry and the Chairman spoke of the splendid work done by Mr. Jones since he came to the parish, and characterized his departure as an almost irreparable loss to the church in Widnes. The Rev. Mr. Jones, who was much affected, thanked them all for the kindness which had always been extended to him since he came amongst them. CLAIM FOR ALLEGED DEBTS OF OLD STANDING.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn County Court, on Tuesday, before ' his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, a claim for the recovery of £1 54. was made by Peter Smith, chemist, Bridge-st., Runcorn, against Henry Whitlow, Astinore, farmer, formerly butcher in Clturch-st., for an advertisement in his directory for 1889. Claimant stated that he received the order in December, 1888, on condition that the sum was taken out in meat.—Defendant denied giving the order, and stated that he had never been asked for the amount previous to receiving the sumnsons.—Mr. Smith aho claimed £1 Bs. from the executors of the late Wm. Fitton, of Weston, for petroleum oil supplied in Sep- tember, 1888. Mr. Ashton represented defendant, and it was stated that no previous notice of the debt had been given. Plaintiff being unable to prove delivery a non-suit was entered.--The same claimant also brought an action against Thames Jeffs, provision dealer, Widnes, formerly of Weston Point, for 7. 2d. for goods supplied. —Defendant denied having received the articles, and stated that he was nearly 70 years of age, and that was the first time he had set foot in the County Court.—A verdict for plaintiff for 4s. 10d. was given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>GRAND JURORS.</head>
        <p>GRAND JURORS.</p>
        <p>Amongst those who were sworn on the Grand Jury at Liverpool ASS:V2S, this week, were Mr. John Dun, of 'Warrington ; Mr. N. E. MeCorquodale, of Newton-le-Willows ; and Mr. W. Lee Pilltington, of Huytoia Grange. TILE 1.1014 D IRON TRADE. _ . ,</p>
        <p>With the exception oi steel, business in all the branches of the English iron market remains un- altered. The favourable nature of the Board of Trade Returns as regards the exports of iron and steel shOuld, however, have a good effect. Pig- iron is quiet ; but in manufactured iron a little more is doing in one or two districts. Tinplates continue to manifest fair activity. A brisker tone is noticeable in steel. Shipbuilding and engineer- ing are still in a languid condition. The coal trade' is slack.—lron. Au. HOUSTMOLD LINF ?f, hemmed and eiarkea. free of charge.--Land W. Dur Tom. Sankey-st.—Adut. FATAL ACCIDENT ON THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held on Saturday morning, before Mr. B. C. Yates, at the Latchford Ship Canal Hospi- tal. on the body of John Price (18), a native of Bir- ininghem, and a fireman, who was employed on the works at Weston Point. It appeared from the evidence which was given that on Thursday after- noon last the deceased slipped from the engine Kenyon whilst uncoupling a waggon Both his legs were alniost severed, and Price died on his arrival at the hospital, to which institution he had , been conveyed without delay.—The jury returned ' a verdict of accidontal death. SEE any NEW STOCK of Dress Materials, Dress Prints, Detainee. is., EVANS, 17, Sankey-it., Warrington.--4drt.</p>
        <p>THE LIBERAL PARTY AND POLICE ENFRANCHISE- MENT. _ _</p>
        <p>One bit of good work has been done this session. On Tuesday the House of Lords read for the third time the Bill to remove the disabilities of the police, which has been introduced by Mr. C. E. Schvvann, J.P., for North Manchester. This Bill—or Act, as we may now term it—enables the police to exercise the franchise in Municipal, School Board, and other local elections in cases in which they possess the qualifications which are necessary to enable any , other citizen to vote. It is a matter of gratification that! a highly respectable and deserving body of public servants are admitted to the full rights at- taching to British citizenship. It is. too, in accord- ance with the fitness of things that this right should have been secured to the police by the Liberal party. Under the Bill—or Act as we may now call it--policemen are prohibited from formal canvassing, a restriction for the possession of which they are to be envied. There was, at first, some Opposition to Mr. Schwann's efforts after en- franchisement for the police, but when it appeared that, alike in England, Scotland, and Wales, they were earnestly desirous of possessing political rights the opposition soon ceased. We congratu- lhte the police on being admitted to the franchise and felicitate the h member for North Manches- ter on the legislative success lie has attained 'not- withstanding the champion obstructionism which has prevailed in the House of Commons. Tea CORSETS. UMDRELLLS, BLOUSES. UNDRECLOTIIING, go to EVANS., 17, Sankey-St., Warrington.—Advt. JUVENILE GOOD TEMPLARS' QUARTERLY COUNCIL</p>
        <p>EETIN G.</p>
        <p>The quarterly session of the South West Lanca- shire District Juvenile Council, was held in the Mission Room, Bootle, on Saturday afternoon, when a good muster of representatives were present from various parts of the district, over whom Sister Duncan, D.15.J.1`., presided. After the opening, the report of the D.S.J.T. was received. It shovved a very tic:aye quarter's work, the 'temples generally being in an uctiveistate. A new temple had been ,opened at Leighr.vvith very .good'' provects of suc- cess. The total, membership was 3,384 in 40 tam- pies, besides a, large number of adults. The report also referred to the National Challenge Shield won for the seeond time by this district Or the largest Increase in membership, and it was urged that every effort should be made to retain it, by work- ing for an additional increase to the end of the pre- Sent year. This year Juvenile Templary will attain its majority in England, as the lontpelier Temple, No. I, was opened 21 years ago at Southport, and was still carrying on a splendid woe... Several good suggestions for the extension of the order were mentioned, and after discussion the report was unanimously adopted. Bro. Newell, P.D.S.J.T., submitted a scheme for the celebration of the 21st anniversary, which was very favourably received, and referred to the Executive Committee to further the arrangements .in conjunction with the adult order. Hster Mrs. Duncan, D.S.J.T., of nothesay,' Isle cif Bute, was present as a visitor, and gave a short address. Thanks were tendered to her, and greetings ordered to be sent to the Scottish brethren. The next Council was fixed for Southport on Satur- day, July 29th. Thanks to the Bootle friends closed the Council, and afterwards tea ws partaken of. A successful public meeting was afterwards held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>TIIE HULL STRIKE.</head>
        <p>TIIE HULL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER FIRE. Another fire broke out at the Efull timber yards on Tuesday morning. The flames were first noticed among the wood stacks lying between Sir Andrew's Fish Dock and Sir William Wright Dock, and they sprelid with such rapidity that the pre- mises of the Humber Shipwright and Boat .fluilding Company were soon gutted. The Strike Committee met again on Thursday and considered proposals that may lead to the settlement of the dispute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AI RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AI RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>The demonstration of Widnes Trades Council was a gratifying success, especially considering that it was their first venture of this description. The procession was most orderly, and generally well marshalled, reflecting credit on all concerned. The banners and various implements used in the various trades were viewed with interest. If the demon- stration leads to an increilse in the membership of the Council by the inclusion of Trades Unions at present holding aloof, it will have done good ser- vice. I( working men with to preserve their rights and their independence they must be united ; but, surely, Trades Unionistn does not need preaching in these (keys. As was observed by more than one speaker on Saturday, workingmen had had to secure their own redemption as far as it had gone, and if they are to attain that regenerate state when all inequalities are swept away, it will have to be by their own efforts. A completely representative Trades Council would be a power in Runcorn and Widnes.</p>
        <p>There were several important subjects before Wik;nes Town Council, on Tuesday last, and they were all dealt with, with a minimum of friction. The members quietly acquiesced in the increase of the estimate for the erection of the Technical school and Free Library from £6OOO to over £lO,OOO. It was felt that if the requirements of the case could not be met for a lesb amount, there was no help for it. They exhibited the laudable spirit of not spoiling the ship for the proverbial ha'porth .of paint. The future as well as the present had to be considered.</p>
        <p>From the expressions of opinion by the mera tiers Alderman Timmins would have a good chance of increasing the membership of the crematoria' society with which he is connected if he would undertake a canvas. Burning v. Burying seemed to be much, in favour, but it is not for poorer mor- tals to indulge in the expensive luxury of crema- tion, and they will perforce have to rot in mother earth as their ancestors have done for centuries. Therefore, it would be well if the committee, Coun- cillor Hodgkinson succeeded in forming, would seriously consider the question of providing a cemetery. The amount of space there is available for graves seems to be somewhat problematical,and is there xot another question, whether some of the ground now being used is fit for the purpose?</p>
        <p>Incorporation is an economy and not an expense. Is not that welcome news for the Widnes rate- payers? With the somewhat heavy charge for incorporation included, and that expense will, of course, not occur another year. The rate required for the Town Hall is 2s. sd. as compared with 2e. 61. under the old Local Board. There is no doubt that the Council are asking the ratepayers for more money than did the Local Boar', but the incidence of taxation is altered and the 2s. sd. rate brings in more than did the 2s. fid. rate.</p>
        <p>Lively doings may be expected when Alderman Sadler brings forward his motion at the next Council. Councillor Bullough's speech may with justice be characterised as remarkable, but perhaps it was all a joke, for who expects anything serious after a champagne football dinner ? However there will be an opportunity for Councillor Bel- lough to explain or to further unburden his soul as to the trades, profession, and capabilities of his fellow councillors.</p>
        <p>The Widnes Church Council have passed a resolu- tion requesting the Schdol Board to elect the new vicar, the Rev. W. Hodgson in succession to the Rev. G: H. Garrett, who has resigned consequent upon his removal from Widnes. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>SHOCkING DEATH OF A WIDNES CLERK'.</head>
        <p>SHOCkING DEATH OF A WIDNES CLERK'.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held on Thursday afternoon before Mr. T. E. Sampson,Coraher of Liverpool,on the body of Wm. Jones, 36, a clerk, who lived At 1, Eleanor. et.; Widnes. On Thursday last some wagons were peing shunted at the works of the Linked Alkali Com- pany at Widnes. From the up line two sets of lines ' branch oft to the works, one for empty and one for full wagons. The lines cross, and at the time in question some wagons were standing on the "empty" line covering the part the moving wagons had to pass over. • The driver of the engine drawing the latter moved his engine so as to roll the standing wagons back a lew feet. There was a gap of a yard or two in the row standing, and it seemed that deceased, thinking the engine was on ths "full " line, stepped into the ,:ap to cross to the other side, in order to get the numbers of the wagons. lie was caught between two of the trucks. By Cis own desire he was taken to the Royal Infirmary, and admitted suffering from a severe injury to the elbow. On Monday, Dr. Richmond stated, the arm was successfully ampu- tated, but deceased had been also much bruised.and this led to blood poisoning, from which he died The jury found that death was due to injuries accidentally re- ceived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MISS CROSBY AND MR.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MISS CROSBY AND MR.</p>
        <p>SALICELD AT RUNCORN. In the presence of a large assemblage at Runcorn Parish Church on 'Tuesday morning, Miss Jessie Tinkler Crosby, eldest daughter of Mr. Samuel Crosby, of 40, Leinster Gardens, was united in the holy bonds of matrimony to Mr. Arthur Salkeld, of Pool Dale, Death-road, son of Mr. Wen. F'. Saikeld. The contracting parties are members of well known Runcorn families and great interest was taken in the event. The weather was gloriously fine and there was nothing to mar the brightness of the scene presented in the old Parish Church. The bride, who was given away by her father, was attired in a grey bengaline poplin, trimmed with white silk and adorning her had was a white hat trimmed with ostrich feathers. She wore a diamond brooch, the gift of the bridegroom, and car- ried a bouquet of lilies of the valley and orange blossoms also the gift ef that gentleman. The bride was attended by her sister Miss Edith Crosby, who wore a dress of I I grey crepon, trimmed with pink shot silk and a white , hat trimmed with white ostrich feathers ;and two young girls Miss Lilian Salkeld,daughterof the bridegroom and I miss Nora Lea, niece of the bridegroom. The two latter , I were dressed in cream cashmere dresses and large white I picture bats. The first bridesmaid carried a basket of Itea roses, and the others baskets of selected flowers. Each bridesmaid wore a gold brooch, bearing the mono- gram of the bride and bridegroom and the year, the gift of the bridegroom. Mr. Joseph A. Salkeld occu- pied the position of groomsman. The guests consisted 1 of Mr. and Mrs. Samuel Crosby, Mr. W. F. Salkeld, Mr. I and Mrs. Harry Crosby, Mr. James Crosby, Mr. aud I Mrs. Leonard Crosby, Mr. Wm. Crosby, -Mr. Arthur I Crosby, Mr. and Mrs. W. E. Lea, Mrs. and the Misses , Johnson (Southport), Mrs. Joule, Miss Statham, and I Miss Flindley. The Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, vicar, performed the marriage service, and after the 1.1111.1711 formularies had been gone through in the vestry, Mr. J. Johnson pealed forth the "Wedding March" on the organ as the happy couple and their attendants left the ehurcn. There was the customary greeting of rice at the entrance to the sacred edifice, as an outward ex- pression of good Wishes. The bridal party and guests were driven to the residence of the brute's fattier, where breakfast was served, and during the aftertioon,Mr. and Mrs. Salkeld left for London, es route for the Isle of Wight, where the honeymoon will be spent. Mrs. Sal. keld'a travelling dress was a grey cheek •trinimed with black velvet and she wore a hat to match. The pre- sents were of a handsome and costly character and numbered about CeVehty. The coaches were supplied from the establishment of Messrs. F. and W. Yarwood. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>"JOHN. BULL."</head>
        <p>THE "JOHN. BULL."</p>
        <p>A striking example of that durability which has ren- dered British manufactures so famous in every clime is seen in the" John B,u11," the oldest locomotive in exist- ence in the United States, which has just steamed at a fair rate from Jersey City to Chicago, hauling two equally antiquated passenger-carriages. Do4bt had been manifested as to the ability of the engine to main- tain the time which had been scheduled for it - while some even questioned whether the train would 14 able reach its destination, the World's Fair, of its own accord. But all qualms have been fully set at rest, for the old English locomotive not only kept time, but it could have been run at a considerably greater speed notwithstanding a heavy and constant rain. Indeed, in the words of the driver, "It responded with wonderful alacrity to every demand made Naturally the engine was furbished up a little before being sent out into ths.cold world once more, but it is practically the same "John Bull" which left the shops of George and Robertil Stephenson at Newcastle-on-Tyne and arrived in Phila- delphia in August, 1831. The circumstances of the im- portation of the locomotive into the United States are interesting. A Mr. Robert L. Stevens, was, about the same period mentioned, president and chief engineer of the Camden and Amboy Railroad. In 1830 he visited this country, as did many more, to learn as much as passible concerning railway matters, and here he wit- nessed the trial 01 Stephenson's " Planet," the success of which so pleased him that he immediately ordered from the makers a similar engine for his line. That locomo- tive was the "John Bull," which is now the property of the United States Government, and considered one of the greatest treasures of the Washington National-Museum colleCtion.—Trant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>WIDNES LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. At Widnes Liberal Club on Thursday, at a meeting under the presidency of Alderman Wareing,the following officers of the Widnes District Council were elected : Chairman, Mr. E. Carey ; vice-chairman, Alderman Wareing, and hon. sec., Alderman Timmins. Mr. H. Wade Deacon was appointed vice president of the Association.</p>
        <p>UNITED METHODIST FREE CHURCH, WIDNES. On Sundv afternoon the choir of the Seamen's In- stitute, Runcorn, rendered the service of song " Temp- est Tost " to a large audience. The Rev. J. Martin presided and Mr. E. Eihrell, of Runcorn read the nar- rative. Mr. D. Price was the conductor. The service was highly appreciated. MILTON CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH; WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The anniversary was celebrated on Sunday when the Rev. J. Simpson, ot Runcorn, occupied the pulpit in the morning, and in the evening theeltoir and &amp;choir rs gave the sacred cantata "Under the Palms." Mr. R0a,.;11 was the conductor and Mr. J. 'l'. Jones was the accom pa nist. Mr. D. Lewis presided. In the afternoon Mr. U. Rae delivered an address to the scholars. There wcre good congregations. Go to PRITCTIARD's, 51. High-street. Rune4rn, or the best FIVE GUINEA WATCIT a workingtnau can have.—Advt.</p>
        <p>CONCEALMENT OF BIILTH AT WiDs At Liverpool ASSiV,S on Wednesday, Mary Sweeney, (37), domestic servant, pleaded guilty to having endea- voured to conceal the birth of her child, at Widnes on the 22nd 01 March. Mr. Collingwood [lope, who prtise- cuted, said that the medical evidence went to show that the child had had a separate existence.—The judge said this was a serious case, inasmuch aa there was a strong suspicion that the child met its death by actual violence Prisoner must go to gaol for twelve months with bard labour. PRITCHARD'S have now stood the test for fifty years, and quality aad tirot-class workmanship are still guaranteed. Adet.</p>
        <p>MILL BROW M'ESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN. The anniversary services were held JR Sunday, in hen large congregations assembled both morning and even- ing in Balton-road Chapel. The Rev. T. Hackett, of Chesterfield, formerly of Runcorn, was the preacher. The choir and scholars sang specially selected music, Miss Clara Taylor kindly officiating as leader. Mr. G. S. Frith was at the organ. The collections realised £26 103. It was announced that the number of scholars on the books is 340, and 20 teachers. 'there were 268 members of the Band of Hope. - -</p>
