        <p>DIE RUNCORN KICKING CASE.</p>
        <p>On Monday at Runcorn Petty Sessions, before Messrs. J. Brundrit and P. Speakman, a labourer, named James Finegan was brought up on remand charged with unlawfully wounding another mar. nam,d Frank Lydon on the 29th ult. by kicking him .-uid breaking his jaw on the Runcorn fair ground. The prosecutor is still an inmate of the Royal Infn miry, Liverpool, and the pris- oner was again remanded for seven days. On the appli- cation of Mr. J. Burton, who defended, the bench granted bail, prisoner in £4O and two sureties of £2O each.</p>
        <p>JOSEPH PIitTCAARD, 51, Iligh-strr.k, Runcorn, keeps SPECTACLES LO suit all sights, from a shilling a air.—Advt.</p>
        <p>TRE GAMING NUISANCE AT RUNCORN. At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, John Ryan and Joseph fk or h, I ahourera, were summoned for gaming %ad, h cards, on Smiday, April 30th. P.C.'s Juett and Gregory proved the case. They found the defendants and others playing cards in Peniteth-lane, at 3.30. They had a gallon bottle containing beer.—Defendants denied the charge, but Booth acknowledged having a drink from the bottle. Fines of 53. and costs, N. 6d. each were im- posed.—George Purnival, Stringer's-court, Runcorn, labourer, was summoned for a like offence on the same date. The officers already named saw the defendant on the Stone Hills with a nuinher of other men, playing cards. He was fined ss. and 93. 6d. costs, or seven days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RIINCORt7 BURIAL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RIINCORt7 BURIAL BOARD.</p>
        <p>_ The Rev. D. Gastaldi presided over the monthly meeting of this Board at the cemetery on Wednesday. ?resent Messrs. W. Whittaker, E. Imison, and W. F. Salkeld (secretary). The report showed that during the month ended April 25 there were 30 deaths against 49 in the corresponding period last year. The fees received during the same period amounted to £3B 12s 81., last yea! £53 9s Bd., decrease £l4. 17s. On the recommenda- tion of the Finance Chromirtee it was resolved to pay accounts amounting to £25 15s. 21. The tender of Mr. Thos. Kirkham, of Ureenway-road, for painting the outside of the buildings and the railings of the cemetery was accepted. The amount of the tender was £32 12s. —lt was resolved to it;sue a precept On the overseers for £6O payable in June on sinking fund account. When In want of a Crusrs ant ELIWAivr WEIiDII6 or BIRTUDIT PRESENT go to Fitircuann's, 51, liigh-at.—Alvo. •</p>
        <p>ettUNLTY TO A. PONY AT PIINCOUN. 'Alfred Cross of Leftwich Green, Northwich,salt dealer, was summoned for cruelty to a horse,by allowing it to be worked whilst in an unfit condition,at Runcorn, on April 21st. Inspector Dowty said on the date named he was in Lord-street, Runcorn, when he saw a boy in charge of a grey pony, attached to a salt cart. It was in a poor and. weak condition, and very ,lame. It was suffering from spavin. The pony was subsequently taken, to a veterinary surgeon, who told him it was unfit for work. The boy was not summoned on account of his youth. P.C. Lee described the animal as in an emaciated con- dßion. Defendant explained that he bad no control over the pony. He and his brother-in-law ware engaged iii thesalt trade, but he simply obtained orders. leaving them to be despatched by those at home. The load was a very light one. He had already lost £5 I.os. on•the animal.—He was ordered to pay 10s. and costs.</p>
        <p>_ PRIVATE STREET MAILING AT _ RONCORN.-TEE COURT</p>
        <p>OF AFPEAL. On Monday morning, at Runcorn Petty Sessions. Mr. Lake (law clerk to the Imnrovement Commissioners) appiied to the magistrates (Messrs. Brundrit, Speakman and Rigby), under the Private Streets Works Act, 1892, to appoint a time for determining the matter of objec- tions with regard to the makinc, of certain streets. He pointed out, what had previcaly been the custom, and the change effected by the Measure of last year, which showed that the urban Authority at any time after ex- piration of the month during which the plans, specific- ations, &amp;c., had been open for inspection, might apply to a Court of Summary Jurisdiction, to appoint a time , for determining the matter of all objections, and should publish a notice of the time and place appointed, when the Court might proceed to hear .and determine the matter of all such objections, which must be raised on me of six grounds. They had received some objections, and he simply asked their worships to fix a date for hearing them—The Bench said the Special Court would be held on Jane sth.</p>
      </div>
