      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SHIP CANAL. ALONG THE SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL. ALONG THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>FROM. TIIELWALL TO WALTON ARCHES. One day this week one of our reporters journeyed to Thelwall with the intention of giving the readers of the " Examiner" a short account ot the progress made on the Ship Canal from that village to Arpley. Making the locks at the " ancient city of Thelwall " a starting point, one is at once struck with tho few wcrkmen to be seen about, a fact which shows that the work as far as possible is almost completed. A great deal, of course, still remains to be done, but it cannot be touched until the old railways are in the hands of contractors. The Latchford locks are entirely finished, and a splendid piece of work they look. The only man seen on them was a mason who was putting on the finish- ing touches. Right opposite the locks, the banks of the canal are being straightened and considerably strength- ened by lining the side with pieces of rock. Coming nearer Latchford, and pa.ssing the pumping engines which are still hard at work, the next noticeable , feature in Connection with the canal is the hydraulic pressure house. On entering the build- ing, the two engines which are to be used for turning the swing bridges by hyraulic power, present a finished appearsuct There were only three : men on the premises, and these were cleaning and brightening the engines. The only thing now wanting is the water, and half-a-dozan navvies were to be seen preparing the way near to, by making a passage trom the canal for the admission of the suction pines. The hydraulie pressure work has been in the hands of the celebrated engineers, Sir William Armstrong, of New- castle-on-Tyne. Looking dawn into the cutting, a few men were engaged in fixing water pipes at the bottom, and some masons were also busy "chipping away," at the opposite side of the canal. Passing the Latchford Hospita4 and the watchman with hia wooden leg at the gates, you come to the Splen- did railway bridge, which spans the canal, but see only, as yet, luggage trains running thereon. Your progress of walking along the canal cutting is now im- peded, as is also the work of the contractor you see a steam navvy or two standing idle, and most likely they will have to stand idle until August, which is th full time given for running luggage trains only, on the new line. Unlike the Cheshire Lines Committee, who have greatly facilitated the work of the contractors by giving up their old lines,the London and North-Western Railway Company, seem to be less complaisant, and will no doubt hold sway on their old lines until the end of their prescribed period of six month's running of goods traffic, which commenced on March Ist. Re- commencing the jaurney on the canal just beyond Latchford station, you coma to the cantilever bridge, which is to be the highway over the canal to Latchford Station, and also leads into Grappenhall-road and Wash-lane. The ironwork of the bridge is now completed, the scaffolding ia being taken down and the paint brush is adding a little colour to the ironwork. A little masonry is still reqafred to be done, which connects the ironwork, and masons were cutting the stone into the required shape, underneath the bridge. The banks of the Canal past here, up to the swing-bridge at Twenty-step Bridge, Stockton Heath, were undergoing the same straightening and strengthen- ing process with stone as were those at Thelwall. A considerable amount of progress has recently been made in the work between Walton Arches and Stockton Heath. Taking as the first point of interest the new river divertion at Arpley with the accompanying new road and preparations for the docks, there is a surprising amount of work to be seen. At the part of the deviation where it joins the old river near the Iron Bridge, there has been a very animated sight for some time. The task of laying tba new road over the river which runs along the deviation from Walton, has been commenced. Sixty or seventy navvies are now engaged there deepen- ing the end of the deviation, and also commencing to build up the foundation of the road across the river. When this is done, the portion of the river which i runs ronnd what are known at the 12 arches, to the com- mencement a [he diviation near Walton Arches, will be utilized for dock purposes—that is, when the projected docks are made. The water here,however' would soon be- come obnoxiously stagnant,and so it is intended to place a culvert beneath the new road over the river bed, and thus at every tide the water in the basin of the old stream will be flushed regularly, and receive daily two fre,ll supplies ef water. 'The culvert will be about 12 feet high, and at the present time the workmen are en- gaged in darning up with piles the river opposite River- road so as to construct the culvert It is generally sup- posed, however, that the docks will not be constructed) in Arpley Meadows for a considerable length of time. lAt the Twenty Step bridge, a connection is being made 1 between the Ship Canal end the Old Quay Canal which runs into the river near Howley Quay. Just near Torn Paine's Bridge where the entrance from the Canal into the proposed new docks is to be made, a cmple of steam navvies have for two weeks been er gaged in excavating from the bed of the canal,through the field and bed of the Old Quay Canal, which divides the river from the Ship Canal. This seems to show that the idea of constructing the docks has by no means been aban- doned. although it may be delayed for a long time. The remaining houses which stood hard by Tom Paine's Bridge have also been demolished within the past two weeks,and such a change has been wrought in that loca- lity a-erierally that to one who 11481 not visited it for three months it is totally unrecognisable. There will be a psint or promontory of a triangular shape, formed by the entrance to tho docks, and at the head of this a house connected with the hydraulic station at Latchford locks will be erectal,probably for the purpose of dealing with the opening and closing of the dock gates. from this point to the Twenty-step Bridge there is nothing new to be seen except that all along the banks the builder is hard at work erecting two or three dozen semi-detached villas. At Twenty-step Bridge, however, there is something more to interest the visitor. The large new iron swing bridge has been completed except for a few details. It will convey the highroad over the Ship Canal, and do away with the present stone bridge. As it has been constructed some distance the other side the present old bridge, it necessarily means a deviation of the road, and, hence, instead of the high- way going round a row of houses, it will now run at their rear, and rejoin the old road right opposite the brewery wall. The demolition of the row of houses has already been commenced, and the new road will be open in about a month, we understand.</p>
        <p>MISHAP AT .RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday tnorning a mishap occurred on the Ship Canal works at Runcorn. A gantry bad been con- structed on piles at the Runcorn end of the Bridgewater Lock, and the structure collapsed without warning. The mishap is considered of so little importance that the filling of the canal is still being proceeded with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>SURPLUS LANDS.</head>
        <p>THE SURPLUS LANDS.</p>
        <p>Only one petition ha been lodged against the pro,i- slims of the newly deposited bill of the Manchester Ship Canal Company for powers to deal with their surplus lands. This petition is signed by Sir It M. Brooke. The Bill, being thus opposed, has been placed in i group of bills for consideration by a Select Committee of the House of Commons,which will commence sitting on Tues- day next, under the presidency of Earl Compton, the other members of the Committee being Mr. Leon, Mr. Wulff, and Colonel Scieley.</p>
        <p>THE CANAL AT RUNCORN. The swing bridge across the Old Quay was used for the first titir,e on Tuesday morning as the road to Wig's works, and is another indication that the great . water- way is approaching completion ja the 'district. The bridge has been previously described in these columns, but a few particulars may, be repeated with advantage. The total length of the structure is 236 feet and the roadway is 20 feet wide, with a footpath on each side. the v,idth of the girders forming the sides-of the bridge at thezentre area feet, tapering downwards to 9 feet at the ends. The to;a1 weight of the iron work is 500 toss. Tlie bridge k tipported by 64 steel rollers which I revolve on. a path 26 feet in cliameter, and can be worked in .either dire4!tion. Water is in the motive , .powerand the hydraulic; linu;e is of such a height that the mae worifig,thei levors-can see a ronsitterable dis- tAnco upend 'join the citual thus enabling him the bet- ter to regylatc_the traffl. To swiog. the bridge occu- pies from to Os three minutes. The machinery is from the worlaoUi Vim. Afinstrong, and the builder of the bridge is Sir Wm. Fowler, who constructed the Furth Bridge. In regard to the Ship Canal, it i confidently expected that Runcorn Ducks will be re-opened in the course of a fevr days.</p>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL SOCKS. A correspondent signing himself " Endangered " writes to the Mancluster " Guardian" as follows : The enormous area of the new Ship Canal dip is now inundated with tvrenty-six feet of sligh diluted sewage. My house is ten minute's walk from this pesti- ferous lake, and many people who live still nearer are uotablo to eat their food whenever a breath of air reaches tkem from the vast reservoir cf putrescent liquid. With the lowered tone or health which this state of things in- duces, what, if cholera arrives, will be the condition of thousands who are living close to the docks? Warm as the spring has been, we are months yet from the hottest period of the year. Who will be to blame if pestilence breaks out on the thickly populated Salford side ? 13 it the Mersey and Irwell Committee, ewhroseprreevceenutt special Act gives them the largest p discharges of untreated sewage, or the Salford Corpora. tion, who, judging them from their last discussion, do not appear even to have infermed therrirlves of the frightful nuisance which has been intieted on their citizens? Shall an appeal be made through our district M.P.'s to the Local Government Board, pointing out th over-indulger committee, and of the Mersey and Irwell • eee, and requesting them to send an inspector down to report with a. view to their requiring the Committee to enforce its Act upon the polluting authorities without another day's delay ? There are not a few sanitary authorities higher up the river who are rejoicing because delayed erection of their sewage works is saving them the cost of purifying material s. They might be Supplied with a motive to hurry forward their erections by reqoiring them meantime to disinfect every drop of the foal few- age they are now discharging tootha; .reara.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN-</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN-</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Last week, in these notes I took occasion to refer to the success of a IVarrington journalist in America. —Mr. J. F. Bate. This week I wish to mention another IV arringtoman witose success as a journa- Lst in London is worthy of note,as will be seen from the following notice, culled from the " Manchester Guardian" :—" I have received an early copy of the first pamphlet of a young litterateur, Mr. Arthur Porritt, whose initials are well known to readers of Nonconformist journals. He is a native of War- rington, and seemslikely to doßceraellsittitco Lancashire. The The little • work is entitled „ Outcast London," and will probably have a large circulation, like the "Bitter Cry" of a few years ago." Mr. Arthur Porritt is the second son of Mr. John Porritt, of Arpley-st., and it is satisfactory to state that both of Mr. PorritCs sone are doing well in the journalistic profession. The elder one, Mr. Edward Porritt,is now in America. The pamphlet in question is very well written, the Illustrations and printing excellent, and it is published by the London Congregational Union. Mr. A. Port= is engaged as sub-editor on the "Independent and Nonconformist," and has not yet reached his majo- rity.</p>
        <p>The statement of one of the prisoners, in the case of cruelty to horses, tried at the Borough Court on Saturday, recalled an account of a visit a friend of mine paid to Antwerp a short time ago. On the voyage across, he joined in conversation with a man who was taking auout half-a-dozen horses to Ant- werp. Taking my friend into his cona lence, the dealer informed him of the ultimate destination and use to which his horses would be put. Doubting the truthfulness of the story he decided to watch the proceedings of tke dealer on lauding, and was Surprised at the SCOWS which met his gaze. A num- ber of butchers in blue smocks came and examined the animals, but not in the manner that is usual in this country. The soundne-s of their lungs and legs seemed of no consequence; tie ribs came in for the severest examination—what was wanted was a plentiful supply of beef. The dealer had very little difficulty in finding a customer, and the follow- ing day the butchers would have as little difficulty in llndipg custo'nere for their prime joints of English beef. This incident, coupled with the knowledge , that some of the beef extracts and essences come from the same quarter, I vowed "no. more for me " however grand the name attached to them. An</p>
        <p>--- .‘nent the decision ot the magistrates in the above-mentioned case, I have heard it stated that the sentence borders en severity. I happened to be one of the few who saw the horses at the police court, brought for the magistrates to look at, and a pitiful sight they presented. One of the animals had one eye almost hanging out, and when walking had to take leaps with its hind legs, whilst the other had a leg twice the size of an ordinary one, and with difficulty could put it to the ground. Its tail heel been so dragged by another horse being fastened to it, that the hair had been pulled away and had been supplemented by twisted traw in order to make a noose for the rope. If such cruelty were to be dealt with leniently it would have a bad effect. Suppose a horse by some occident is rendered unfit to do any work. The owner is influenced to keep it in its misery in the hope of meeting one of these Belgian horse traders, and thereby secure a better price than could be obtained from an Englisti horse slaughterer. It is after- wards dragged across the country during the night time to Grimsby, tied to the tail of another, in order to save railway charges. No, three months' hard labour is not too eevere a sentence to men who will inflict upon dumb animals such pro— longed torture.</p>
        <p>I am glad to see that the band-stand at the Park is to be again occupied to-morrow afternoon. The Mayor and Mayoress wilL I am told, bo present, and no doubt, should the weather be tine, them will be a large crowd present to en;o7 the music of the Volunteer band. As I have before said in this column' the allowance made by toe Corporation to the bands who provide entertainment for the towns people is not a very munificent one, and I am told that about £l4 is needed to ensure performances for the season. In order to make uj this deficiency the collecting boxes will again be placed in the Park, and the public will be given the opportunity to subscribe. It =it b) admitted that previous attempts to obtain voluntary contributions were not conspicuously successful. I believe the largest contribution on any one occasion totalled 21. 10d. In addition to the current coins of the realm— chiefly of the smallest denomination—which were found in the boxes, th ere was a varied collection of buttons, but as these were not negotiable they did not augment the band fund. Readers of the "Examicter" are familiar with the fact that a very fair proportion of Warringtonians • have "pitched their tents," so to speak in the great Republic of the West, and your columns show, too, that they have not by any means lost interest in the affairs of the "good old town." lam told that twc of these—the Rev. Mr. and Mrs. Ellison—will be on a visit to England, and to Warrington par- ticularly, towards the end of June. Mr. Ellison is, I need hardly say, a sou of Mr. Luke Ellison, of Latchford, and Mrs. Ellison is a daughter of Nit H. Whitham, who was a few years ago a familiar figure in Warrington, and who still often sends in- teresting contributions to the "Examiner."</p>
        <p>The passengers in one of tlle 'buses which ran to Latchford last Wednesday night, had a rather unpleasant practical joke pla3ed upon them, although they perhaps don't know it. A certain shopkeeper was one of the first to enter the vehicle which, however, became quite full before the jour- ney was commenced. When the 'imo4 reached the bridge a man hailed it and got in; but he was unable to get a seat. Sail the tradesman to him : " I haven't seen you for more than two months. How are you?" 'the reply came slowly and painfully" tosu't feel well—in fact I've only just been discharged from the smallpox hos- pital ; and I hardly feel as tho' I'm over it yet." In three minutes two passengers had vacated their seats, and long before the journey was ended there were only the tradesman and his friend in the vehicle. The stratagem acted very well, but it would not do to repeat it too often.</p>
        <p>Since the Rev.. J. A. Drummond mooted the or- ganisation of the Amateur Photographic Society in Warrington, the institution has met with continued success. The society was founded in 1886, when Mr. T. J. Dawns was mitda president, a po-ition he still retains and does credit to. Prom a Member- ship of 12 in the first year, the number has increased to over 100, whilst the society has been able to buy a library and apparatus valued at over £5O. The chief thing for which it deserves conuratulation is, however, the excellent work which its; members are turning out.</p>
        <p>The members too are repated to throw more zest and real interest into the work, thin a Yuan is generally expected to throw into his profession,and</p>
        <p>the results may be pretty well understood from the following lines by a Warrington lady, wife or an amateur photographer: You prate of the joys of an amateur's life, Of his pride in his lens and his outfit But realty, unless you're an amateur's wife You know very litrfe about it. 'Tis pleasant to welcome a box by express Expectinc a new Easter bonnet, And find it is chemicals—horrible mess! With jaw-breaking labels upon it ! * * * * I cant buy a book, car a new magazine, Not fine statuette, nor a picture ; If there's any spare cash, John lately has seen Some new kind of poisonous mixture. Or gold he most purchase, or hydroquinone, And all sorts of holders and presses' • To get them he'd willingly dine on a bane ' And "don't see the sense of new dresses." MERLIN.</p>
        <p>FRODSHAIE OVERSEERS V. EDWARD&amp;</p>
        <p>In the High Court of Jtratice, London, on Tues- day, the hearing of the ceso, of the Overseers of Frodsham, v. Mr. James Edwards, of nett town- ehtp took place. The overseers had sued Mr. Edwards before the magistrates for his lighting rate, hut the local bench declined to issue a distress warrant for the mount Edwards declined to nay.. The overseen applied for a rule calling upon the magistrates to shnw cause why they should not issue a distress wlrra.nt. The judges held that the rate having been tvissed and signed by the magistrates it was only a magisterial act that they should issue distress. r hey therefore made the rule absolute and gave costs against Mr. EL/lauds.</p>
        <p>2s. TO se. IN TILE £, OF, on 11 Cash orders nt R. W. Ansve's, Fornlturo W-rebonsA, Briclo:r Got. Warrtog.tors. —See fuller advt.. I ale 4. —Advt.</p>
      </div>
