      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>THREH MONTHS FOR CRIJELTY TO Er ORSB9.</p>
        <p>At the IA arrington Borough Court, on Saturday, John Atherton was charged with abusing two horses by travelling them in an unfit tAte, and Edmund Collins was &amp;target] with causing them to be travelled on the Sth May.—DS. Monaghan said he saw Atherton with four horses, and in answer to questions said he was taking them to Manchester. He brsiught the man and the horses to the police stati.n. Three of the horses were tied to the tail of the front hose, which WAS a pretty good one, and it was with difficulty they could get along. Soon afterwards, Collins came up, and said they belonged to him. P. C. Ben also gave evidence as La the lameness of the animal i. Inspector DowtY. R.S.P.C;.A., said that he examin-il the two horses. They were both suffering very acute pain, and were unfit to travel to Manchester.—Collins said he bought the horses at Chester, he being an agent in England tor buying horses, and was sending them for butcher's meat to Mr. Joseph Pool, Rotterdam. Inspector Dowry, said these agents always travelled the horses in the night tim, in ender to wive the railway charges, and when stopped they said they were going to the next station. Ather- ton, the tiriv.r, was sent to gaol for fourteen days hard labour. Collins, who was principally concerned in the cruelty, was made an ex:toil:oe of and sent to gaol for three months' hard labour. Mr. Burgess and Mr. Voisey condemned the practice of buy Pig worn-out horses for Shipment ;On ad, remarking that they had a good idea of the purpose they were used for, it was ordered that the horses be destroyed. HER StSINESS AT CHESTER RACES SPOILED.</p>
        <p>At the Wm rington Police Coon, on Tuesda.V. before Messrs. R. Vrarnett, and E. J. Guest, and Dr. S MO, Harriet Riley, a stylishly dressed woman, was charged with being drunk and ditorderly in Buttertnarket-st., on Monday night. P.O Newey, said that he Saw pris- oner in Winwick-st.. drunk and making use of fearful langusge. lie t 'flowed her to Market ate, end her behaviour became so violent that he was obliged to lock her up. in answer to Mr. Harnett, prisoner said that she had come from Manchester, and was going to the Chester races. Asked what sue would do there, she replied that she would de a hit of bookmaking. The magistrates considered that it would be better to send her to gaol for seen day's impr,sonumnt, 80 that she will be out of the way during tiie races. FLASH NOTEB TOR THIC_CHESTES HAMA:</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on Wednesday, Thomas Kenny and Thomas Brown were earm charged with travelling from Leeds to Warrington without pay- ix,: the railway fare, on the London and North-VVestern taitway, on Tue-day. Detective Ede add that prison- ers were found under the seat of a carriage at Arplev Station, on Tuesday morning. He brought them to the Police qi,tion. A ticket collector said that the fare from Leette to Warrington was 55., and the prisoners Were on h.- way to the Chester races. The Chief Con- stable said that two flash notes were found in Kenny's poeseash.n. Du magistrates inflicteq a fine of 20a. and emits 1r one month's hard labour upon Kenny, and 10s. and coats or 14 day,. upon Brown. Ttwy also ordered each to pay the railway tare from Leeds to Warrington, and the fi,ish notes were corifis7atel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>MINOR, CASES. WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>MINOR, CASES. WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. —i3efore Mr. John .Burgess (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Mr. L. Voidt-y.</p>
        <p>MUNE AND DISOADZALY.—DinieI Croley, John Car- roll, and Edward Cook, were each tined ss. and costa or sleven days for being drunk and disorderly in Scotland- road, on Friday night. ASSAULT.— William Bridge, a married man, elm- /Awed at eoekhedge Mai, was Marled with *Smirking Anna Mary liaads, at the hou-e of Mrs. Bennett, For- haw-et., on the Monday previous.—Mr A. Browne re- presented the defendant. From the evidence it appeared drat the erimplainant went to Mrs. 13.-tinett's to do wash- leg, where the delendant, who had had some drink, was asleep on ;be sofa. Win-n he awoke he commenced " skylarking " and assaulted her, and also broke her canines. Hor screams attracied the attention of some seigiaboiirs who came to her assiatance, and found her 1 on the fl or, unable to speak. Om the following Thurs- day night defendant went to see the complainant ana apologised tor what be had done, and offered to pay for the earrings, but complainant's father was not satisfied, and D.S. Mensghan brought him to the police station on recliy.—Mr. Browne said all his client atid he did, was to put his arms round her neck. He was sent to gaol for Iwo month's hard labour.</p>
        <p>_ kleemax.—Before Alderman Herrison (chairman), 11.6 sari. F. 3.lanks, J. W. Flty% 'rd. and S Wright. DRUNX. AND DISOIIDIRLY.—Joseph Thorpe, 11, Apple- -ss. Inc.u.ang costs or seven days; Peter Gorre- boy. 22, Percivare Fold, 51. or seven daps ; Joseph Peel, 32, Sharpe-st., Sa. or seven days; Joseph and Richard Warburton, 8. fluckley-st., ss. and costs eaeh or seven days; ThOUIRS Rennort, 12, II .Im:sr:it4ane. ss. and costs svven day. ; John Kearns, 7, fiiil-.t., ss. and costs or seven days; 8.1,104 Knockton, 54, and costs or seven days ; Mary AR n Park, ordered 11 leave the town; Miry Bennett, 120 Pierpoint-st, ss. or seven days; Thomas Knoektore 27, Erwood-st., 5.. sod co4a or seven days. Froirriwo.—William C. mor, 22. Bank-st., and John Murphy, 5, Lower Ship Yard, were charged with com- mitting a breach of the peace by lighting in Home- on tie. 3rd inst. They were !squill over in two sureties of £5 to keep the poaels fer sex months or in &amp;fault seven days' imprisonniubt Connor chose the seven days, and said "It will get the freq.,. out of me." Cnntestes osz Ftee.John Buckley, 124, School Brow, and Ticimas Cross, 11, Paul-at., were each fined Is. for *this offence. CROWDED ROUSEEOLD.—Mary Rounds, 3, Buckley's Yard, St. Ja Ines-st., was charg-d with, mils se ull let- ting part of her house in lodgings 'dont being regis- tered.—P.S. Smith said that on April 29 et about 2 a.m., from information received, he entered eefendant's house. He found a bed on the floor, oecupi-d by seven persons, vie., defendant, four daughtera aeed 16. 13. 7. and 5. respectively, and two unfortunates, Annie Johnson and Ellen Price. Defendant said that he did not know the character of the two women, but let them in the house, as she felt sorry for them. They had a bed downstairs. but ran upstairs when the officer came in.—The Chief Constable and inspector Norman each said that defend- ant had a good character, and there was no previous conviction against her, and the magistrates dismissed the case, en elefendaut promising not to repeat the offence.</p>
        <p>TlESDAT.—.4ofore Measra. R. Garnett (chairman), and E.</p>
        <p>-3 -43-13-pRt-Z4 Dr. ..-17.-a-riey DRUNK AND DISORDERLT.—For this Offr...e Alfred Woods and John Woods were each fined ss. and costs or seven days.</p>
        <p>WIDPESDAY.—Refore Mr. John Fairclough (chairman),</p>
        <p>and Councillor J. H. Flemirik. SMASHING PUBLIC-HOUSE WiNpows —Margaret RllGrke was charged with committing damage to the window of Joseph Burkhart!, beerhouse keeper. Crossley-sr., on May 8, and MO with being drunk on the licensed pre- mises of M. Wolstenholme of the Manx Arms on the same date.—Joseph Burkhard said that on Monday prisoner came to his house for some beer, but he 'refused to serve hor a sh • was drunk. She then threw a jug at him, and W.rst: ntv and broke his window doinl t3ainatze of 23. Ili. •Mr. W.,l4eNt,nline said that she Caine to his house drunk, and he refused to serve her. She refused to go out, and he was obliged to put her out. he</p>
        <p>cracked one of his windows also.-Prisoner was ordered to pay 2s. 6d. for the damage to Buckhard's window or seven days, and 104. and coots fir her disorderly -eon, duct in the 111,111 E Arms or 14 days. TRAVELLING WITHOUT A TICERT.-Arthur Johnson was charged with travelling from Manchester to War- rington on the London and Narth-Western railway, without ticket on Tuesday.-Detective Ede stated that he caught prisoner under the carriage seat. Prisoner was fined 10s. and costs and ordered to pay die railway fare or 14 days. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.-For this offence James Rooney was fined 10s. and costs or 14 days, and Mary Rooney was dismissed, and for being drunk Thomas Hinds was fined sa. including costs. TRUILSDAY.-Before Alderman Chandley (chairman), Alderman Sutton, ADC/ Captain Ridgeway. Danzig AND DISORDERLY.-John s bite, Satikey-street, 14 days' hard labour ; Thomas Daley, Butterinarket- street, 14 days' hard lahnur. RIDING ON THE RAILWAY WIT/1017T TICKET.-R young lad, named William Baxendale. Was charged with trav- elling from Liverpool to Warrington on the Che,hire Lines on Wednesday, without having paid the railway fare. A railway official named Marshall, said that he saw prisoner get out of the 8 55 p.m. train on Wednes- day, and as soon as the collectora had collected the tickets in the train, he got on again. Witness brought him out of the train and took him to the office, whore he said he had bought a ticket to Manchester at Liver- pool, but had low it. The station inspector said that he telegraphed to Liverpool and found that there were 176 tickets issued for the 6.55 train, and they were all accounted for. In answer to the magistrates, nrisoner said that he had left a ship at Liverpool a fortnight ago, and was on his way to Manchester. He had sold all his things, and had paid 311. tor his railway fare at Liver- pool. Prisoner was fined 10i. or seven days. Scnoor. CAsEs.-The following were dealt with for contravention the Elucatinn Act :-lohn, -Crnok, Tanners-lane, fined 2s. 61 ; Patrick Brady, Buckley- , street, 2s. 61. ; Jaseph Gerrard, Hopwood-street, 2s. 6d; George Jaundrell, Wartaiit in-street. ss. ; George McGuire, Bank-street, 24. 6 ; Frederick Pritchard, Cartwright-street, 2s. 61.; Frederick Roberts, Kean- street. ss. : Edwin Stacey, Pierpoint-street, 55.; Richard Trantar, Warhurten-atreet, 24. 6d.; George Travis, Refuge Square, 34. 81. ; John Watson, Eaton-street. 38.6 d. Mr. George Riley prosecuted. FRIDAY.-13efore AMerman Harrison (chairman), Alder- man Wright, and Councillor H. Roberts. REFUSING TII QUIT.- Wm. Roberts. no address, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Green-st.,and with refusing to quit tbe Green Men,and alas with assault,- ing the landlord, Wm. Jenks, on bursday. P.C.Barlow said that on Thursday afternoon, he saw a crowd of people round the Green Man Inn, and on going thither, he saw prisoner who was drunk, hold of Mr. Jenks by the throat. Witness had great difficulty in getting him to the police station. Prisoner was fined 54. and costs, or seven days' imprisonment. &amp;Hoot CAsas.-The following were summoned for not sending their chinren to schaol :-Joaaph Darby, 17 Howard-st., Sankey Briklges, fined ss. ; Patrick McNally 13 Dale-st., Sankey Bridges, 24. 61.; Thomas Riley, Howard-st., Sankey Bridges. 2a. 61. ; Rich ird Heath-road, Penketh, adjourned for one mnnth ; Joseph Smith, 52, Evelyn-st., Sankey Bridges. 2s. 61.; Joseph Smith, 91 Howard-st.,Sankey Bridges, 5.4. Mr. 'hearer prosecuted.</p>
