      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>RUNCORN PE CTY SER;IONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PE CTY SER;IONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. —Before Mew% J. Brkindrit, P. Speakman, and</p>
        <p>T Riehy.</p>
        <p>ITN3IDZLLFD Doci.—Williatn-S;atham, Trentham-street, was summoned for allowing hi. dog to be at large with- out a muzzle cr collar, on the 28th ult.—P.C. Lee having stated the circutmtances. defenJant said a number of collars had trees lost.—Fined 5.. AN OLD OFFENDHR flampson, who has ap- peared before the Court between 30 and . 40 times, was sent to prison for 14 days for an offence committed on Saturday night. DautatsktiEss.—John Evans and James Williams, navvies, were each fined ss. for being drunk and dis- orderly on Saturday night.</p>
        <p>WIDNES POLICE COURT. • MONDAY.--Rofore %Ir. 0. Pilkinenß. DRUNKENNESS —Thomas K Ily, Appleton ; Wfliam Tarleton, Caroline-street ; Griffiths Lnesdale-rd ; Elizabeth Ogdev, Marsh-street •, J .11n Rigby, Sinker- street ; Patrick Can, no fixed abode; R,,se Kelly, Ellza- -1 beth-street, (99th conviction) James Power, no fixed abode; and Mary Smith, Water-street, were each fined far this offence.</p>
        <p>&amp;tor Dooa Tuarrs.—John Howard. no fixed abode, was brought up tin remand charged with stealing a pair of trousers, valued at 84. 6 1., the property of Mr. G L. Houghton, pawnbroker, VI4 trim-road, and further with stealing a mat, valued at 1.2‘. 13 1., the pmpery of Mr. F. Neil, pawnbroker. On being formally charged accused made no reply, and as he ia an old offender he was committed to the Quarter Sessions on both charges.</p>
        <p>WIDNES PET IV' SESSIONS. THIMSDAY.—BeforP MeB.4rB.J Lister ritidJ. Brook. _</p>
        <p>DRUNEENNESS.-WillißM Hewitt, Millfield-road ; Mary Ann Tobin, Sankev-street ; Joseph Gerra-d, Haltoo-viPw and Thomas Roberts, Watelloo-road, were Ailed for this offence.</p>
        <p>• AssAutvs.—Sarah Jane MeCaslin, Elizabeth-street, was summoned by Catherine Keogh, a widow, for assaulting her. Defendant did not appear. A fine of 58. and costs was inflicted.—A youth named Thomas Moore, Eleanor-street, was summoned for assaulting a single woman named Jane Dooley, 13, Eleanor-street, who stated that defendant struck her in tbe face with his fist, causing her to have two black eves. Defendant denied the charge, but was fined 24. ed. and costs, or seven days in default. ROBBERIES.—Anu Turton, Hale, was charged with , stealing five silver table spoons, valued at £5, the pro- perty of James Owens, High-at., Hale, between April kali and the Bth of May. Mrs. dwens ueposad that pri- soner was at witness's house serving for two or three hours, alone in the parlour. Previ.ms to that, witness 11 had occasion to use one of half-a-doz nap, sins which were I in a drawer in the parlour, and she did not miss the five in question until the B,h inst., when she went to put the other one hack. C. Webb, Ann-tit., assistant tin Mr. G. Neil, pawnbroker, stated that the prisoner came into their shop on the 3rd inst. and asked Mr. Neil to give her £1 on the spoons produced,which he did. She said they were her property. The prisoner was committed for trial at the Wailer Sessions.—A married woman named Ann Kinsey, Mill-brow, was placed inn toe dock charged with stealing a dolman and a shawl, valued at ss. 6 1., the property of Matthew Connor, 2, Caroline-at. Mrs. Kinsey stated that on the 7th inst. delvinlant was at her house. After she had gone the articles were missed. P.C. Davidson spike to searching detendant's house and finding the articles produced. When charged with steal- ing them she said her daughter, who was with her at the house, had stolen them. Finite., 205., or 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>KINGSLEY FARMER AND HIS FAMILY POWIRAITS.</head>
        <p>A KINGSLEY FARMER AND HIS FAMILY POWIRAITS.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn County Curt, on Tuesday, before His Honour, Judge Wynne Foulkes, an action was brought by Samuel Harvey, tarmer, Kingsley, against Charles C. Bradshaw, artist, Ducie Cliamb ers, 57, Market-street, Manchester, to recover £l6 15s. for lodgings and maintenance in tbe autumn of last year. A counter claim for .£l2 12s. was brought by Bradblaaw again t Ham ey for portraits of himself, wife, and two sons.—Mr. Burton ap- peared for Harvey, and Mr. E Wynne Humphreys represented Bradshaw.—With regard to the original claim it Was stated that a sitting-mom, two bedrooms, , and a darkroom were taken by Bolt, traveller to Brad- shaw who occupied them for •ix weeks, thus incurring expenses to the amount of £lO 10s. Bradshaw and Isis son were there during the period when the rest of the liability was incurred, and defendant had paid into court £4 Us., which he contenoed was ample.—For the plaintiff it aas sworn that when Holt absconded the defendant said he would make it all right. Vi ben the bill was presented he repudiated El iit's authority to pledge his credit. As to the counter-c laim Harvey denied giving orders for the pictures, remariting that farming was too bad to afford portraits. He acknow- ledged that the engravings of John Wesley and Dr. Kitt() were taken &amp;yen from the walls of one of his rooms by Bradshaw, and their places filled with the portraits painted by him. They remained there until returned to Manchester iii Feb. last with some others sent to his house. Evidence as to the claim and the non- ordering of the portraits were given by Harvey, his wife, his two sons, two servants, arid airs. Spruce, a neightour. The rooms were occupied with his goods until February.—Bradshaw positively stated Au orders were given, and written descriptions of the Weans painted made in their presence. He urged that Holt was simply paid by commission, and bad no authority to take the rooms.—John E. Holmes, his manager, corroborated his statement as to the terms of Bolt's engagement.—His Honour considered there was not sufficient evidence to justify him in coming to the conclusion that Ildt was acting as Bradshaw's agent in engaging the rooms. The plaintiff, however, had his rooms occupied by defendant's boxes, and therefore was entitled to something on that account. He would there- fore allow him £3 in addition to the £4 16s. paid Lao court. With regard to the counter-claim there had been unblushing and most monstrous perjury committed, and the question arose—Who was to blame? On the plain- tiff's side it was sworn by Harvey, his wife, two sons, two servants, and Mrs. Spruce most positively that no order was given for the portraits. This confirmed the statement that farming would not allow for such things, and everybody knew that last year farming was not so bright that they usuld afford to indulge in luxuries of , that kind. Thus it was very natural for the man to say it. The only evidence on the other side was that of the defendant alone, as even his manager could not corro- borate it. He must put the reflection of perjury on the defendant, and he did not hesitate to do so. He gave judgment for Harvey on the counter-claim.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 13, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 13, 1893.</p>
        <p>CRICKET. . 1 '</p>
        <p>Secretaries of Crio Pt Clubs are requested to note that reportwof matches, played on Saturday fault be sent to the bead office, Bewsee-street. Warrington, not later than ten o'clock on Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>CRICKETERS. Lawn Tennisers, and Golfers are remlnded that GEO. G. BUSS Ar Co. manufacture high-'lass requi3ites. Their new catalogue is chtainable from Local Dealers, or the Sport- dianutactory, -Peckham Rye, 8.13. Trade &lt;-E-EatiEe Mark.</p>
        <p>BOTELER GRAMMAR SCHOOL IT. WALKER'S BREWERY 2.l.m.—This match was played tn Saturday and resulted in ant easy win for the Grammar School, who scored 106 against 45. F. H. Gornall and Moore made some sur- prising runs,the latter making 25 and the former 23. • __</p>
        <p>ASIPLSY Jtmous v. HOWARD-ST.—This match was played on the ground of the. former on Saturday Wrigh; bowling and Hughes batting well for the visitors. Lang. J. Reynolds. and Pritchard batted well for the homesters, Reynolds also bowling in good style. Final score :—Arplev Juniors. 119; Howard-st. 42.</p>
        <p>BZWYKY-TIOAD WICSLEYANS V. Wurracuoss.—Thia matoh was played on the ground of the former on Saturday, and ended in a victory for Beweey Wealeyans by 40 rune to 37. Whitfield made the largest score for the White- cross and Rowe did .the same for the Bewsey Wes- Leyan.s.</p>
        <p>FARNWORTH Si'. LIMNS CC..,., SANKEY WIRE MILLS C.C.—A match between these two clubs was played on the ground of the latter club, in Lovely-lane, on Satur- day. The game resulted in 41 runs for Farnworth and 19 runs tor Sankey Wire Mills. SUCILBY-STREET S.S. V. HOLT TRINITY.—This match was played on,tne ground of the former on Saturday, and resulted in an easy win for Buckley-street by 76 runs to 41. BRICIL.ST. AND FRIMLS GREEN 2ND V. WIDNES VICTORIA 2No.—Clayed on the ground of the former on Saturday last, and resoited m a tie. Scores :—Brick-st. and Friars Green, 27; Widnes Victoria, 27. WIDNES VICTORIA IST TEAM V. BRICE-ST. AND FRIARS GRERN SUNDAE. SCHOOLS IST TRAlL—Played on the ground of the to met. on Saturday last, and resulted in an easy win for the visiting team. Special 'mention ought to be made with reference to the batting of Critchley, Woods, Bibby and Barton who scored res- pective 4 55, 16, 16, 13. Scores :—Widnes Victoria. 21 for 7 wickets innings; Brick-st. and Friars Green,l26 for 7 wickets.</p>
        <p>TILE BO RUITLI ii ARMS OF WIDNES:</p>
        <p>There was submitted for the approval of Widnes Town Council on Tuesday a sketch of the proposed 6oroug,h arms and also of the jewel for the Mayor's !chain. The fiet-igns had been prepared by the ' (Beers of the College of Arms. The coat Of arms is rtieided into four quarters. In two of these quar- ters is depicted 4 hive wit Ii bees in gold on a rich blue ground, and in the remaining two quarters is !- he red rose of Laricoster on a white ground. The crest is an 'alembic and the motto Industria which interpreted means inoustry enriches. The badge to the Mayoral chain, 'which Alderman Gosaage, the Mayor,h.is generously expressed his intention of present- ing to the Corporation, is in the form of a shield, which hears on the face, the coat of arms and crest, and is sur- mounted by a soda crystal, as the emblem of the town- The links of the chain will be composed of rose, leaf and shield alternately. The designer of the chain is Mr. Bernard C. Collier and of the jewel Mr. E. Harrison. With the designs was presented a report by Mr. Jonathan Carr, of the School of Art, Bedford Park, London,which : reads as fo.lows</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WARRINGTON V. CREETEIAM HILL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. CREETEIAM HILL.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Cheetham Hill, on Satur- day.-The borne men had the first innings which resulted in Heap,Dudar, and Murray hitting well and assisting to run up a total ot 126. Warrington started with Sugg and S.M.Crosfield„ and dile Cheethatn Hill, the start was not promising. Both Sugg and J. J. Crosfield were dis- missed by the wicket keeper standing back at six and eleven respectively, but with S. M. Crosfield and Smith associated runs were put on at a rapid pace. At 40, however, the opening batsman was beaten by Penning- ton, but Smith found a useful partner in G. Clarke. The lattcr displayed more patience than usual, so with the total of 52, ituriay relieved Swallow. The change worked,for Smith failed to gut a proper hold of one from the Hew bowler, and was taken deep in the slips. Clark, Voisey, and Dutton, were quickly got rid of, hut a stubborn resis'anee was offered by Podmore and Clarke. At 80 the last-named was bowled off his legs and the match was styled as good as over. The unex- pected took place, for Podinore and Jeans batted finely, the former hitting Peuningten for a dozen in one over, one being a beautiful drive for six. At 110 Pochnore was out to a grand catch in the country, and Jefferson whipped in. Swallow again took up the attack, and " yorking " Jeans the first ball, ‘Varrinrdon were ha a tninnriry of 15 runs when the innings closed. The Scores are RS inflows 0111CF.TH AM HILL. WARRPIGTON. B C Holt b M Ores' Sugg c Orford b H A field G Heap c.l J Oros&amp; d S M Crosfield b Pennington 7 b Smith 27 J Crosfield 0 Ormrd b A 74 Cadman c Vol,ey b Pennington Sugg Riley b$ M Oros&amp; d 1 ray S Murray e Clarke b 0 elute ti Pennington Smith Gil Ruder et Clark b S L A Iroisey 0 Itl ey b Nur- ' M Crosfiel Blessekley eS M ros- W Outten b Pennington field b Smith 4 W 1-1 Podmore c Murray b W J lifo,gandi Bugg ......11. Riley \2l 'W 11 Pennington net out 13 W 1) Je rt. bH A Swall..w 8 A N Orford c and bSUgg 3 Y Jefferson not out 0 H A swaliow b S 32. Crosfield 1 Ex ms 5 Bxtrts 7</p>
