      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>COAT OF ARMS.</head>
        <p>THE COAT OF ARMS.</p>
        <p>Pre-eminently --i-t--w(7ail-ii-FP;(11 deaTiable that in the forefront of the Arms o? a Lancashire town, the Red : Hose should be an important feature, and dealing as we . are with a comparatively modern town whose associa- tions, apsrt frown commerce,, are interwoven with the history of he County, it would seem that no more suit- able expression of its commercial prosperity could be found than the Hive and Bees, and its insertiott on the arms is a proud assertion of the successfui career of the town and its cause. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>TIER CREST.</head>
        <p>TIER CREST.</p>
        <p>If it is admitted-that-Chi; -"iTn•i; Carry the designation of the town up tan certain point, the story is carried on by the Crest, the alembic has been chosen as at once the most primitive and yet historic form of chemical research. It may be remarked that it is believed the use of the alembic, if adeptedi will be its first introduction into either arms or crest. The small earthenware vessels used for distillation in primitive times have been adopt- ed in one or two instance., but where chosen they may be considered is only exemulifying ,the principle in its experimental or smaller form, but cannot be taken as emblematic of commerce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>TSB MOTI'O.</head>
        <p>TSB MOTI'O.</p>
        <p>The motto must be regarded as the spoken word of those who bear the Arms mid Crest,. not associated with locality but esprtrising the sentiment of the bear, rs. The mato chosen wins to pithily and fitly express the sen- timent of those who recovaiie the cause of their success.</p>
        <p>TEN CHAIN. In choosing the design for the fThain, it must be remembered, it is not only to hold the Badge of Office, but is supposed to he emblematic of the district in which the office is held. To carry out this idea—the Rome,Leaf, and Shield—as indicating Lancashire in the simplest and most direct form have been introduced. The Red Rose Can taloned:le borne by a Lancashire town. The Shield is suggestive of the part Lancashire has always borne in armed support of the rose, and the Leaf may be taken as showing the distric:. in County Palatipe of Lancashire. and not confining the meaning to a town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>THU 3RWItL.</head>
        <p>THU 3RWItL.</p>
        <p>The Jewel is atipposed to cointine all the emblems borne by the boryuizh, and may be taken to embrace in a decorative form—(l) The Coarof Arms, (2) The Crest, (3) The Emblem, (*) The NI-At°.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>EMBLEM.</head>
        <p>THE EMBLEM.</p>
        <p>In choosing the sodacrstal as the emblem of the town. the story is made truwe complete, for. while many Lancashire towns might claim to owe the success to chemical science, toe adoption of the crystal unerringly points to the pirticolar commerce of the town itselt.</p>
        <p>OBNICRAL REMAJIKS.</p>
        <p>It may be taken as a test a 4 to whether the Emblems have been successfully selected, and to each other their proper relative proportion astogned. whether its story could be told tr. anyone ignorant of the locality and yet able at once to. designate the town. No other but a Lancashire town could associate itself with the Red Rose. The Rose has been taken from the Arms of Henry VII., and it may be intereto ing to note, is incorporated ia the Arms of the present Duke of York.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>CORONATION OF THE MAY QUEEN , AT 601 BORNE.</head>
        <p>CORONATION OF THE MAY QUEEN , AT 601 BORNE.</p>
        <p>Glorioas summer weather favoured the annual custom of crowning the May Queen,which was per- formed on Saturday, on the Recreation Ground The streets were early thronged with spectatorsopecial trains being run from Warrington, St. Helens, Wigan ann Leigh, besides wagonetl es from various outlying districts. The principal streets were gaily decorated with nags and bunting, the Queen's residence in High- st. having an arch of evergreens over the door and a,. rope of evergreens stretched across the road bearing the inscription "God bless our little May, Queen." The pro- cession, consisting oh over 1,000 childrenjeft the field by , the .Grundy-at. entrance; and afier parading the streets 1 to the Smithies, returned by way of Bridge-at., entering , the field by Caunce'e-court. Lady archers on horseback 1 headed the procession, followed by the Golberne Bind, ' Maypole dancers, flower girls, bakers, standard bearer, a hurry containing the " rising generation," butchers,l and hospital nurses; after which came the Warrington Ragged Sunday School Band, the Upo Bo troupe of dancers. footballers. lied Riding Hoods, soldiers, Grace Darting in a boat (represented by Miss Ruth Cooke), old folks, Highlander playing the bagpipes,Darby and Joan, blacksmiths and strikers, milkmaids, flower girls and stable boys • then came the Ramenhead Rand, followed ' by the Hindley Green monis dancers, who danced seve- ral times along the route. gipsy dancers,Colonel Thomp- son's miniature volunteers, sword, sceptre, and crown bearers ; the Queen, Miss Agnes Clayton, in a carriage! drawn by four greys, tollowed by pages and beefeaters, lifeguards, maids of honour in carriage, carriaoes con- taining ex-Queen with maids of honour, blue jackets, hat binders, gardeners, idasterers, and slaters; the sea- sons were represented, alter which came Britannia (Miss ' S. Wright) on a furry accompanied by persons represen- ting the United Kingdom ; peasants, queen of the gipsies (Miss M. McMullen) and her tribe, Firs-lane Band, Sweeney Toti the barber, farmers and colliers, while bringing up the rear were two camels and an elephant from Da1,711 menagerie. After the procession had marched round the ring. Mr. G. W. Hurst, the trick 1 bicycle, gave an exhibition of his skill. The Maypole dancers, conducted by Mr. J. Dearden, next performed the plaiting of the ribbons round the pole, after which the coronation took place, the ceremony being ably per- formed by the ex-Queen, Miss F. H. Eckersley. Dances were now given by the Upo Bo Troupe of clog dancers, flindley Green morris dancers, and the gipsy dancers, alter which the volunteers went through their exercises, the aftetnoon's amusements closing with the band play- ing the National Anthem. Dancing was afterwards kept up till dusk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>SINGULAR BLOOD-POISONING CASE.</head>
        <p>SINGULAR BLOOD-POISONING CASE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday Mr. Brighouse held an inquest at the Town Hall. tit. Helens, on the body of Wm. Ashcroft, of 54, Birehley-st., a glass grinder and polisher, who died from the effects of blood-poisoning, which supervened from a cut which he received on his big toe by the breaking of a sheet of glass at the Ravenshead Plate Glass Works cn the 31st April. George Davies, 84 Dun- riding-lane, said it• was employed at the works. On the date named he was sent tor to nut a piece of glass in one of the waggons which draws the glass from casting to the grinding *lied. He had cut a portion at the top. hut had to draw the glass from the waggon to get lower down, and when pulling it to the end of ths waggon the glass" flew" and dropped on Ashcroft's toe. John Bridge, a St. John Ambulance man, employed at the works, washed out the wound and bandaged it, but hlood-poinoning set in. A juror said as Ashcroft was a good workman he would move that a rider should be added to the verdict that di,. company should compen- sate the widow for the loss of her husband. The Cor- oner said the jury had only to find out the CAM of death. There might be,a great many grievances, but they had nothing to do with them. The jury return- ed a verdict of" Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>BABE. liel:F-CROWNS.</head>
        <p>BABE. liel:F-CROWNS.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes, on Wednesday, before Mr. Justice Wills, John Wilson, aged 39, who was described as a mason and a ticket-of-leava man, pleaded guilty to passing base coin in Manchester. By the help of children he had enticed into his nefarious service he had succeeded in uttering many counterfeit half-cross among poor people. His Lordship said the prisoner had been guilty of a cruel and heartless fraud, and sentenced him to seven years' penal servitude.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>IMPERIAL INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>THE IMPERIAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>The imperial Institute was opened on Wednesday by the Queen. who was attended by many members of the Royal Family. The Prince of Wales read an address to tier Mrnajessttpyre . who then declared the Institute open, with n Ayer that it might never cease to flourish aea as a lasting emblem of the unity and loyalty ot her Empire. The Queen on returning to Buckingham Palace appeared on the kalcony and warmly acknow- ledged the acclamations of the assembled thousands.</p>
        <p>SHOCKING FRATRICIDE BY A BOY OF SIXTE EN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night, at Ilkley, Frederick Cook, a youth of sixteen year: of age, a printer's apprentice, killed his brother, a little boy of two years, by striking him on the head wit Ii a hatchet. He afterwards auertm- dered himself to the police, statin2t, he had killed his brother, as he wanted to do away with himself. Pris- oner was charge n Thursday with the murder, and .remanded.</p>
        <p>Probate has been gratitod of the %All of the late Mr. " Abington" Baird, the gross p-rsonality being sworn at £846.051 12. 11., :mei the net value at £8,31,719 The proh, te snd estate duty paid amounted to the sum of £33,272.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>AT OUR LOCAL_CiIURCHES,</head>
        <p>AT OUR LOCAL_CiIURCHES,</p>
        <p>WA R RI N G TON. ANNIVERSARY SERVICES AT BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The anniversary services of the-T;er-st., Wesleyan Schools, were held in the Bewsey-road Chapel on Sun- day. The preacher in the morning and evening was the Rev. F. C. liable,. formerly stationed in this circuit. there were good congregitioni. During the morning an anthem "Judge me, 0 God," by Mendelssohn was well rendered by the choir assisted by an orchestral band. In the evening "Art thou weary," th a tune composed by the organist,Alr. J. M. Muir,was also sang. The anthem " Hear my. prayer" (Mendelssolin), was exceedingly well given by the choir and band, Mrs. Dobson (lib Miss Riley) taking the solo parts with her usual good taste. In the afternoon a musical service by the choir and children was given under the leadership Of theorganist Mr. J. M. Muir. i• Mr. J. Taylor presided, and gave a short address on " A good name is better than great riches." The opening hymn was" When he . cometh "in which the congregation joined. The con- , gregation also joined in the following hymn" Brightest and best " (Bishop Heber), A rnotett entitled "Angels Holy "(Hy Smart) followed, in which the parts were well taken by Misses Kent, L. Skelland, L. Lawton, L. 'Place, Master H. Bennett, Messrs. W. White, and E. Whitehouse. The next piece." Sanutus " (Gounod) by ' Mr. A. Baker and choir, was very ably gone through. The congregation then joined in" Here we suffer grief ,1 and pain," after which a mntett " 0 Lord most holy " (F. Abt) by Miss Pemberton • and choir was well given. Tennyson's " Crossing the bar " (Dr. J. F. Bridge) was , thegem of the aftersinon, the choir entering into the , spirit et the piece, and therefore did full justice to it, so that in the evening, they repeated it at tile ite.sire of the Rev. F. C. Hahne. Mr. F. Cole, next gave a cornet solo "0 rest in the Lord" (Mendelssohn) after which "When i-is salvation bringing" (Joshua King) was sung by the choir and congregation. Miss Muir then sang t' I love to hear the Story" (F. Clay) very tastefully-, and the children jnined in the chorus, and was felloweel by a duet" For so bath the Lord commanded" (Mendelssohn) by Messrs. W. Whiter and J. Bennett. Au enjoyable, service was brought to a close by the well-known Sunday school hymn " Wiien mothers of Salem." The collections for the day amounted to £4O is. Bd. THE P.S.A.„ Al' WYCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, the usual Pleasant Sunday Afternoon meeting was held in Wycllfe Church. The membership still increases, eighteen new members being enrolled on Sunday. As an instance of the growth of the P.S.A. movement it may be stated that thetotal membership of the societies in Lancashire, and Cheshire is between 30,000 and 4000. Mr. G. Wallington presided, and delivered a short address referring to the decision come to, it a meeting of representatives of P.S.A. Societies, held at Ashton-under-Lyne,to form a federation of P.S.A. societies in Lancashire and Cheshire, for the purposes of fellowship council, and co-operation. Mr. Taylor, Police Court Missionary, also delivered an address, basing his remarks upon a passage of scripture in Isaiah, dwelling more particularly on the word " Water." Mrs. (Heave and Mr. W.B. Howard contr•buted smlos. The Rev. J. Yonge, Mr. T. Wallington. and Mr. T. Hawthorn also took part in the service. GOLBORNE-ST. BAP fIST CHAPEL. - - -</p>
        <p>The Sunday School anniversary services were held on Sunday. The Rev. A. J mes-Parry preached to an unusually large congregation in the morning and even- ing. In the afternoon there was a children's service, 'which was very well attended. A short and suitable address was .'elivered by the Rev.D. W. Roberts, pastor. FArtions of the Scriptures were recited by Miss R. Ward and Master A. Patrick. A noticeable feature of the service was the excellent singing of the children, and great credit is due to Mr. A. Fieldhouse and Mr. F. Hickman for their careful preparation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHiPEL.</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHiPEL.</p>
        <p>The distributiou ot priz-s awarded to the scholars at- tending the Sunday school attached to this church took place on Sunday afternoon in the Primitive Methodist Chapel, Legh-st. Mr. Hudson,. Stockport, performed the interesting ceremony. During the service which was held, solos were delivered by Mr. C. Littlewood and other members of the choir. ANNIVERSARY SERMONS.</p>
        <p>The LRtchford Primitive Methodist Sunday School anniversary services were held on Sunday in the Primi- tive Methodist Chapel, Latchford, Mr, J. T. Turley, a student of the Primitive Methodist College, Manchester, being the preacher. In the afternoon the choir ably rendered the service of song, entitled "Lost in the Wild Wooda." Mr. S. Drought, of Widnes. read the connec- tive readings. There was a good congregation at path lervice, and the collections were in aid of the SChool funds,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>OPENING OF A NEW CATHOLIC MISSION.</head>
        <p>OPENING OF A NEW CATHOLIC MISSION.</p>
        <p>The new Roman Catholic Mission Church, situated in Liverpool-road, Bank Quay, and which is intended to be the nucleus cf a larger church and schools was opened on Sunday, by the Right Revd. Dr. O'Reilly, Bishop of Liverpool. There were also present the Very Rev. Dean. Finnegan. of 81. Bede's Appleton ; Very Rev. Dean Kelly, of Bt, James's Bootle ; Father Clarke, St. Mary's Widnes ; and Father Quirk, of St. Joseph's Wigan. High Mass was sung at 10.30 by Father Clarke, awl the preacher for the day was the Very Rev. Dean Kelly. In his discourse in the morning the rev.. gentleman spoke of the missionary spirit which had attended the church in every century since her establishment by Jesus Christ, and went on to enumerate the moral onnciples by which she built up the civilization of Europe. At the evening service there was the Rosary, sermon, and Benediction. Dean Kelly preached on the devotion to the Mothernf God, and dwelt at length upon its advan- tages. Bishop O'Reilly offieiated at the Benediction, assisted by Father Clarke and Father Roach, his secret- ary. The congregations at each service were large. The musical part of the services. were very well carried out by a choir improvised for the occasion, and made up of members of the choirs belonging to St. Mary's and St. Alban's. The music in the mornin2 was Father Turner's let Mass, of which a very careful rendering was given. Mr. Mather, organist ot Bt. Mary's Buttermarket-st., presided at the organ, and the choir was under the leadership of Mr. Whitehead. The inside of the church was very nicely decorated with plants, kithlly lent by Mr. J. Crosfield. of Walton Lea. The mission is capable of, holding about 200, and is more comfortable and larger than the cutting shop room in whieh services were held previously. About an acre of land has been acquired, which will be sufficient for a good sized church and school. The work of building a school, will we understand be proceeded with as soon as possible. Father Ryan is in charge of the mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>MESSRS. EVANS'S GOLBORNE MINERS.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. EVANS'S GOLBORNE MINERS.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED TERMS _OF_ SETTLEMENT- NOT</p>
        <p>ACCEPTED.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of miners connected with the Got- borne collieries of Messrs. Richard levana and Co., Limited, was held on Tuesday, for the purpose of con- sidering the result of Saturday's conference between the heads of the firm and the men's representatives. The questions were fully gone into, after which it was unani- mously agreed not to accept as final the proposed terms of settlement offered by the firm. This decision wai telegraphed to the secretary of the Miners' Federation, ho arranged to call the committee together with a view of sanctioning or otherwise the giving of fourteen days' notice on behalf of the men next Tuesday, that being the day when the miners comm-nee their week. The Golborne collieries were stopped on Tuesday, but it was agreed that work should be resumed to-day pending the decision of the federation. Meanwhile, 96 per cent. of the men have voted for a general stoppage, if a satisfactory settlement is not arrived at.</p>
        <p>At Leeds Assizes on Wednesday, John Henry Gold,the madman who kshot Police Constable Henderson dead while that offifYr was trying to capture him at Middles- brough, was inticted for murder. The Judge ordered him to be detained in a critninal lunatic asylum.</p>
        <p>The Royal Laundresses say Critefiley's "STANCH °LOB/4 s unequalled for imparting a beautiful finish to starched linen. 4c., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. Corn- wall, anys s•—•' Illy wife will not finials our linen without yonr Starch Gloss:" From Mrs.—, Bradford s— "Dear sit.— I am pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at Id., 3c1., and 6d.. hy most starch sellecs. It not in doe&amp; will procure it. 12., cuaczE,ny. Blackburn. P:oprietor,--Advt...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
