      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MONDAY,</head>
        <p>MONDAY,</p>
        <p>In the House of Comm-ins- on Monday the Sheriffs of the City of London attended to present a petition Against the Government of, Ireland ,Bill from the Lord Mayor, aldermen, and Common Connell. '</p>
        <p>Mr. Seminar addressed a series of questions to Mr. RETCH regarding county magistrates in the Duchy of Lincaster, an alleged refusals to take the declarations of memb •rs of a different political party at election times. Mr. BRYCE explained that he did not bring any charges whatever, and that the remarks he made were quite general.</p>
        <p>The SPEAKER ruled out of order instructions by Lord , R. Churchill, Mr. C. Darling, and Major-Oeneral Golds,, worthy,: on the Herne Rule Bill, and at half-nose-flair the ' House went into Committee on the measure. Mr. CIARBERLLIN, moved the psstponement of Clause 1, which provides : and after the appointed'day there shall be in Ireland, a Legisliture consisting of her Majesty the Queen and of two Houses, the Legislative Council and the Legislative Assembly." Mr..GLADSTONR Said the result of the adoption of the course of nroceid- ing proposed by the member for West Birmingham would be that practieal discussion on all bills would be impossi. ble. Colonel Sounderison, Sir H. James, Mr.' Asquith, Mr.. T. W. Russell, and others took part in the debate on Mr. Chamberlain's amendment, which was rejected by a maintity of 57. Mr. DARLING moved an amendment to safeguard the supremacy of the Imperial Parliament. Upon this a verbal amendment was moved. by Mr. DOTIAPIELD, and after discussion, Mr. J. Mormay moved the closure, which was carriini by a majority of 48: Mr, Bousfield's amendment was rejacted by a majority of. 52, and Lord R Catinctum, then moved to report progress. Mr. (ILAD;TONE declined, pointing out that he was not responsible for the introduction of the closure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House.of Commons on Tuesday evening, Sir E. GREY said the Argentine Government had hitherto re- fused to surrender Jima SPENCER BALFOUR in &amp;gibe- quence of their. being no extradition treaty between them and the United Kingdom. . Leave was given to Dr. Cameron by 248 votes to 180 to bring in a bill to put an end to the Establishment of the Church of Scotland and to deal with the public endowments thereof oiri the occurrence of vaea, cies. . The Howe again went into Committee on the ' Home Rule Bill. Mr. BaatettEranoved an amendment to clause Ito make the Irish Legislature "subordinate to Parlia- ment." Mr.GLAnsToNE said the intention of the amend- ment was to cast a slight on the Irish Parliament. The amendment was rejected by 292 to 257. Mr. W. RED- MOND moved an.ainendnient to describe the Irish Legis- isture by the word " Parliament." Mr. Gnensromt and Mr. BALFOUR spoke against the amendment, and Mr. I JOHN MORLEY appealed to Mr. Redmond not to press it to a division. A division, however, was taken, with the result that the amendment was rejected by to 40. Mr., T. W. BUSSeLL moved an amendment to omit the Legislative Council, leaving only one Chamber Thel debate was continued till near midnight, wheu progress ' was reported., ,</p>
        <p>In the finuge of Lords the Railway Servants Hours of Labour 13111 was read a second time.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday, Committee on the Home Rule Bill was resumed. Mr. r. w.Russtlrs amendment to chuse 1, to establish one House oely,was further discuss,al. Mr. GLADSTONZ said the wide expe- rience of the colonies had sanctioned the prineiple of a Second Chamber. Mr.J. MTAErrint add the Nailohalist members were quite prepared to accept the principle of a Second Chamber as a safeguard 'against toe, vivid legislation and a gearahtee for the protection of the minority. Mr. RA-Irwin spcke in support of the amend- ment. Mr. J. RULAIOND said the Nationalists would vote olidk for the Second Chatraht4. On a division there voted—tor the amendment, 244 ; against, 225 majority for the Government, 51..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>THURSDAY'.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY'.</p>
        <p>Ti the flouse of Commons on Thursday eveninr Mr. OLANITONN informed Mr. Woods that while it was desk- able there should tie fiirther opportunity for ciiiicussion on the Miners' Eight Flours Rill, he thought, seoitir that there was so censiderab;em, minority opposed to tne hi!! on matters of principle, that it was hardly a matter which should be referred M the Grand Committee on Law. Repking to a further question, 'the right. Wm. gentleman said be was unable to promise an opportu nity for di,cossing the bill.</p>
        <p>Mr. Asqctinz broughtin a bill to amend the vaccina- tion taws in accordance with the recommendations of the Vaccination • Commission. The Rouse again went into Committee on the Mine Rule Bill. On clause 1 Mr. W. REDMOND moved an amendment that the. Legislative Assembly should l, called the Rouse of Commons of Ireland. This was 1-4- - jeered tu 482 t 0,34. On the question that clause 1 itano part of the bill Mr. CHAMENFiLAIN complained that thi &amp;Merriment had not brought forward any proposal for dealing with Ulster separately, and asked what were their intentions respecting the retention of Irish mem- bers, finaneial arrangements, kc. Mr. GLADSTONE ae- used the ript hon. gentleman of seeking to eodang,,r the hill by prolongation of debate, and took hi- stand on his sp.,•,c'i in intro-liming the bill. The debate was car- tied en till midnight.</p>
