      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Zrttsrs to tin Vidor.</head>
        <p>Zrttsrs to tin Vidor.</p>
        <p>THE NEW WARRINGTON BATHS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The announcement of the date for the opening of the new baths will no doubt have been hailed with no small feeling of satisfaction by the public generally, but particularly so by those who find this means of recrea- tion th 9 most suited to their disposition and inclization. Now, that we shall shortly be in possession of a bath worthy of the town—all credit to those to whom we owe it—l think it most desirable that some impetus Should be given to make the baths popular amongst all sections of our community, and I venture to snake one suggestion towards the accomplishment of this, which I hope will meet the eyes and approval of those who can give effect to it. It is a fact, I believe, that the number of season tickets issued of late has not been se great as in former years, owing no doubt, in a measure to the condition of the baths themselves, but, mere, I think to the rather prohibitive priCo charged for them. With- out going so far as to suggest a permanent reduction, might not the present year be made an experimental one—in view of the fact that it will be almost half gone ere the baths are opened, and the season tickets offered at a reduction (without prejudice) to say 10s. and 7s. 6d. in case of two in a family under the prescribed age. Such a reauction would induce many to obtain them this year who perhaps (like myself) would other- Wise feel inclined to hold back until next year. I feel confident the Corporation would not be losers by the oxperiment —Yours truly,</p>
        <p>WHAT IS SALVATION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>FRO TEMPORE.</head>
        <p>FRO TEMPORE.</p>
        <p>".There are more thinge in M:aven. and earth,yoratto,</p>
        <p>Than are d ra,tmt of in 'thy phi lasophy."—ifan:let.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have been hitherto as the voice of one crying in the wilderness, none heeding. To my question only the mocking echo has replied, " What is Salvation"? It is a question upon which we, sea nation, art spending millions of good money, and, under the form of prosecu- tion and persecution, one in which thousands of geod lives have been spent in wasted agony. The Christian rat burnt the Jew and thin, to show themselves to be believers in the doctrine of peace and goodwill, turned upon each other and tortured and burnt those who differed in the slightest, from the dominant dogmatism. What is it, this question that has filled the world with sorrow and woe for the better part of 18 hundred years? From every pulpit, every Sunday, we are implored to put our foot upon the first rung of this dream-reared Jacob's ladder ; but still the implorers will not, cannot, or dare not, tell us what is the salvation they preach about, and of what event it is the consequence. It is all very well to tell me that the top of this Jack's Beanstalk reached to leaven, but ere I climb, let me see where its roots are and if its stein warrant such high pretension ! Nor do I need any one to quote to me more or less inappropriate texts from Scripture. What I want is the living opinion of living men and women on this head. I have no respect for the opinion of the Fathers—men who believed the weather to be ruled by atmospheric devils. The weather forecast of to-day haa relegated these old duffers to the limbo of detected humbugs. It was the fashion ot the time to prate of things they did not understand. And to-day every pulpit in the land is ' an evidence that this fashion has not quite gone out. The failure of this fashion would be the destruction of preaching. It is easier to rant than to reason upon his question as is evident from the number of people who are prepared to do the first, who can not be brought to sio the last. Your correspondent " An old Chartiat " I admire for his very evident sincerity. But he advances nothing to induce me to come down on his side of the fence. As a fellow mortal, having a fellow interest in this grave question, he will not he offended with me for speaking openly, but, withal, kindly, upon it. I would premise that the Supreme, as the God 4f truth and reason should have notking tut truth and reason offered jn his service. Our friend says—" I will tell him some- ttiing about salvation which I have learnt these 46 Ars." Now I submit that during the time he men- ms the whole aspect of this question has undergone a radical change. Whatever of reason and logical con- sistency this dogma might have had 50 years ago, has now entirely disappeared before the advance of positive knowledge, the possession of which renders it utterly impossible for any reasonable and reasoning being now to hold this doeina. I think I know somewhat of the history of the Rev. J. Barker he speaks of. He was a " barker." upon quite inadequate grounds he turned from ranting Methodist to equally ranting Atheist and, then, upon equally inadequate ground, reverted to his original ranting. His type is not yet extinct. There are men even now who pose as having once been "doubter.," and who shake their empty heads over the sad lapse, with the remark that now by special interfer- ence of their esp-riol Providence they are again able to swallow any amount of doctrinal absurdity. Sometime I may go more fully into this matter. Meantime what, in the light of our present knowledge, is salvation P—. Truly yeurs,</p>
        <p>DAREt,BURY NNW RATE</p>
        <p>_ The School Board rate for the townships of Dares- hury,lieck wirk,Newt on. Moore, Acton Grange,llat ton and Preston Brook has just been fixed at I+i. in tli i; for the half-year ending Michaelmas, 1893, that tor the previous half-year being lid. in the .e.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="4" unit="words">
