      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>No big individual totals were run up by either of the teams in the match between our Ist Eleven and ' Cheetham Hill. R. Smith put in 23 runs, which was the highest on the field, and Clark scored the smallest—one ; that i, with the exception of W. Dutton who overtaxed the scorer by getting him to enter naught less than— well it was 0. S.M. Cr psfiela displayed some capital bowling, and he took four wickets. J. d. Crosfield and Sligg exhibited discreet bowling, whilst the fielding all round earned for the players a figurative shoulder slap of approbation. G. H. Dudar for Cheetham ran up 29 much to the surprise of everyone, as he commenced in a most indifferent and disheartening style. But to re- produce a far-fetched pun made by a spectator—he evidently meant to Cheet-'em. Warrington were rather lax in their batting for the total scores were :—Cheetham 128, Warrington 111.</p>
        <p>The warm rays of the summer sun had a considerable amount to do with many of the large crowd attending the second eleven v. Cheetham match at Arpley. But somehow, even when there is a plentitude of climatic benevolence on hand, there is not exactly the saris show of enthusiasm in cricket mad* at Arpley as there used to be. What's the cause? By the way, I was told as I was leaving the field on Saturday, that a lady enthusiast, attired in a new fashioned crinoline presented herself at the narrow entrance, but through the boundaries not having been extended for the entrance of a spacious crino- line' the good lady had to return. Now I know crinolines II are used in town, and, en passant, I should like to cheer ' up the wire-drawers by telling them there is a prospect of a much greater demand being made for wire hoops. But to get to the game.</p>
        <p>It was rather slow taking it all round. For Warring- ton, W. Proud did the part of the champion batsman, making 30 not out. Bibby and Unsworth went in to- gethor at the initial stage, and by the time Unswurth had played for 11 and Bibby for eight, the former was bowled and Brown thou came in; hit two boundaries in succession and was bowled for ten ; and then the ball hit his wicket instead of his bat, and he came out. In- cluding Proud, who merited a great deal of praise, these named were the four chief batsmen. The scoring of the remainder was indifferent, and the total came to 70 against 100 for Cheetham. Bibby and Woods did the bowling, between them taking nin• wickets altogether. Taking the Cheetham men all round, they showed better fielding and batting than their opponents.</p>
        <p>By the way I ace that rising club, the Bewsey Forge Engineers clinked the willow with the White Rose, on Saturday, and when they came off the field their admirers were justified in attuning their otherwise pro- illinUol.l4 warbles to the rhythm of " Here the conquer- ing Heroes come." The 'gineers did the trick by 27 to 18. Seven of the White Rose men scored nothing which strikes me that in thus giving their opponents a chance,thesa gentlemen wereshowing a kind of generosity or the modesty that ought not to be the " prop" of a cricketer. That is unless the superior bowling of the "other side," was the cause.</p>
        <p>Thorpe batted in excellent manner for the Engineers and the Blathers Davey " bowled everyone out," as it were in taking wickets.</p>
        <p>Sankey Bridges neighbourhood has turned out some ferocious footballers in the winter ; but it's starting out early to provide a few willowmen of a different calibre. The place boasts of the Sankey Wesleyan C.C., and the Sankey Wire Mills, C.C. Now these two teams have early opened a competition for ascendency, to see which shall be " cock of the walk," or in other words each of them is struggling to secure the buttered crumb. The mill men have played two games at home and the defeats were inflicted in a brilliant manner—but by the other side. Not likeing to follow their example the Wesleyans wop their first match, and list Saturday when they met Daniels Band Club, of St. Helens, they triumphed by 100 to 39. " Dave "Roderick for the Wesleyans ran up 44 out of 100 runs, which is what I call " hitting hard " at the other side. E. Watkins was noteworthy for the good bowling he did,and performed the "hat" trick. But I say,who is going to prove the better team next week ?</p>
        <p>By the way, I have heard this week that a movement is on foot for petititioning the Corporation to provide a proper recreation ground, where small cricket teams can Pitch their wickets. Now, no one can deny the neces- sity of this. At any rate no one can who I,appened to pass behind the Gymnasium on Saturday afternoon. There were actually five teams of juveniles playing on the open space there. That is an eloquent argument in favour of a public recreation ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>BOWLS.</head>
        <p>BOWLS.</p>
        <p>Saturday saw the inaugural match of the season in connection with the Warrington and district bowling league. The league is made up of si hotel bowling clubs. They play a match every week u..til September. The officials are : Mr. John Evans, president ; Mr. Wm. Mclntyre, vice-president ; and J. T. Whittle, lion. sec.; and Mr. E. Macaulay, lion. treasurer.</p>
        <p>The Bulls Head and Bank Quay clubs were the con- testants on Saturoay, and a tight match ended in a victory for the Bulls Head, by 37 points. No less than el2ht of the Bulls Head players scored 15 points each These are the scores . BULLS HEAD. BINH QuAr. James Shaw ls— John Taylor l— 2 :•,haw ls— 30 J Massey s— 17 Ben. Hall lo— 40 E Weave ls— 32 Fair. Turner Thomas Locker R Anderton (capt.) 14— 84 John Hutchinson ls— '7 Thomas Critchley ... 15— .S 3 W Gleave (capt ) Frank Berry ...... ... 10-109 8 Massey ls— 87 W Bolton l5-124 J Dleave D Daniels 3-127 John Helsby R Fairbrothe T Hackett l5-157 T Ashton -120 Majority for Bun Head, 37.</p>
        <p>Last year the aggregate ACOPER made were ;1-13a1ls Head, 1,304; Cemetery, 1,245; Rink Quay, 1,193 ; Red Lion, V 9 ; Saracens Head, 783 . The Bulls Head evidently mean to lead the way again this year, from the start they made.</p>
        <p>Saturday was fruitful of another bowling match of interest. The club from the Warrington L. and N.W. District Good's Managers Department went to Gateacre and contested with the merry men of the District Traffic Department at Garston Dock. It was an enjoyable game: especially to Warrington, because they came out 20 points ahead of tha other club. Warrington's total was 141 ; Garston 121.</p>
        <p>Hurrah for the Warrington Cemetery Inn Club In a Lancashire and Cheshire Bowling Association match on Saturday they played the Conquering Heroes with that redoubtable club—the Brooklands of Sale. This was indeed a good victory, especially as it was won by 62 points. These are the mores :</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. BROOLLANDS. W Wood 2l It Twilly J Ashton 2l F Simpson l3 J Hewitt (mot) 2l T Rook J Nevins 2l W W Baker R Farrington 2l It Goulden 2O RU Sewell 2l AM Walker (cap) l5 W Melllroy J Allison 2l J Bunton W Littlevrood F Howard l2 S S Faulkner ••• 2l T Bolton 2l J Binns A J Jones</p>
        <p>_ Total 236</p>
        <p>_ Total 174</p>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>Cycling generally, was lively about tha town during the week end. The most noticeable feature about the ex- ercise was that there appeared to be a much larger num- ber of lady cyclists abroad. I was toll that a few of the ladies, possessed of considerable enterprise, are already taking up the suggestion I made the other week that they should form a ladies' club in town.</p>
        <p>By the way, I see the great question which is con- stantly being asked of the belated cyclists by policemen, is " Where's your lamp," and a great !many police court prosecutions arise from the answer given. Last week, a policeman stopped a cyclist at Go'borne, and asked how it was he had no lamp. " Oh, it's just gone out," was the reply, so the policeman felt to see how long it had gone out, and found it had never come nut with the cyclist at all. That young man was fined 2s. 61. But Mark the difference. A certain IVarrington cyclist was out the other day without a lamp. A policeman stopped him and the following conversation took place My lamp's just gene out —feel if it isn't warm yet" won't feel at it—give me your name." " Are you going to feel and see that it has only just gone out I," "Na I won't—now come on with your name." " Very well said the cyclist darkly, and then he handed him a card with a Liverpool address on. Ile hasn't heard anything yet.</p>
        <p>Two detachments of the Warrington wheelers left their headquarters on Saturd,y and made tracks for the scene of the coronation at Golhorne. They had a jolly run,and didn't they enjoy themselves at the coronation? No, not much. But did any of the " crush " recognise that triangular club that passed them ? It came from Widnes, and consisted solely of officials—the captain, secretary, and treasurer. They don't want to be called triangular if they can get members to make it "Square.'</p>
        <p>It is said that since the introrluctisn of pneumatic tyres no sensation in cyclinz circles has equalled that caused on Saturday, when the wood track at Herne-hill was used in public for the first time. But for an unfor- tunate accident C. E. Barden,of Harlesden, who for saw that on this new surface a higher gearing could be ' utilised, would now have bean in possession of the ten miles record. On a machine geared tip to seventy-four, he ran clean away from some oT the speediest of track riders, and even the collapse of his cyeL9, which neces-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>TIN tXAMINER. SATITRDAY, MAY 13, 1893.</head>
        <p>TIN tXAMINER. SATITRDAY, MAY 13, 1893.</p>
        <p>sitated a change of mount, costing him twenty-four or twenty-five seconds, failed to Prevent his winning in 25 mins. 8 1-5 secs., which is only 18 secs. behind Osmond's record. The question wnich occurs to me is, why do bicycles collapse on such smooth and easy- going tracks? Is it because they are made so " feather- weight " that they will not stand real Use? In that case the maker may lay almost as much claim to the credit of a " record " as the rider.</p>
        <p>Who said cyclists can't have a red letter day I" Well the Warrington C.C. had one on Saturday, when they wheeled their wheels to Cranage, and carried off with them in a vehicle, their president (Mr. T. Morris), and their vice-presidents, Mr. P. Davies, and Mr. William Pierpoint, J.P. No fewer than 52 members turned out, and, dear me, how they did sing " Oh, what a time we're having," or they ought to have done. It was the brightest, breeziest, pleasantest, jolliest run they've had. On arriving at Cranage a " rare set out" was provided at the country quarters there. Tracks for home were made about 8 o'clock and on reaching Mr. P. Davies' residence the party were invited in and, through the kindness of Mr. and Mrs. Davies, araple refreshments were handed round. Warrington was reached about 11 o'clock, after one of the most enjoyable runs that has ever taken place in connection with the club.</p>
        <p>Everyone present and especially the president and vice-presidents were thoroughly delightrd with the out- ing. The arrangements were carried out by the captain Mr. W. Mounfield ; the sub-captain, Mr. D. Davies ; and the hon. sec., Mr. E. rickavance_</p>
        <p>The club had a run on Tuesday to Cronton when 28 members turned out and spent a very pleasant evening with music and singing at. the Unicorn Hotel, where the members of the club always receive a•cordial welcome. To-day the run is to Gawsworth, another pretty village situated near Macelesfield and another record muster is confidently expected.</p>
        <p>grandson will really be ahle to collect that debt, wheifit reaches him as a legacy.</p>
        <p>There is another class of people in the court,how- ever, at whom we have not yet had a glance. These you will see standing about a door leading into a small room. This is the mom where the judge takes what are called contested cases; they are the cases which generally are published in the newspapers, consisting of claims made as a rule either for damage or debt, and consisting of sums generally from a upwards. The people are a better class of individuals as a rule who come here, farmers, shopkeepers, professional people, &amp;c. The room is a very small, one and just holds the jadge, "who sits at a table, two solicitors, a couple of reporters, and the parties concerned in the case. The spectators have to satisfy their curiosity by obtaining promiscuous peeps over each other's shouldered= There is one dusty window in the room through iehich steal streaks of dingy light. Directly below the window, outside, is a slum alley, and all day long:the voice of the female inhabitants of that locality penetrate the rood, and their choice selec- tion of expletives conselike sweet, wild, eloquent flowers of rhetorictin comparison with the dry, formal,unimpassioned eloquence of the legal frater- nity, whose more refined voices have to duck under to the ones from without. Consequently, a solici- tor's address often gets mauled something like this: Your Honour will observe—(from outside): Mar-ry A-an—that the defendant in his letter to the plaintiff said—(from outside): Whoo I Look at the mud on your clogs, you little whelp"—but the voice is lost in a series of energetic whacks. "So it goes on; and you have to weed out the utterances of the two parties; and hew the Judge manages it, I don't know.</p>
        <p>The first day I was in there, an ambitious aspirant to musical fame had armed himself with a melodian,and sat down beneath the window. A case was on concerning some mechanical struc- ture, for the solicitor had before him a heap of mathematical calculations. They started together —be and the melodian. The latter had only small ground to cover, just. one verse of that air " Tidli wink the barber.' You know its monotone, yet enticing rhythm. No one seemed to heed it at. first; but presently the usher, who was dozing gently away, pricked up his ears and listened attentively ; then he smiled; then gently tapped his foot in tune; and eventually his chin sunk a little nearer to his breast, his eyes half closed, and his head moved slowly in pendulum fashion with the tune. The judge had been gently decoyed from the solicitor's utterances by the birOple air, and you could see him fairly struggling to fasten on the tune as he beat time On his finger nail with his pen. The self-confident air had left the solicitor's face; he looked troubled, as though he was trying to think of two things at once; every now and then he would stumble over his words, like a lame horse over bricks; and then he would start off again .with a peculiar rising ana falling note in his voice. Not his own style at all. He made sentences fit in to "Tidli wink the barb-er" like this—' We shall press it hard-er." He seemed to have got a kind of Mellifluous set-anywhere kind of tune to his words. The plaintiff was net lisfening to him he was glancing at the window, and his lips, though uttering no sound, were plainly mov- ing to "Tidli wink the barber.' Well, the end of it was, a request from the solicitor that the usher should get the man with the melodian removed,and it was done without comment from anyoue,j oat as though nothing had happened.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>I MEMORABLE DEBATE.</head>
        <p>I MEMORABLE DEBATE.</p>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE AND MR. CHAMBERLAIN. Writing with regard to the debate in the House of Commons on Thursday evening,as to whether the first clause should stand part of the Home Rule the Parliamentary correspondent of the Bradford " Observer" says :--Tbe debate on this proposal took a very wide range. It was, in fact, a second read- ing debate, and Mr. Chamberlain made a clever, tren- chant, but one-sided and unscrupulous attack on the Bill. He insisted on his right to discuss the whole policy and scope of the Bill, and held that they were especially entitled to repeat arguments which had not been an- swered by the Government. The amendments put on the paper by supporters of the Government were,he said amendments pour rire, and he made a good deal of fun of Mr. Labouchere for putting down an amendment and then voting against it. Eirly in Mr. Chassbe speech a lively scene occurred, in which Mr. Healy took a conspicuous part. He rose to order, but hail to face for some time the angry and overbearing shouts of the Unionists. He had some difficulty in making his pur- pose clear, but he at last succeeded in asking whether 1 Mr. Chamberlain was in order in going ioto the Bill at I large. The Chairman, however, decided, that the right hon. gentleman al as entitled to speak on I the general principles, and all efforts to get Mr. 11;hamberlain brought up on the point of order abso- lutely. failed. In the course of Mr. Chamberlain's spoech a remarkable scene occurred, in which Lord Churchill played e very strange and a sorry part. The noble lord suddenly rose and moved that the words of somebody should be taken down. Nobody tor a moment knew whose words he referred to, or what the words were. Commotion and astonishment prevailed on the Irish benches, and everyone wondered to what the noble lord was alluding. Lord R. Churchill again rose, and stated that he heard the member f.,r North Leuth (Mr. Healy) say, " You are knocked up." This awful intimation was received with much laughter, and nobody could conceive why, even if they had been uttered, they should be taken down. It turned out, however, that the noble lord was completely and ludicrously mistaken as to the words used and in the person who used them. There was much confusion, and the Chairman, unfortunately, was not strong enough to calm the storm. At last, however, Mr. W. Johnston had the candour to explain that he was an in- terrupting member, and that he had usEd the very in- nocent words, " They are all dumb." Lord Randolph Churchill did not yet discover what a singularly foolish blunder he had made, and amidst inueb laughter he pointed to Mr. Healy as the author of the words, and he still proposed that the words should be taken down. The Chairman, instead of stamping on the proposal as absurd, seemed disposed to entertain it. Mr. Ross, how- ever, made a further explanation which compfived " the comedy ct errors." He stated that after Mr. Johnston said " they are all dumb," Mr. Swift M'Neill added " and it would be a great advantage if you (Mr. Chamberlain) were too." This statement produced general laughter, and of course Lord R. Churchill' pro- posal became so supremely ridiculous that it was alio wecl to drop. Mr. Chamberlain wound up his speech by describing tke bill as containing a sham supremacy, a sham veto, sham safeguards, and sham finance. Then came a speech from the Prime Minister which will be an epoch in the history of the Bill, a speech which rivalled and almost surpassed Mr. Gladstone' greatest Parliamentary exploits. Never have I seen the great statesman in grander form. He has mere than on.:e given Mr. Chamberlain a good dressing, but never has he inflicted on the right hon. gentleman such a mer«iless, such a thoroughly deserved punishment as he administered to-night. Tine Prime Minister saw very clearly the purpose of Mr. Chamberlain, And lie spoke with a superb and lofty scorn of his methods and tactica. Mr. Chamberlain is usually cool,and bears the attacks of his opponents with an easy indifference which is perhaps not always assumed. But to-night he could not main- tain an attitude of supercilious calmness. tie positively writhed under the castigatiOn which he received, and some of Mr. Gladstone's sentences made his face livid with anger. Mr. Gladstone told the right hon.gentleman that the one weapon in his hand was time, and that the only chance of defeating this Bill was by an indefinite prolongation of the proceedings. The member for Bir- mingham complained that his arguments were not ans- wered, but with regard to Ulster, with regard to tha retention to the Irish memberS, and other phits full and explicit explanations had been gives bvthe Government: •• We are asked why we do not announce our intentions definitely on every point; but," replied Mr. Gladstone, with convincing force, " are we to shut our minds against discussion and against argument ?" "I do net and will not tie myself,' said the right hon. gentleman with an emphasis which roused the cheers of the House, " to any plan with regard to the Irish members. We are not going to play the game of the right hon. gentle- man." His treatment of Mr. Chamberlain's quotations from old speeches of his own and ef the Irish members was brilliantly effective. "If we are going to be ex- amined on our old speeches, is the right lion, gentle- man willing te submit to the same ordeal." •‘ If we are going to stand in a white sheet, the right lion, gentle- man will wear that ornamental garment longer than any other body." I have never seen Mr. Chamberlain look worse than at this moment, and the ringing cheers with which tie stroke was sent home must sound in his ears for many a day. Mr. Gladstone strongly advised , the Irish members not to be drawn into explanations by the flattering, seductive, and siren-like invitations of the hon. member.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone sat down amidst such a burst of cheers as is rarely heard in the House of Commons. There was waving. of hats and clapping of hands, and the cheering was kept up for several minutes. The Unionists hurried in and out of the House, and they were followed by a good many Gladstonians. ,t'ome of the latter, however, lingered and gathered in a crowd about the door, while the Irish members remained in their places still cheering the Grand Old Man. The Nationalists, whose feelings had btren wound up to the highest pitch, sprang on the feet, and began to cheer the old man with the greatest fervour. The Liberals, who had gathered on the benches below the bar, not only joined in the cheering, but, as if to give additional significance to the demonstra- tion advanced up the floor of the House. The Chairman had left the chair for the usual interval when Mr. Gladstone had finished, and therefore, an exhibition of feeling, which would have been irregular with the Chairman in the chair was allowed to go on unchecked. The Nationalists and the Liberals on the floor of the House cheered Mr. Gladstone with passionate warmth. Even this, however, was not sufficient to express their feelings. So deeply were they stirred by Mr. Glad- stone's noble speech that they waved their hats and their arms with frantic enthusiasm, and made the chamber ring with their acclamations. Mr. Gladstone accepted this demonstration with calm dignity, but never has he left tne House with the reverberation of so much feeling and so much admiration sounding in his ears.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NEW BATHS.</head>
        <p>THE NEW BATHS.</p>
        <p>It has been decided to open the new baths on the 24th of this month, and the work is now in full progress, and it is expected that they will be finished for that date. Appleton water is to be used, and the baths are connected with a main in Wilson Patten-at., from which will be obtained an efficient supply. The balcony of the new baths is com- pleted, and men are now engaged in painting it. It is expected that the bottom of the baths will be completed by this week end. It is covered with glazed bricks, and the depth will vary from three to seven feet. The old second class bath is intended as a ladies' bath, with dressing boxes, vapour batli and shower bath.</p>
        <p>FOOD FOR THE PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>A BUTCHER HEAVILY FINED.</p>
        <p>---..—. . A Derby butcher, named Richard Clay, was on Thurs- day fined £2O and costs, for having meat unfit for food. It was proved that animals hinge were badly effected with tuberculwis which rendered partakers of the fl,sh liable to consumption. The Inspector saw the lungs and they were thrown into the river, and whilst ho went to recover them defendant and one of his men made off with the carcase which has not been traced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>SKETpIiESI 4T THE COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>SKETpIiESI 4T THE COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>PEOPLE WHO GO THERE.</p>
        <p>BY LIME BLUNT.</p>
        <p>I have often been in the Warrington County Court—which is a feet few of my friends, probably from excessive modesty, are prepared to admit. It is a musty,dark,dismal place—apiece Into which the sunlight does net easily penetrate,and a place where you can get the dumps for nothing if you cannot obtain a judgment summons for the same amount. Once upon a time it was the house of • greater administrator than a County Court Judge—At was the Rouse of the Great Judge—and was erected by the Wesleyan@ 'I believe. The court room itself is the large hall of the chapel; it has scarcely been altered. The dim, lonely untenanted gallery is there, with its dusty pews. The place where the minister stood to preach is now occupied by the judge and his desk : and as a pious friend of mine rather inconsistently put it '/where the kneeling congregation Used to stand,sit a forlorn and slattern.- ly attired crowd of debtors—chiefly .women." It ,is a very long time since I first pissed through the portals of this place cud so in any allusions to persons which 1 make, they do not concern anyone connected with the court ,now, or recently. With a slow step I sauntered through the door into the court room, and I had moved about two feet into the room when a loud petulant voice shouted "'Ats hoff in Court 1"- I turned my head round to see what was the matter when the same voice simultaneously with two others bawled "Take yer hat off there.' Now, like many others I was quite ignorant of the great breach of the court laws I was committing by keeping my hat on, so I didn't take it off. But it seemed a terrible thing to do; the crowd of people in court stared at me in blank amazement; the three bailiffs exchanged glances which showed their official dignities had . been knocked into cocked hats; and the Registrar bay- ing scratched his white wig, fixed me with a stony stare, and said " Remove—that—man's hat." In a minute those three bailiffs had pulled my hat off and crowded it into my hands; but I put it on again with a show of indignation, and displeasure, and said I had a cold. But the Registrar said that if I wanted to remain in court I should just have to pull it off, cold or no cold. So I did it..</p>
        <p>Then I had a look round at the sort of people whom I had fallen among. The whole of the benches were occupied by a most miserable collection of poor people; women with clogs on their feet, shawls over their dishevelled heads of hair, and torn dusty dresses on; women whose faces bore the traces of emaciating, pinching want., and often also the vul- gar expression of -face which bears a relation to vice. In four cases out of five they carried child- ren in their arms. There was a sprinkling of old men, and girls amongst them, but all were crowded together, apparently from a sympathy Ythieh was the outcome of their circumstancea. At the oppo- site side of the court was another group, but how different in,the people who composed it? They were not slatternly dressed ; neithee did they bear any particular traces of want, nor carry babies. They were mew—debt collectors, who wen there to apply for orders to be made upon the sort of people across the room. Dividing the two groups was a large circular desk where sat a enuple of solicitors conning their briefs, and a ceuple of those omni- present beings—reporters, who were tranquilly waiting for something to turn up; but in the mean- time chewing the ends of quill pens. There was a permanent low, St .ady hum going on ; but ever and anon it woislil grow louder and louder, and a tide of mingled voices would come washing up to the Registrar and the bailiffs. And it was on such occasions that my attention was drawn to a gentle- man who there discharged the arduous and respon- .sible duties of ,a " bum." —lle was one of the funniest, most pliable, jumpy, soft-headed men I ever saw. When the tide of voices washed rather strongly up:in them,he would just drop down from his elevated perch—drop as solid as a gob of m ud—ind then for about five minutes you would see his shiny bald head bobbing about in the crowd,and hear him twittering, like a sparrow" Some times he would seem close at hand, and he would "hi" right in your ear, and then he would bob up at the other side of the room and the faint " shi— shi" Would come softly thro' the hum. And he seemed to have such a silly, girlish heart. The nateae of a woman was called as owing £5 to aa undertaker. She stepped slowly and wearily into the defendant's box. He; ,age would be 22; her , black dress with its crape trimmings looked seedy and threndbare ; her face looked wasted and thin, and her large eyes bad a far away look about them. And when she was telling the .Registrar that she contracted the debt by burying her husband eight months after their marriage, and now had to get her living by sewing, she broke right down and sobbed like a child. Well, what did that bailiff fellow do. He came right round, and in a loud voice shouted ". Pay attention to the Registrar ; and answer his questions. See." Then he sneaked his handkerchief out of his pocket, and slid it into her hand and said softly " Don't cry—don't. There And the next minute he shouted; (pretending to he fierce and dignified, poor man 0 " And what d'yre bring childer here for—eh?" Tien in a husky low voice, that bad some relation to the tear in his eye: "Here gig:oam to hold 'um." Retook the .Child from her and the next minute he vrasbobbinu about withliis " shi, shi." Presently he sneaked a look round at the Registrar; and as everyone' seemed busy, he quietly dropped on a form* In six seconds he was joggling the child about on his knee as jubilant as a nurse girl, and saying" Tucky tuck, tuck. l'il give it yet-1 will." And it" coo- cooed,"'considerably. Presently he heard the Registrar calling him; he jumped up quick and began to return the child—when everyone com- mencecl looking at his legs, and trying to hide smiles that were too large to be covered with their hands. Well, if a fellow will take to wet nursing with only one pair of pants on hand, be has tostand it, and it isn't right to laugh at him, But he was silly, don't you think se.</p>
        <p>But it is quite as well to listen to some of the applications for orders. They are all of a type, orders for the recovery of small debts chiefly incurred by taking groceries in,on that very unwise system, commonly termed "the "strap," or " trust "; poor people who run up a debt gradually for small quantities of tea and bacon, &amp;c.; and who are anable to meet a bill when it reaches the dimensions of about £2. The husbands never appear in these cases; the conduct of household business is an unfat boilable and foreign question to him which he leaves to his wife. As a rule there is a consider- able number of these cases each court day. This is a typical one. Mrs. S elina Elizabeth Twiggem is summoned for owing £2 10s. to Mr. Josuall B. Blatherskite, for groceries received. Mrs. Twiggem steps in the defendant's box and having osculatoiily demons- trated her intention to speak the truth the follow- ing conversation takes place : Registrar (to defendant) : Do you admit this debt.</p>
        <p>Defendant : Partly I do ; partly I don't. Registrar: Now what do you mean. Defendant : Well, I'm sure I never had all thoosa yer things on 'im. Its beyond my recomrnember, it I did.</p>
        <p>I've got them in the book, sir. Defendant (to plaintiff) : Eh I your a bad 'on you erre. Got 'ern in't book—and who tevrd thee to put them theere. (Then fiercely): He whom with thee, thm money grabbler—thee." Registrar: Hold your tongue woman. Defendant : If he done— Registrar (angrily) : Hold your tongue, will you. Defendant: I tell you-- Registrar (rising angrily) : I'll have you put out of court if you don't behave.</p>
        <p>Defendata : Humph. Plaintiff : I ask for an order of eight shillings per week. Her hutbanct's in regular work, making 365. per week. Defendant: That's another fib. He's only a labourer, Sir, getting 181. a week. Registrar : How many of a family. Defendant: Lemme, see, (counting on her fingers), there's John, little Willie, Jane, Liz and tother. ''es thrrn eight of us.</p>
        <p>Registrar : Are you paying to anyone else in court.</p>
        <p>Defendant : Yes—to two.</p>
        <p>Registrar (to plaintiff) : Well, we have to accept their statements. I shall make an order for 2s. per week.</p>
        <p>The defenclftnt clears out with a face bright with elation and triumph, and knows that Mr.Blather- skite will be a grey headed octogenarian before he gets a cent. ; and Mr. B. on the other hand leaves the bax with a sad, lack, lustre eye, and mentally curses the County Court method of helping a man to recover (!) his debts. And he wonders if his</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>NOTES TAKEN DURING THE DRY YEAR OF 1868. [BY MR. ROBERT DANIES.]</head>
        <p>NOTES TAKEN DURING THE DRY YEAR OF 1868. [BY MR. ROBERT DANIES.]</p>
        <p>Having been much struck by the similarity be- twixt the weather in 1868 and the weather (so far) of the present year, I venture to set out a few notes taken at my house in Birch Dale, thinking they nvy interest your readers, many of whom have doubtless seen with concern the prognostica- tions of a wet harvest and autumn published by Professor Falb.</p>
        <p>After a mild and rather wet winter and a violent gale on the Ist February 1868, which blew down the large cedar at Orford flall,aml a tall chimneY in Warrington, killing a man, and unroofing many buildings (this last winter 1892-3 give us, it will be recollected a mouth's frost, and little, or no violent wind).the weather moderated.</p>
        <p>On the 16th February, 1868„ snowdrop and some crocuses were in bloom. 28th February. Lilacs, gooseberries, currants, and white rose trees were bursting their buds. 15th March. Aprlcot trees, early plums, and daffodils. . . .</p>
        <p>. „ in full bloom. The hedges partly green, crocuses ever; winds W., S.W., and S. chiefly. 22nd March. Yellow Japanese roses (Kerrie Japonica, dog tooth, violets, and flowering currant tries, all in</p>
        <p>. , bloom. . 29th March. Cloudless and warm days, horse chestnut trees in partial leaf. 4th April. Lovely week ;, pear trees, and out-door hyacinths la bloom. 10th April. (Easter Day), glorious weather ; ilelds thick with grass 'no east winds this year. 19th April:!.„ilaukand syca mores in partial leaf. 30th April. 'Apple trees and chestnut in full •bloomt ; gooseberries large enough to gather; brilliant weather. 4th May. Oaks in full leaf ; cloudless still ; very dry. The rookS built and hatched at the usual time and the</p>
        <p>n 7 a Fields getting very dry. 24th May. A sprinkling, short shower—no other rain. 31st May. Maycutting commencing,rain water finished. 14th June. Drought continues ; fields getting burnt up ' and white ; gathered green peas; leaves of the trees</p>
        <p>crisping with the heat and drought. 20th Julie. Roses in full bloom ; no blight or honeyfall this year. 4th July. My garden becoming a dry sandhole. The farmers cutting the July shoots off their trees for the cattle. Rhododendrons drooping and losing their leaves. The humming bird hawk moth plentiful, hovering about the jessamine lowers like' humming birds themselves. A locust caught in Arpley fields. No clouds since February. Prayers everywhere for rain. The farmers cutting beans and oats green for their cattle. Straw hardly a foot high. 12th July. Oats cut at Weston,first I have heard of this I year. 1h July. St. Swithin's Day. Very slight thunder- shower. 19th July. Weather not disturbed. St. Swithin not strong enough. _Wheat and oats cut. Several fields . .</p>
        <p>abouCdtociaon Heath got in, no weathering required: No such season, old farmers say, since 1826, the year of the great drought and comet. My grandfather u,ed to r?lare that gooseberries were gathered for a tart on the 24th. April in that year. 23rd July: Thermometer fell to 36 degrees in the night aftcr $2 in the shade during the day. Many trees bare of leaves, the pastures as white as a haystack. Bottom of Hill Cliffe reservoir mostly dry and c.vered with grass and weeds. Sunday 2nd August. Hotter than ever ; carried an umbrella to church as a sunshade for the first time in my life. August 3rd. 82 degrees in shade. August 4th. 92 degrees in shade. Harvest all got in. August 16th. Thunderstorm and heavy shower followed by wind and more good showers.</p>
        <p>August 23rd. Wonderful crop of mushrooms sprang</p>
        <p>up. September 6th. Very hot weather has returned. 29 September. Cooler ; more rain last week. October 2nd. More rain and cold winds (often east); the pastures green again at last ; three several crops</p>
        <p>, . .-, . .. of mushrooms this year, now twopence per quart. November 7th. Ice half an inch thick—at least an inch of snow—startling change.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>rENKETII.</head>
        <p>rENKETII.</p>
        <p>BAND SIINDAT.—On Sunday a rather novel feature was introduced in the Wesleyan Chapel. The first Sun- day in May it appears is set apart all through the coun- try as a day specially for brass bands. The energetic bandmaster, Mr. J. Bowyer, this year wished to intro- duce this custom into the village. On Sunday morning therefore the inhabitants of Penketh were treated to a parade of the band which played suitable selections. After the parade the band proceeded to the service in the chapel and at the close of the service an anthem " We praise thee 0 Lord" was admirably rendered by the band. In the evening the band formed and marched to St. Mary's Church, Sankey, to the music of several pretty hymns. The band has made wonderful progress since its formation.</p>
        <p>SANKIT BRIDGES WO3I.IIYANC C. C. v. PEICCBTEI.— This match was played or. the ground of the latter and resulted in a win for the former. For the vieitors Alfred We:ding batted and bowled well and J. Pusill batted well for the hcone team. Score : PENKETH. SANKEY BItMG FS WPM. I..YA:f3. D Davies c Griffiths b id Pritchard c Singleton Morrell diPitsill bWe ding 11 Singleton b Welding A B .wyer b Wright A Boardman b Welding 0 CI Morrall c Singleton b 31 Glover c Griffiths b Push l 9 Wright 6 W Woolley b Glover 0 F Forster b Dunn 4 E Wright b Posill P Singleton not out l2 Dula c Singleton b Clare ... 0 J Clare b Wel. dug T Hignam b Woo ley W Chamberlain c Gott erill 11 Gorst rua out b Dunn 7 tiorat not out 1 Extras 4 Extras 8</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>LATEST NE WS.</p>
        <p>ALLOWANCE.THE DUKE OF YORK'S</p>
        <p>The "Morning "says :—One of the subjects that will shortly occupy the attention of the House of Commons will be the provision of a suitable establishment and annuity for the Duke of York. The most likely arrange- ment will be to continue the allowance made to the late Duke of Clarence from the fund of £36,000 per annum formed in 1889, and to add to this the Duke of York's own allowance; and this can be done without applica- tion to Parliament.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>CHILD STEALING.</head>
        <p>CHILD STEALING.</p>
        <p>At Cardiff Police Court, yesterday, Lilian Matthews, a single woman, aged 24, a native of Tredegar, was remanded on the charge of stealing the two-vear old son of Mr. Walter R)berts, accoantant, Cardiff. The child had been missing since April 27th. and was found in prisoner's possession at Bristol on Wednesday last. The case excited extraordinary public interest, and a large and angry crowd assembled in the vicinity of the court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>THROWING AT TRAINS.</head>
        <p>THROWING AT TRAINS.</p>
        <p>A DRIVER LOSES AN EYE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday night about a quarter pest eleven,wben the express train from King's Cross to Peterborough was passing between Finsbury Park and Harringay Stations, some person threw something from the bridge. The missile struck the spectacle glass and broke it, causing the broken glass to enter the eyes of the driver, Elias Brown, of Tramway View, Peterborough. Be was taken to the hospital, where it was found that the sight of one eye was destroyed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>COMMERCIAL FAILURES IN AMERICA.</head>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAILURES IN AMERICA.</p>
        <p>NEW YORK, FIZIDAY.—The fall in industrial stocks has caused toe suspension of a number of banks throughout the country, and many leading business men are finan- cially embarrassed. The craze for investments in in- dustrial stocks began about the time the great trusts came into existence. The monopoly idea continued to flourish until about a month ago, when it leaked out that the President and his Attorney-General (Mr. Olney) intended to abide strictly by the terms of the Chicago platform, on which Mr. Cleveland was elected, and to make war on all combinations and pools. When specu- lators heard this they withdrew from all the watered trust and industrial stocks, which caused the heavy fall in them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>BE&amp;TING THE ATLANTIC RECORD.</head>
        <p>BE&amp;TING THE ATLANTIC RECORD.</p>
        <p>The Cunard steamer Campania passed Browhead at seven o'clock yesterday morning, thus making the fastest passage on record to the Eastward, though on the longest route, her speed averaging twenty-one knots per hour. correspondent says :—The Cunard steamer Cam- pania, which left New York on Saturday last for Queens- town and Liverpool on her maiden Eastern voyage, was signalled passing the Fastnet at 639 a.m. to-day, and arrived off Queenstown at 9.42 a.m., the time occupied ,to the Fa,stnet being live days fifteen hours, and to Queenstown five days seventeen hours twenty-seven minutes, being the fastest Eastern passage across the Atlantic on record, a truly phenomenal performance for a ship on what may ba termed her maiden voyage. The Campania left Sandy Hook Lt 11.40 a.m., American time, on the 6th hist., and her daily runs were :—To noon on the 7th, 510 miles; Bth, 490; 9th, 474 ; 10th, 517; 11th, 493 ; and to Queenstown 344 knots, arriving at 9.42 A.M. yesterday, the total distance traversed being 2,923 inUes, which was over the king southerly route. Toe Campani4Zs best run on the westward voyage was 503 knots; on tile eastern 517.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>YESTERDAY'S PA RLIAME T.</head>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S PA RLIAME T.</p>
        <p>HOUSE OF LORDS. ROYAL ASSENT TO SHIP CANAL BILLS.</p>
        <p>Their Lordships met at five minutes past two for the purpose of the Royal as;ent by Commis.ion being given to n number of Bills, includinr, the Manchester Ship Canal (Additional Capital, &amp;a.) and the Manchester Corporation (Ship Canal) Bills ; also the Manchester Cor- poration Bill, Saltord Improvement Bill, Wirral Railway Bill, kc. The Commissioners were the Lord Chan- cellor, Lord Breadalbane, and Lord Carrington. Not a single Peer was present in the House. The Speaker was accompanied to the bar by about forty members of tbe Commons. The House adjourned until a quarter past four. HOUSE OF COMMONS. A COMMITTEE ON ELECTRICITY.</p>
        <p>A motion was agreed, to that a joint committee of Lords 9.nd Commons be appointed to consider whether the grant of statutory powers to use electricity ought to be Qualified by any prohibition or restriction as to earth return circuits or as to leakage, induction, and similar matters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>PATENTS.</head>
        <p>PATENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. BURT, in reply to Mr. Schwann, said that about twenty volumes of the abridgements of specifications of pa:Cents were now ready. The remaining volumes, probably 100 in number, might be expected by October next.</p>
        <p>"THE REPUBLICAN TLAO OF IRBLAND."</p>
        <p>Mr. DARLING asked the Prime Minister if he. could give the House any information as to a statement in that day's paper to the effect that a ship bearing what purported to be the Republican Flag of Ireland was saluted in New York Harbour by the American ships there assembled.— The Firms MINISTER asked the hon, member to put the question on the paper in order that he might have an opportunity of making inquiries.</p>
        <p>BAHL'S CLOSING.</p>
        <p>Sir J. Lubbock inir-o-clUce-cri-Bill to provide for the earlier closing of shops. THE HOME MILE BILL: A SCENE.</p>
        <p>The House then went into Committee on the Govern- ment of Ireland Eill.—SirJ. Gorst resumed the debate on the amendment for the omission of clause I.—Mr. T. W. RussELL was addressing the House, when he sud- denly paused and then said : Those words ought mete be used. (Cries of "What words ?")—Mr. GIBES : Tbe words are "What the devil are you talking about ?" The CHAIRMAN : Who used those words ?—Mr. Res- : The member for South Donegal (Mr. M'Neill).— The CMAIRMAN : What has the hon. member for South Donegal to say ? Did he use the words imputed to him P —Mr, M,Nelia.: What are the words P—Lord Cain- BORNE : We all heard them. They were " What the devil are you talking about P"—Mr. M'aixtza. : I de. Mare, sir, on my honour, that I never used those words, and never used any such expression.—Mr. T. W. Rus- mar. ! I heard him. I heard him. I heard him. (Oh, and ieterruption.)—Mr. Dzit.ox : I was sitting beside my hou.friend,and I declare that he made use of no such expression.—Mr.M'Cartrrev : I quite concur in what the hon.member has just said. It was the member for North Kerry (Mr. Sexton) who made use of the words. (Op. position cheers).—Mr. SEXTON: Sir, the statement is untrue. (Loud Irish cheers.) I made use of no such words. (More cheers.)—Mr. fleatemaros : I can confirm that statement. (Irish cheers.)—Sir W. Haecouirr, who was uproariously cheered from the Ministerial and Irish benches, and who spoke to a crowded and excited House, said: lam obliged to call your attention to the fact that not only to-day but yesterday gentlemen have risen up to charge other gentlemen with making particular state- raents and asking that the words 'should be taken down, when those gentlemen have state I, and those around them have confirmed their declarations, that no such language escaped their lips. (Ministerial cheers.) I wish to put it to you air, that gentlemen ought to be a very great deal more cautious in getting up in their places and saying they heard a gentleman making a statement which he says on his honour he never made. (Loud Ministerial cheer.)—Mr. BALFOUR, who was cheered by the Opposition, said,—Sir, I rise— Mr. Al•Nsim, (interrupting) : I rise, sir, to call for a distinct humble and absolute apology from the gentle- men ladio made the charge against me. (Laughter.) Mr. Batroug said it was quite certain that the words were used by some gentleman sitting below the gang- way, and he expressed a hope that the hon. members would restrain their emotion and not indulge in expres- sions unparliamentary in themselves and which cer- tainly did not conduce to harmony. (Cheers.) Mr. V. Gums apologised to the hon. ?member for South Done- gal (Mr. M'Neill) for attributing to him the blackguardly language which he' had denied using. (Cheers.) The CHAIRMAN : The hon. gentlemen who has just sat down has taken the proper and only course open to him.—Mr. SEXTON who was received with cheers and cries of order, said so far as he *as concerned the incident could not terminate until the hon. member for South Antrim had withdrawn the accusation against him.—Mr. MACARTNEY said he had endeavoured three or four times to obtain an opportunity of expressing his regret that he should have been led by a similarity in the voice to attribute the words to the hon. gentleman, Mr. Sexton.—The matter then dropped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-13_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>RIVAL TO THE CRYSTAL PALACE.</head>
        <p>A RIVAL TO THE CRYSTAL PALACE.</p>
        <p>Chicago people, not satisfied with tha navelties as- sembled at the great Fair, are credited with the inten- tion of erecting in a leading thoroughfare of their city " a tall building, in which there shall not be a stone, brick, or piece of timber." The promoters of the plan, 'Messrs. flolabird and Roche, do not of design pose this description as a sphinx-like riddle. They simply pro- ject the construction of a tenement of an allay of 90 per cent, of aluminiutn and 10 per cent, of copper, fastened in the form of sheets over a wall of steel-framed fireproof material. There will of course be glass windows, and , as the vitreous uhstance is to be used to a greater ex- tent than is customary in large buildings it is expected Nt the structure, which is to cost 700,000 dollars, will form an imposing spectacle, especially when the sun shines on the glass frames, 22 feet wide, and the bright aluminium plates. It will, in fact, bb the Chicagoan's Crystal Falace.—/ren.</p>
