        <p>POOR. ON [lO,OOO A YEAR. AN " APCS'FOLIC " BISHOP. The Bishop of London is says a London cortemprindent credited with an income of £lO,OOO a year with t*,. large homes, hut lie is said to have recently stal eit that he is pourer now than he was as Bishop of Exeter ( wheii His income was £4,000 a year), and that he flints it impossible to save looney for his wife and children. This &amp;script ion of the right rev. .prelate's melancholy. condi. tion, given on the Bishop's spar elixir, was, I hear, pm forward on Sunday by more thon one of the preachers or the Bishop ot London's Fund as a reason . why the laity should give it increased support. As the Bishop does not keep.up the See house in St. James's .Square, which is net even fittnislisd—and as his Lordship's rive, side stinage is distinguished for apostolic simplicity, it is difficult to see how the legend can be borne out by the facts. THE GLASS TRADE. DEPUTATION TO MR. EIUNDELLA. A deputation organised by the Amalgamated Glass Trade Association of Great Britain, and including dele- gates from among others the trades councils of Man cheater. Glasgow, Preston,Crewe, and St. lielens,wai tai on Saturday on Mr. Mundellasthe President of the Beard of Trade, so urge upon the Government the nrcessity,in the interest of British labour, that every foreign article imported into this eountry should be marked with the place of origin, unless that was absolutely impossible by reason of the nature of the article in which case the vendor should give the purchaser ?air means of knowing that he was buying a foreign article and encouraging foreign labour to the detriment of this country. Mr. Mundella, in replying, remarked that, while he was anxious for the maintenance of the supremacy of our commerce and manufactures, it was impossible to put the mark of origin on all foreign goods which came to this country. The Act, he asserted, had been a great sitecess. It was not surprising that there hail been evasions of it but he was prepared to enkrce it vigor- ously if those evasions were, brought to his attention. What was wanted was that the present Act should be thoroughly Administered, and that he promised to see carried out.'eThey should also try to get from foreign Governments the same honest treatment of our exports which we extended to foreign goods when they came to this Country.</p>
